('storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32','story.japanese.okuninushi','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十至三十二节（长篇讲述）','Kojiki, Chamberlain translation, Sections XX–XXXII (long retelling)','source.japanese.kojiki.chamberlain','Sect. XX–XXXII (kj027–kj039)','lang.en','神圣文本档案所收张伯伦 1882 年英译《古事记》第二十至三十二节','大国主的五个名字、稻羽白兔、手间山的赤猪、大树的裂缝、根之国的蛇室与蜈蚣蜂室、火烧原野与老鼠、带着宝物逃走、八千矛的求婚之歌、少名毘古那、御诸山的神、天若日子与雉鸣女、建御雷与国让','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'张伯伦 1882 年译本属公共领域；本站发布按节顺序的独立讲述，神名采用现代通行读法。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43','story.japanese.ninigi_sea_palace','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十三至四十三节（长篇讲述）','Kojiki, Chamberlain translation, Sections XXXIII–XLIII (long retelling)','source.japanese.kojiki.chamberlain','Sect. XXXIII–XLIII (kj040–kj050)','lang.en','神圣文本档案所收张伯伦 1882 年英译《古事记》第三十三至四十三节','天孙降临的诏命、天之八衢的猿田毘古、神器与五伴绪、降临高千穗、猿女君与海参、木花之佐久夜毘卖与石长比卖、火中生子、海幸山幸、海神之宫、井边的玉、潮盈珠与潮干珠、鸬鹚羽的产屋','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'张伯伦 1882 年译本属公共领域；本站发布按节顺序的独立讲述，神名采用现代通行读法。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90','story.japanese.yamato_takeru','《古事记》张伯伦译本第七十九至九十节（长篇讲述）','Kojiki, Chamberlain translation, Sections LXXIX–XC (long retelling)','source.japanese.kojiki.chamberlain','Sect. LXXIX–XC (kj086–kj097)','lang.en','神圣文本档案所收张伯伦 1882 年英译《古事记》第七十九至九十节（景行天皇纪第四至十五部分）','小碓命与兄长、少女装扮刺杀熊曾建、出云建与木刀、伊势的姑母与草薙剑、尾张之约、相武的野火、弟橘比卖投海、足柄坂的白鹿、酒折宫的火守老人、伊吹山的白猪、当艺野与三重的腿、化为白鸟','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'张伯伦 1882 年译本属公共领域；本站发布按节顺序的独立讲述，神名采用现代通行读法。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10','story.finnish.kalevala_sampo_forging','《卡勒瓦拉》第一至十章（克劳福德译本，长篇讲述）','Kalevala, Runes I–X (Crawford translation, long retelling)','source.finnish.kalevala_crawford.gutenberg','Runes I–X','lang.en','古腾堡计划所收克劳福德英译《卡勒瓦拉》第一至十章','大气之女与鸭蛋、万奈莫宁的诞生、播种与大橡树、约卡海宁的挑战、艾诺的命运、鱼与失去的新娘、约卡海宁的箭、鹰与洛希、彩虹上的少女与斧伤、铁的起源、伊尔马里宁被送往北方、锻造三宝','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'克劳福德 1888 年英译本属公共领域；本站发布按诗章顺序的独立讲述，人名采用现代芬兰语通行拼法。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15','story.finnish.kalevala_lemminkainen','《卡勒瓦拉》第十一至十五章（克劳福德译本，长篇讲述）','Kalevala, Runes XI–XV (Crawford translation, long retelling)','source.finnish.kalevala_crawford.gutenberg','Runes XI–XV','lang.en','古腾堡计划所收克劳福德英译《卡勒瓦拉》第十一至十五章','萨里岛的花、雪橇上的新娘与誓约、破誓、流血的梳子、波赫约拉的巫师与盲牧人、希西的麋鹿、森林之神、火焰之马、图奥内拉的天鹅、黑河里的碎片、母亲的寻找、铜耙与蜜蜂','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'克劳福德 1888 年英译本属公共领域；本站发布按诗章顺序的独立讲述，人名采用现代芬兰语通行拼法。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36','story.finnish.kalevala_kullervo','《卡勒瓦拉》第三十一至三十六章（克劳福德译本，长篇讲述）','Kalevala, Runes XXXI–XXXVI (Crawford translation, long retelling)','source.finnish.kalevala_crawford.gutenberg','Runes XXXI–XXXVI','lang.en','古腾堡计划所收克劳福德英译《卡勒瓦拉》第三十一至三十六章','两兄弟的仇恨、摇篮里的复仇者、杀不死的孩子、无用的仆人与出卖、藏着石头的面包、断刀、主妇之死、找到父母、路上的少女、急流、出征与复仇、卡勒沃之子的结局','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'克劳福德 1888 年英译本属公共领域；本站发布按诗章顺序的独立讲述，人名采用现代芬兰语通行拼法。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43','story.finnish.kalevala_sampo_theft','《卡勒瓦拉》第三十九至四十三章（克劳福德译本，长篇讲述）','Kalevala, Runes XXXIX–XLIII (Crawford translation, long retelling)','source.finnish.kalevala_crawford.gutenberg','Runes XXXIX–XLIII','lang.en','古腾堡计划所收克劳福德英译《卡勒瓦拉》第三十九至四十三章','宝剑与哭泣的船、勒明凯宁加入、大梭鱼、第一把康特勒琴、万物倾听、泪珠、唱睡波赫约拉、挖出三宝、鹤的叫声、雾与海怪与风暴、鹰形的洛希、三宝的碎片','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'克劳福德 1888 年英译本属公共领域；本站发布按诗章顺序的独立讲述，人名采用现代芬兰语通行拼法。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala','story.hindu.nala_damayanti','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事（甘古利译本，长篇讲述）','Mahābhārata, Vana Parva, Nalopakhyana Parva (Ganguli translation, long retelling)','source.indian.mahabharata_ganguli.sacredtexts','Vana Parva, Sections LII–LXXIX','lang.en','神圣文本档案所收甘古利英译《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》第五十二至七十九节','坚战的悲叹、天鹅传情、四位天神、使者那罗、五个那罗、迦利的怨恨、掷骰失国、森林中的分离、达摩衍蒂的漂泊、迦尔郭吒迦、寻找那罗、百由旬的车程与骰子的学问、相认、赢回王国','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'甘古利 1883–1896 年英译本属公共领域；本站发布按章节顺序的独立讲述。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri','story.hindu.savitri_satyavan','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》莎维德丽故事（甘古利译本，长篇讲述）','Mahābhārata, Vana Parva, Pativrata-mahatmya Parva (Ganguli translation, long retelling)','source.indian.mahabharata_ganguli.sacredtexts','Vana Parva, Sections CCXCI–CCXCVII','lang.en','神圣文本档案所收甘古利英译《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》第二百九十一至二百九十七节','马主王的祈求、女神的恩赐、无人求婚、自己去选、那罗陀的预言、不改的心意、林中的婚姻、三夜的斋戒、最后一天、阎摩的绳索、跟随死神、五个恩惠、黑夜里的归途、净修林中的光明','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'甘古利 1883–1896 年英译本属公共领域；本站发布按章节顺序的独立讲述。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra','story.hindu.kurukshetra_war','《摩诃婆罗多》斡旋篇至夜袭篇：俱卢之野大战（甘古利译本，长篇讲述）','Mahābhārata, Udyoga to Sauptika Parvas: the war at Kurukṣetra (Ganguli translation, long retelling)','source.indian.mahabharata_ganguli.sacredtexts','Udyoga Parva (Bhagavat-yana) to Sauptika Parva','lang.en','神圣文本档案所收甘古利英译《摩诃婆罗多》斡旋篇、毗湿摩篇、德罗纳篇、迦尔纳篇、沙利耶篇与夜袭篇','黑天的和平使命、战车上的阿周那、毗湿摩的箭床、激昂与轮阵、阿周那的誓言与胜车之死、瓶首之死、马嘶死了、迦尔纳的车轮、难敌藏身湖中、杵战、夜袭、战后的寂静','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'甘古利 1883–1896 年英译本属公共领域；本站发布按章节顺序的独立讲述。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam','story.persian.zal_rudabeh_rostam','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本：扎尔、扎尔与鲁达贝、鲁斯塔姆（长篇讲述）','Zimmern, The Epic of Kings: Zal; Zal and Rudabeh; Rustem (long retelling)','source.persian.epic_of_kings_zimmern','“Zal”, “Zal and Rudabeh”, “Rustem”','lang.en','麻省理工学院古典文献档案所收齐默恩 1883 年英文散文转述本《列王纪》','白发之子、西摩格的巢、萨姆的梦、扎尔回到人间、喀布尔的公主、长发之绳、父亲与国王的反对、王后的出使、智者的谜语、婚礼、难产与西摩格的羽毛、白象与拉赫什','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'齐默恩 1883 年的英文散文转述本属公共领域；本站据此发布独立讲述，人名采用现代通行的波斯语转写。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan','story.persian.rostam_seven_labours','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本：进军马赞德兰、凯·卡乌斯的更多愚行（长篇讲述）','Zimmern, The Epic of Kings: The March into Mazinderan; Kai Kaous Committeth More Follies (long retelling)','source.persian.epic_of_kings_zimmern','“The March into Mazinderan”, “Kai Kaous Committeth More Follies”','lang.en','麻省理工学院古典文献档案所收齐默恩 1883 年英文散文转述本《列王纪》','魔鬼歌手、白魔的冰雹、两条路与狮子、沙漠中的公羊、恶龙、女巫、乌拉德、阿尔章魔、白魔的洞穴、重见光明、化为岩石的国王、飞天的宝座','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'齐默恩 1883 年的英文散文转述本属公共领域；本站据此发布独立讲述，人名采用现代通行的波斯语转写。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab','story.persian.rostam_sohrab','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本：鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布（长篇讲述）','Zimmern, The Epic of Kings: Rustem and Sohrab (long retelling)','source.persian.epic_of_kings_zimmern','“Rustem and Sohrab”','lang.en','麻省理工学院古典文献档案所收齐默恩 1883 年英文散文转述本《列王纪》','丢失的拉赫什、塔米娜的夜访、留下的信物、苏赫拉布的成长、阿夫拉西亚布的阴谋、白堡的女战士、国王的召唤、营帐边的夜探、错认、第一天与第二天、第三天、长生药与哀悼','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'齐默恩 1883 年的英文散文转述本属公共领域；本站据此发布独立讲述，人名采用现代通行的波斯语转写。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13','story.chinese.soushenji_tales','干宝《搜神记》选读十三则（长篇讲述）','Gan Bao, Soushen ji: thirteen selected tales (long retelling)','source.china.soushenji.gutenberg','juan 1, 11, 14, 16, 19, 20','lang.lzh','古腾堡计划所收《搜神记》二十卷本','董永与织女、干将莫邪、三王墓、范巨卿与张元伯、东海孝妇、韩凭夫妇、嫦娥奔月、女化蚕、毛衣女、紫玉与韩重、宋定伯卖鬼、李寄斩蛇、义犬冢','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',1,'《搜神记》原文属公共领域；本站发布白话的独立讲述，不照录原文。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z'),
('storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10','story.japanese.izanagi_izanami','《古事记》张伯伦译本第一至十节（长篇讲述）','Kojiki, Chamberlain translation, Sections I–X (long retelling)','source.japanese.kojiki.chamberlain','Sect. I–X (kj008–kj017)','lang.en','神圣文本档案所收张伯伦 1882 年英译《古事记》第一至十节','天地初开、神世七代、淤能碁吕岛、绕柱与水蛭子、大八岛、众神诞生、火神与伊邪那美之死、斩火神、黄泉、千引石、禊祓与三贵子','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',0,'张伯伦 1882 年译本属公共领域；本站发布按节顺序的独立讲述，神名采用现代通行读法。','2026-10-24T18:00:00Z');

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO story_sections(
    id,story_version_id,section_order,heading_zh,heading_en,body_zh,body_en,anchor_claim_id,evidence_note,uncertainty_note
) VALUES
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.1','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',1,'大国主的五个名字','The five names of the Master of the Great Land','《古事记》在讲完须佐之男在出云建宫、娶栉名田比卖之后，接着列出了他的子孙：须佐之男和栉名田比卖生下八岛士奴美神，此后一代代往下传，传到天之冬衣神，天之冬衣神娶了刺国大神的女儿刺国若比卖，生下的就是这一部故事的主人公——从须佐之男往下数，正好是第六代。书中说他有五个名字：大国主神、大穴牟迟神、苇原色许男神、八千矛神、宇都志国玉神。这些名字后来会在故事里一个个出现：年轻时人们叫他大穴牟迟；在根之国，须佐之男叫他苇原色许男，也就是“苇原中国的丑男子”；求婚时他叫八千矛神，也就是“拥有八千支矛的神”；最后，须佐之男在黄泉比良坂上命令他成为“大国主”和“宇都志国玉”，也就是统治人间国土的神。他还有许多兄弟，书中称为八十神。他的母亲刺国若比卖，在后面的故事里两次哭着去救他。故事开始的时候，他还只是兄弟中最受欺负的一个。','After telling how Susanoo built his palace in Izumo and married Kushinada-hime, the Kojiki lists his descendants: their son Yashima-jinumi, and so on down the generations to Ame-no-fuyu-kinu, who married Sashikuni-waka-hime, daughter of the great deity of Sashikuni, and fathered the hero of this part — the sixth generation from Susanoo. The text gives him five names: Ōkuninushi, Ōnamuji, Ashihara-shiko-wo, Yachihoko and Utsushi-kunitama. They surface one by one in the story: as a youth he is Ōnamuji; in the Nether-Distant-Land Susanoo calls him Ashihara-shiko-wo, the Ugly Male of the Reed-Plains; as a wooer he is Yachihoko, the Deity of Eight Thousand Spears; and at the Even Pass of Yomi Susanoo charges him to become Ōkuninushi and Utsushi-kunitama, ruler of the living land. He has many brothers, the Eighty Deities, and a mother, Sashikuni-waka-hime, who twice goes weeping to save him. When the story begins he is the most put-upon of the brothers.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.01','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.2','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',2,'稻羽的白兔','The white hare of Inaba','八十神都想娶稻羽的八上比卖，一起出发去求婚，让大穴牟迟背着袋子，像仆人一样跟在后面。走到气多岬，他们看见一只被剥光了皮的兔子躺在地上。八十神对兔子说：你去海里洗个澡，然后躺在高山顶上吹风。兔子照做了，海水干了以后，它的皮肤被风吹得裂开，痛得直哭。大穴牟迟最后赶到，问它为什么哭。兔子说：我本来住在淤岐岛，想渡海过来，就骗海里的和迩说，我们来比一比谁的族人多，你们排成一行，我从你们背上踩过去数一数。和迩们排好了队，我一路踩着它们的背走过来，快到岸时却说了一句：你们上当了！最后那只和迩就抓住我，剥光了我的衣服。大穴牟迟教它到河口用淡水洗身，再把蒲草的花粉铺在地上，在上面打滚。兔子照做，身体恢复如初。书中说，这就是稻羽的白兔，现在叫作兔神。兔子对他说：八十神得不到八上比卖，虽然你背着袋子，她一定会嫁给你。','The Eighty Deities all wished to marry Princess Yakami of Inaba, and set out together, making Ōnamuji carry their bag like a servant. At Cape Keta they found a hare lying stripped of its fur. They told it to bathe in the sea and lie on a high mountain in the wind; it did, and as the salt dried its skin split, and it lay weeping with pain. Ōnamuji came last and asked why it wept. The hare said: ''I lived on the island of Oki and wanted to cross. I tricked the wani of the sea, saying, let us count whose tribe is larger; line up and I will count you as I step across your backs. They lined up and I trod across, but just before landing I said, you have been deceived! The last wani seized me and stripped off all my clothing.'' Ōnamuji told it to wash in the fresh water of the river-mouth and roll in the pollen of the sedges. It did, and its body was restored. This, says the text, is the White Hare of Inaba, now called the Hare Deity. The hare said: ''The Eighty Deities will not win the princess. Though you carry the bag, she will be yours.''','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.02','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.3','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',3,'手间山的赤猪','The red boar of Mount Tema','八上比卖果然对八十神说：我不听你们的话，我要嫁给大穴牟迟。八十神大怒，商量要杀死大穴牟迟。他们把他带到伯伎国手间山的山脚下——伯伎国大致在今天鸟取县的西部——对他说：山上有一头红色的野猪，我们把它赶下来，你在下面等着抓住它。要是抓不住，就杀了你。他们在山上把一块像野猪那样大的石头用火烧得通红，推下山去。大穴牟迟去抓那块石头，被烧得粘在石头上死了。他的母亲哭着上天，向神产巢日神求救。神产巢日神派了两位女神下来：蚶贝比卖和蛤贝比卖，她们的名字就是赤贝和蛤蜊。蚶贝比卖刮下贝壳上的粉末，蛤贝比卖取来水，把两样东西调和成像母亲的乳汁一样的药，涂遍他的全身。大穴牟迟又活了过来，变成了一个英俊的年轻人，到处走动。这是他第一次死而复生。','Princess Yakami told the Eighty Deities: ''I will not listen to you. I will marry Ōnamuji.'' Furious, they plotted to kill him. They took him to the foot of Mount Tema in Hahaki, roughly the west of present-day Tottori, and said: ''There is a red boar on this mountain. We will drive it down; you wait below and catch it. If you fail, we will kill you.'' Then they heated in fire a great stone shaped like a boar and rolled it down. Ōnamuji caught it, was burned fast to it and died. His mother went up weeping to heaven and begged Kami-musubi for help, and Kami-musubi sent down two women, Princess Cockle-Shell and Princess Clam, named for the ark-shell and the clam. Princess Cockle-Shell scraped powder from her shell and Princess Clam brought water, and they mixed them like a mother''s milk and smeared it over him. He came to life again as a handsome young man and walked about. It was the first time he died and lived again.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.03','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十二节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.4','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',4,'大树的裂缝','The cleft in the tree','八十神看见他又活了，再次设下圈套。他们把大穴牟迟带进山里，砍倒一棵大树，在树干的裂缝里打进一个楔子，让他站进裂缝中间，然后猛地拔掉楔子，把他夹死在树干里。母亲又一次哭着到处寻找，终于找到了他，劈开大树，把他取出来救活了。这已经是他第二次死而复生。母亲对他说：你再住在这里，终究会被八十神害死的。她把他送到木国——也就是后来的纪伊国——的大屋毘古神那里躲避，张伯伦把这位神的名字译作“大屋之王子”。可是八十神一路追到了木国，拉满了弓，向大屋毘古神要人。大屋毘古神就让大穴牟迟从一棵树的树杈之间悄悄逃走，对他说：你到须佐之男所在的根之坚洲国去吧，那位大神一定会为你出主意。大穴牟迟就这样离开了人间，去寻找自己的祖先。','Seeing him alive again, the Eighty Deities set another trap. They led Ōnamuji into the mountains, felled a great tree, drove a wedge into the cleft of the trunk, made him stand in the cleft, and then pulled out the wedge and crushed him to death. Again his mother searched for him weeping, found him, split the tree, took him out and restored him — the second time he had died and lived again. She said: ''If you stay here, you will be destroyed by the Eighty Deities in the end,'' and sent him to the deity Oho-ya-biko, Chamberlain''s Great-House-Prince, in the land of Ki, later called Kii. But the Eighty Deities pursued him there, fitting arrows to their bows and demanding him. Oho-ya-biko let him slip away through the fork of a tree, telling him: ''Go to the Nether-Distant-Land where Susanoo dwells. That great deity will surely counsel you.'' So Ōnamuji left the living world to seek his ancestor.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.04','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十二节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.5','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',5,'须势理毘卖与蛇室','Suseri-bime and the snake room','根之坚洲国——张伯伦译作“幽远之国”——就是当年须佐之男哭着要去的母亲之国，如今他住在这里。大穴牟迟来到须佐之男的宫殿，须佐之男的女儿须势理毘卖出来迎接，两人四目相对，一见钟情，当即结为夫妻。须势理毘卖回到殿里对父亲说：来了一位非常英俊的神。须佐之男出来一看，说：这是苇原色许男啊。“色许男”字面是“丑男子”，也有人理解为强悍的男子。须佐之男把他叫进来，却让他睡在满是毒蛇的蛇室里。须势理毘卖偷偷把一条驱蛇的领巾交给丈夫——领巾是一种披在肩上的长巾——对他说：蛇要咬你的时候，你就把这条领巾挥三下，把它们赶开。大穴牟迟照她的话做了，蛇都安静下来，他安安稳稳地睡了一夜，第二天平安走了出来。这是须佐之男给他的第一次考验，此后还有三次。','The Nether-Distant-Land, the mother''s land that Susanoo had once wept to go to, was now his dwelling. When Ōnamuji came to Susanoo''s palace, Susanoo''s daughter Suseri-bime came out; they looked at each other and were married at once. She went in and told her father: ''A very handsome deity has come.'' Susanoo went out, looked at him and said: ''This is the Ugly Male of the Reed-Plains'' — shiko-wo, literally ugly male, though some read it as a fierce one. He called him in, but made him sleep in a room full of snakes. Suseri-bime secretly gave her husband a snake-scarf, a long cloth worn over the shoulders, and said: ''When the snakes are about to bite you, wave this scarf three times to drive them off.'' He did as she said, the snakes grew quiet, and he slept peacefully and came out unharmed in the morning. It was the first of Susanoo''s trials; three more were to follow.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.05','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.6','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',6,'蜈蚣与蜂之室','The room of centipedes and wasps','第二天晚上，须佐之男又让他睡在一间满是蜈蚣和马蜂的屋子里。须势理毘卖又交给他一条领巾，书中叫作“蜈蚣与蜂之比礼”，像上一次一样教他在危急的时候挥动。大穴牟迟又平安地度过了一夜，第二天照样走了出来。须佐之男的考验并没有到此为止。这位大神当年在高天原上也曾胡作非为，被众神驱逐出天界，如今轮到他来考验来娶自己女儿的年轻人。在这位暴躁的大神眼里，这个年轻人还远远没有证明自己。而在故事里，每一次真正救了大穴牟迟的，都是须势理毘卖的智慧与爱。她站在丈夫一边，悄悄地违背父亲的意愿；在《古事记》里，这样在关键时刻出手相助的女子并不少见，后面的丰玉毘卖和弟橘比卖也是如此。一次次的考验，也让这个曾被兄长们欺负的年轻人渐渐成为能统治国土的神。','The next night Susanoo put him in a room full of centipedes and wasps. Again Suseri-bime gave him a scarf, which the text calls the centipede-and-wasp scarf, and taught him to wave it in danger as before, and again he came out unharmed. Susanoo''s trials were not over. The great deity who had once run riot in High Heaven and been driven out by the gods now tested the young man who had come for his daughter, and in his eyes the youth had proved nothing yet. In the story it is Suseri-bime''s wit and love that save Ōnamuji each time: she takes her husband''s side and quietly works against her father''s will. Women who step in at the decisive moment are not rare in the Kojiki; Toyotama-bime and Oto-tachibana-hime will do the same later on. Trial by trial, the young man once bullied by his brothers was becoming a deity fit to rule the land.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.06','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.7','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',7,'火烧原野与老鼠','The burning moor and the mouse','须佐之男又把一支鸣镝——箭头上装着中空的响器、射出去会呼啸作响的箭——射进一片大草原，命令大穴牟迟去把箭找回来。等他走进草原深处，须佐之男就从四面放火，烧起了整片原野。火从四面围上来，大穴牟迟找不到出路。这时一只老鼠出来对他说：里面空荡荡，外面窄窄窄。他明白了老鼠的意思，用脚一踩地面，就掉进了一个地洞，躲在里面，大火从头顶烧了过去。不久，老鼠又把那支鸣镝衔来交给他，箭上的羽毛已经被小老鼠们啃光了。须势理毘卖以为丈夫已经死了，哭着拿着办丧事的器具来到原野上；须佐之男也以为他死了，走出来站在烧过的原野上。大穴牟迟却拿着那支箭走了出来，把箭献给了须佐之男。老鼠的那两句话，在原文里是“内者富良富良，外者须夫须夫”，读起来就像一句咒语。在高天原上，众神曾用火与笑声把天照大神请出岩户；在这里，救下大穴牟迟的却是一只地洞里的小老鼠。','Next Susanoo shot a whizzing arrow into a great moor and sent Ōnamuji to fetch it. When he was deep in the moor, Susanoo set fire to it all round. Ōnamuji could find no way out, until a mouse came and said: ''Inside, hollow-hollow; outside, narrow-narrow.'' Understanding, he stamped on the ground, fell into a hole, and hid there while the fire passed over him. Soon the mouse brought him the whizzing arrow in its mouth; its feathers had been eaten by the mouse''s children. Suseri-bime, thinking her husband dead, came weeping with the things for a funeral, and Susanoo, thinking the same, stood on the burned moor. But Ōnamuji came out with the arrow and presented it to him. The mouse''s words in the original, uchi wa hora-hora, soto wa subu-subu, sound almost like a spell. In High Heaven the gods had drawn Amaterasu from her cave with fire and laughter; here it is a small mouse in a burrow that saves Ōnamuji.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.07','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.8','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',8,'头上的蜈蚣','Lice that were centipedes','须佐之男把大穴牟迟带回家里，叫进一间八寻宽的大屋子，让他替自己捉头上的虱子。大穴牟迟一看，须佐之男头上爬的根本不是虱子，而是一只只蜈蚣。须势理毘卖悄悄递给丈夫一把椋树的果实和一些红土。椋树的果实是黑紫色的小果子，嚼碎了就像碾碎的虫子。大穴牟迟把椋树果放进嘴里咬碎，再和着红土一起吐出来。须佐之男以为他在把蜈蚣一只只咬碎吐掉，原文说他“心里觉得这个年轻人可爱”，就放心地睡着了。从蛇室、蜈蚣与蜂之室，到火烧原野，再到头上的“虱子”，须佐之男的考验一次比一次危险，而每一次都被他的女儿悄悄化解。这是最后一次考验，也是大穴牟迟逃走的机会。须佐之男曾经在出云斩杀八岐大蛇、救下栉名田比卖，如今他睡着时，命运却交到了这个年轻人手里。','Susanoo took him back into the house, called him into a great room eight fathoms wide, and made him pick the lice from his head. But the creatures on Susanoo''s head were centipedes. Suseri-bime secretly gave her husband berries of the muku tree, small dark fruit that look like crushed insects when chewed, and some red earth. Ōnamuji chewed the berries and spat them out with the red earth, and Susanoo, thinking he was biting the centipedes to pieces and spitting them out, felt fond of him in his heart — the text''s own words — and fell asleep. From the snake room to the centipedes and wasps, the burning moor and now the ''lice'', each trial was more dangerous than the last, and each was quietly undone by his daughter. It was the last trial, and Ōnamuji''s chance to escape. Susanoo, who once slew the eight-forked serpent in Izumo and saved Kushinada-hime, now lay asleep with his fate in the young man''s hands.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.08','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.9','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',9,'生太刀、生弓矢与天诏琴','The sword of life, the bow and the speaking lute','大穴牟迟趁须佐之男睡着，把他的头发一缕缕绑在屋子的椽子上，用一块要五百个人才拉得动的大石头堵住屋门，背起妻子须势理毘卖，带上须佐之男的生太刀、生弓矢和天诏琴，逃了出去。逃跑时天诏琴碰到了树，大地都震动起来。须佐之男被惊醒，一拉头发，把整座屋子都拉倒了。等他把绑在椽子上的头发解开，两人已经逃得很远。须佐之男一直追到黄泉比良坂，远远望着他们喊道：用你拿走的生太刀和生弓矢，把你的兄弟们赶到山坡下、河滩上去！你要成为大国主神，成为宇都志国玉神，让我的女儿须势理毘卖做你的正妻，在宇迦山下把宫柱深深地立在地底的岩石上，把屋梁高高地架到高天原，住在那里吧，你这家伙！大穴牟迟照他的话赶走了八十神，开始造国。八上比卖也来到他身边，但她害怕正妻须势理毘卖，把生下的孩子夹在树杈之间就回去了。','While Susanoo slept, Ōnamuji tied his hair to the rafters of the room, blocked the door with a rock that would take five hundred men to move, took his wife Suseri-bime on his back, and fled carrying Susanoo''s great sword of life, his bow and arrows of life, and his heavenly speaking lute. The lute brushed against a tree and the earth resounded. Susanoo woke and pulled the whole house down with his hair; by the time he had untied it from the rafters they were far away. He pursued them to the Even Pass of Yomi and, gazing after them, called out: ''With the sword of life and the bow of life drive your brothers down the slopes and into the river-beds! Become Ōkuninushi, become Utsushi-kunitama, make my daughter Suseri-bime your chief wife, and at the foot of Mount Uka set your palace pillars deep in the bedrock and raise your cross-beams to the Plain of High Heaven, and dwell there, you rascal!'' He did so, drove away the Eighty Deities and began to make the land. Princess Yakami came to him too, but in fear of the chief wife she left her child in the fork of a tree and went home.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.09','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.10','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',10,'八千矛神的求婚之歌','The songs of the Deity of Eight Thousand Spears','以八千矛神为名的大国主，又到遥远的越国去向沼河比卖求婚。他站在她家门前唱歌，唱自己怎样听说越国有一位聪明美丽的女子，怎样远道而来，拉着门板等到天明，林中的鸟已经开始啼叫。沼河比卖隔着门回唱：请你不要着急，等到夜里，我就会是你的。第二天夜里，两人结为夫妻。正妻须势理毘卖为此十分嫉妒。大国主准备从出云上大和去，一只手搭在马鞍上，一只脚踏在马镫里，唱歌给她听：他换了三套衣服，黑色的不合适，翠鸟一样青色的也不合适，最后换上山田里的染料染成的衣服，才觉得合适；又说，你将来会像山上孤零零的芒草一样哭泣。须势理毘卖举起大酒杯走到他身边，唱道：你是男子，到处都有妻子，而我是女子，除了你没有别的丈夫。两人用酒杯立下誓约，手搭着彼此的脖子，从此和睦相处，书中说他们直到今天仍安然相守。','As Yachihoko, the Deity of Eight Thousand Spears, he went to distant Koshi to woo Princess Nunakawa. Standing at her door he sang how he had heard of a wise and beautiful woman in Koshi, how he had come from afar and stood pulling at her door until dawn while the birds of the forest began to cry. She sang back through the door: be not impatient; when night comes I will be yours. The next night they were married. His chief wife Suseri-bime was deeply jealous. As he was about to go up from Izumo to Yamato, one hand on the saddle and one foot in the stirrup, he sang to her: of three robes he had tried, the black was not right and the kingfisher-blue was not right, but the robe dyed with the juice of the dye-plant sown in the mountain fields suited him; and she would weep like a single clump of grass on the mountain. Suseri-bime came to him lifting a great wine-cup and sang: you are a man and have wives wherever you go, but I am a woman and have no husband but you. They pledged each other with the cup, their hands on each other''s necks, and the text says they are at peace together to this day.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.10','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十四至二十五节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.11','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',11,'少名毘古那','Sukuna-bikona','有一天，大国主在出云的美保岬，看见一位神乘着用萝藦豆荚做成的小船，踏着浪花漂过来，身上穿着用整张剥下来的小动物的皮做的衣服——原文的字一说是鹅，一说指飞蛾。大国主问他的名字，他不回答；问随从的众神，谁也不知道。这时，一只蛤蟆说：久延毘古一定知道。大国主召来久延毘古，久延毘古说：这是神产巢日神的儿子少名毘古那神。大国主去问神产巢日神，神产巢日神说：他确实是我的孩子，是我那些孩子中从我的指缝间漏下去的那一个。你们两个结为兄弟，一起把国土造好、巩固起来吧。从此，大国主和少名毘古那一起造国。后来，少名毘古那渡海去了常世国。书中说，那位知道他名字的久延毘古，就是今天田里的稻草人，虽然不能走路，却知道天下所有的事情。','Once, at Cape Miho in Izumo, Ōkuninushi saw a deity riding the crests of the waves in a little boat made from a seed-pod of the kagami plant, clothed in the whole stripped skin of some small creature — the character is read by some as a goose and by others as a moth. Asked his name, he did not answer, and none of the attendant deities knew it. Then a toad said: ''Kuyebiko will surely know.'' Kuyebiko, called for, said: ''This is Sukuna-bikona, the son of Kami-musubi.'' When Ōkuninushi asked Kami-musubi, she said: ''He is truly my child, the one of my children who slipped through between my fingers. Be brothers, and make and consolidate the land together.'' So the two made the land together, until Sukuna-bikona crossed over the sea to the Eternal Land. Kuyebiko, the text says, is the scarecrow in the fields today, who cannot walk but knows everything under heaven.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.11','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十七节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.12','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',12,'御诸山的神','The deity of Mount Mimoro','少名毘古那走了，大国主忧愁地说：我一个人怎么能把这片国土造好呢？要和哪一位神一起造才好？这时，海面上亮起光来，一位神照着海面过来，说：你要是好好地供奉我，我就和你一起把国土造成。不然的话，国土是造不成的。大国主问：那么，要怎样供奉你呢？那位神说：你把我供奉在大和那青翠如垣墙的群山东边的山上。书中说，这就是住在御诸山上的神。御诸山也就是今天奈良的三轮山。《古事记》在这里没有写出这位神的名字；《日本书纪》的一段异文则说，这位从海上来的神是大国主自己的“幸魂奇魂”。至此，大国主造国的故事告一段落。书中接着列出了大年神的子孙，然后，高天原上的众神开始关心下界的这片国土了。这座山在后来的故事里一直被视为神所居住的山。','With Sukuna-bikona gone, Ōkuninushi said in sorrow: ''How can I make this land alone? With what deity should I make it?'' Then a light shone over the sea, and a deity came illuminating it and said: ''If you will worship me well, I will make the land together with you. Otherwise it cannot be made.'' Ōkuninushi asked how he should worship him, and the deity said: ''Worship me on the eastern mountain of the green fence of Yamato.'' This, says the text, is the deity who dwells on Mount Mimoro, the mountain now called Miwa in Nara. The Kojiki gives the deity no name here; a variant in the Nihon shoki says that the deity from the sea was Ōkuninushi''s own spirit of good fortune and wondrous spirit. Here the making of the land rests. The text goes on to list the children of the Great-Harvest Deity, and then the deities of the Plain of High Heaven turn their attention to the land below. The mountain remains, throughout the later stories, a mountain where a deity dwells.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.12','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二十八节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.13','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',13,'天若日子与雉鸣女','Ame-no-Wakahiko and the pheasant','天照大神说：丰苇原的瑞穗国应该由我的儿子天忍穗耳命来统治。天忍穗耳命站在天之浮桥上往下一看，说：下面吵闹得很！就回来报告了。高御产巢日神和天照大神在天安河边召集众神商议，思金神出主意，先派了天菩比神下去，他却去讨好大国主，三年都没有回来复命。于是又派天若日子，赐给他天之麻迦古弓和天之波波矢。天若日子一到下界，就娶了大国主的女儿下照比卖，想把这片国土据为己有，八年都没有复命。众神又派了一只名叫鸣女的雉鸡去问他。雉鸡停在天若日子门前的桂树上，传达了天神的问话。天佐具卖劝他射死这只鸟，天若日子就用天神赐的弓箭射穿了雉鸡，箭一直飞到天安河边高木神的面前。高木神看见箭羽上有血，说：如果天若日子没有违命，射的是恶神，这支箭就不要射中他；如果他有邪心，就让他死于此箭。他把箭从箭孔投回去，正射中躺在床上的天若日子的胸口，天若日子当场死去。书中说，“当心还矢”这句话就由此而来。','Amaterasu declared that the Luxuriant Reed-Plains Land of Fresh Rice-Ears should be ruled by her son Ame-no-oshi-ho-mimi. He looked down from the Floating Bridge of Heaven, said that the land below was in a great uproar, and went back to report. Takami-musubi and Amaterasu assembled the deities by the Tranquil River of Heaven, and on Omoikane''s counsel they sent Ame-no-hohi, who curried favour with Ōkuninushi and sent no report for three years. Next they sent Ame-no-Wakahiko with the heavenly deer-bow and feathered arrows. He married Ōkuninushi''s daughter Shitateru-hime, schemed to take the land for himself, and sent no report for eight years. The deities sent a pheasant, Naki-me, to question him. She perched on the cassia tree at his gate and spoke the heavenly deities'' words. Urged by Ama-no-sagu-me, he shot the pheasant with the heavenly bow; the arrow passed through her and flew up to Takagi-no-kami by the Tranquil River. Seeing blood on its feathers, Takagi said: ''If Ame-no-Wakahiko has not disobeyed and shot at evil deities, let this arrow not strike him; if he has an evil heart, let him die by it,'' and threw it back down through the hole it had made. It struck Ame-no-Wakahiko in the chest as he lay on his bed, and he died. Hence, says the text, the saying ''beware a returning arrow''.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.13','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十至三十一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect20_32.14','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect20_32',14,'建御雷与让国','Takemikazuchi and the yielding of the land','众神最后派出了建御雷神，由天鸟船神陪同。两位神降落到出云国伊那佐的小滨，建御雷神拔出十拳剑，剑尖朝上倒插在浪头上，盘腿坐在剑尖上，问大国主：天照大神和高木神派我们来问你：你统治的苇原中国，是天神之子应当统治的国土，你意下如何？大国主说：我不能回答，我的儿子八重事代主神会回答，可他到美保岬打鸟钓鱼去了。天鸟船神把事代主找来，事代主说：遵命，把这国土献给天神之子吧。说完踏翻了自己的船，拍出“天之逆手”，把船变成青柴垣，藏身其中。另一个儿子建御名方神用指尖托着千引石走来，要和建御雷比力气。他一抓建御雷的手，那只手就变成了冰柱，又变成了剑刃；建御雷反过来抓住他的手，像抓一根嫩芦苇一样捏碎扔开。建御名方逃到信浓的州羽海，求饶说：我决不离开这里，也不违背父亲和事代主的话。大国主终于说：这片国土就献出去吧。只请为我建一座宫殿，宫柱深深立在地底岩石上，屋梁高高架到高天原，我就隐退在弯弯曲曲的道路深处。于是人们在出云的多艺志小滨建起了宫殿，建御雷神回到天上复命。','Last, the deities sent Takemikazuchi, with Ame-no-tori-fune. The two came down to the little shore of Inasa in Izumo. Takemikazuchi drew his ten-grasp sword, stood it upside down on the crest of a wave, sat cross-legged on its point and asked Ōkuninushi: ''Amaterasu and Takagi-no-kami have sent us to ask: the Central Land of Reed-Plains that you rule is the land the heavenly deity''s child should rule. What is your mind?'' Ōkuninushi said: ''I cannot answer. My son Yae-Kotoshironushi will answer, but he has gone to Cape Miho to hunt birds and fish.'' Fetched, Kotoshironushi said: ''I obey; offer this land to the heavenly deity''s child.'' He trod on his boat to overturn it, clapped his hands in the heavenly backward clap, turned the boat into a green fence and hid within it. Then another son, Takeminakata, came carrying a thousand-draught rock on his fingertips and challenged Takemikazuchi to a trial of strength. When he grasped Takemikazuchi''s hand it turned into an icicle and then into a sword-blade; Takemikazuchi grasped his in return like a young reed, crushed it and flung it away. Takeminakata fled to the sea of Suwa in Shinano and begged for his life, vowing never to leave it and not to go against his father or Kotoshironushi. At last Ōkuninushi said: ''Let this land be offered. Only build me a dwelling with pillars set deep in the bedrock and cross-beams raised to the Plain of High Heaven, and I will retire into the winding ways.'' A palace was built on the shore of Tagishi in Izumo, and Takemikazuchi went back up to report.','claim.v0350.kojiki_okuninushi.14','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十二节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.1','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',1,'天孙降临的诏命','The command to descend','建御雷神回到天上，报告苇原中国已经平定。天照大神和高木神——也就是高御产巢日神——就命令天照大神的儿子天忍穗耳命：现在国土已经平定了，你就照原先的委托，降下去统治那片国土吧。天忍穗耳命回答说：我正在准备降临的时候，生了一个儿子，名叫天迩岐志国迩岐志天津日高日子番能迩迩艺命。应该派这个孩子降下去。这个孩子是天忍穗耳命和高木神的女儿万幡丰秋津师比卖命所生，也就是说，他同时是天照大神和高木神的孙子。于是两位大神对迩迩艺命下令：这丰苇原的瑞穗国，是交给你统治的国土。你就照着命令降下去吧。张伯伦把迩迩艺命的长名字逐字译出，大意是“天地丰饶、天日高照的稻穗丰熟之王子”。这位“天孙”，就是这一部故事的主人公之一。','Takemikazuchi went back up and reported that the Central Land of Reed-Plains had been pacified. Amaterasu and Takagi-no-kami — Takami-musubi — then commanded Amaterasu''s son Ame-no-oshi-ho-mimi: ''The land is now pacified; go down and rule it as we entrusted it to you.'' He answered: ''While I was making ready to descend, a son was born to me, named Ame-nigishi-kuni-nigishi-ama-tsu-hi-daka-hiko-ho-no-ninigi. This child should be sent down.'' The child was born of Ame-no-oshi-ho-mimi and Takagi-no-kami''s daughter Yorozu-hata-toyo-aki-tsu-shi-hime, so that he was grandson both of Amaterasu and of Takagi. The two great deities commanded Ninigi: ''This Luxuriant Reed-Plains Land of Fresh Rice-Ears is the land you are to rule. Descend as commanded.'' Chamberlain translates the long name word by word, roughly Heaven''s-Plenty-Earth''s-Plenty-Heaven''s-Sun-Height-Prince-Rice-ear-Ruddy-Plenty. This August Grandchild is one of the heroes of this part.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.01','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.2','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',2,'天之八衢的猿田毘古','Sarutahiko at the heavenly crossroads','迩迩艺命正要降临的时候，在天之八衢——天上八条道路交会的地方——站着一位神，他身上发出的光，向上照亮了高天原，向下照亮了苇原中国。天照大神和高木神命令天宇受卖神说：你虽然是个柔弱的女子，却是一位能与任何神面对面而不退缩的神。你去问问他：天神的儿子要降临，是谁在这里挡路？天宇受卖神就是当年在天之岩户前狂舞、逗得众神大笑的那位女神。她上前去问，那位神回答说：我是国津神，名叫猿田毘古神。我听说天神的儿子要降临，所以前来迎接，愿意做天孙的向导。原来这位在路口放光的神，并不是来阻拦，而是来引路的。张伯伦把他的名字译作“猿田之王子神”。原文说天宇受卖命是“与神相对而能胜过对方”的神，所以众神派她去问话。《古事记》没有细写猿田毘古的样子；《日本书纪》的一段异文则说他鼻长七咫，背长七尺多，眼睛像八咫镜一样闪闪发亮。','As Ninigi was about to descend, at the heavenly eight-forked crossroads stood a deity whose light shone up to the Plain of High Heaven and down to the Central Land of Reed-Plains. Amaterasu and Takagi-no-kami commanded Ame-no-Uzume: ''Though you are a delicate woman, you are a deity who can face down any deity. Go and ask him who stands in the way where our child is to descend.'' She was the goddess who had once danced before the Heavenly Rock-Dwelling and set the gods roaring with laughter. When she asked, the deity answered: ''I am an earthly deity named Sarutahiko. I heard that the child of the heavenly deities was to descend, and I have come to meet him and to serve as his guide.'' The shining deity at the crossroads had come not to bar the way but to lead it. Chamberlain renders his name the Deity Prince of Saruta. The text says Ame-no-Uzume was a deity who could outface any deity, which is why she was sent. The Kojiki does not describe Sarutahiko; a variant in the Nihon shoki says his nose was seven hands long, his back more than seven feet tall, and his eyes shone like an eight-hand mirror.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.02','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.3','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',3,'神器与五伴绪','The regalia and the five chiefs','天照大神为迩迩艺命安排了五位随从的神，称为“五伴绪”：天儿屋命、布刀玉命、天宇受卖命、伊斯许理度卖命和玉祖命，他们都是当年在天之岩户前出过力的神。天照大神又把当年把她从岩户里引出来的八尺勾玉和镜子，以及须佐之男从大蛇尾中得到的草薙剑交给他，还派了思金神、天手力男神和天石门别神同行。天照大神说：这面镜子，你要把它当作我的御魂，像祭拜我一样祭拜它。又说：思金神，你要承担起祭祀的事务，掌管政事。书中接着说，这两位神如今在五十铃宫受到祭祀，也就是伊势的神宫。故事里的镜、玉、剑，在这里第一次被一起交到天孙手中。书中还一一记下了五位随从神的后人：天儿屋命是中臣连的祖先，布刀玉命是忌部首的祖先，天宇受卖命是猿女君的祖先，伊斯许理度卖命是作镜连的祖先，玉祖命是玉祖连的祖先；天石门别神又名栉石窗神、丰石窗神，是守护宫门的神。','Amaterasu assigned to Ninigi five attendant deities, the five chiefs of companies: Ame-no-koyane, Futo-tama, Ame-no-Uzume, Ishi-kori-dome and Tama-no-oya, all of whom had played their part before the Heavenly Rock-Dwelling. She gave him the curved jewels and the mirror that had drawn her out of the rock-dwelling, and the Kusanagi sword that Susanoo had taken from the serpent''s tail, and sent with him Omoikane, Ame-no-tajikarao and Ame-no-iwato-wake. She said: ''Regard this mirror as my august spirit, and reverence it as you would reverence me,'' and: ''Omoikane, take charge of the rites and carry on the government.'' The text adds that these two are worshipped at the shrine of Isuzu, the shrine at Ise. Mirror, jewels and sword are here placed together in the hands of the August Grandchild for the first time in the story. The text also records the descendants of the five chiefs: Ame-no-koyane is ancestor of the Nakatomi, Futo-tama of the Imbe, Ame-no-Uzume of the Saru-me, Ishi-kori-dome of the mirror-makers and Tama-no-oya of the jewel-makers; Ame-no-iwato-wake, also called Kushi-iwa-mado and Toyo-iwa-mado, is the deity who guards the palace gates.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.03','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.4','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',4,'降临高千穗','The descent on Takachiho','迩迩艺命离开了天上的岩座，分开天上层层叠叠的八重云，威风凛凛地开出一条道路，经过天之浮桥，降落在筑紫日向的高千穗的久士布流多气峰上。天忍日命和天津久米命背着天上的石箭袋，佩着头椎大刀，拿着天上的波士弓和真鹿儿矢，走在天孙的前面开路；书中说，他们是大伴连和久米直两族的祖先。迩迩艺命站在那里说：这里正对着韩国，有一条笔直的路通到笠沙的岬角，早晨的太阳直直照来，傍晚的太阳也照得明亮。所以这里是非常好的地方。他在那里把宫柱深深地立在地底的岩石上，把屋梁高高地架到高天原，住了下来。从高天原到人间的这一段路，就这样走完了。日向的高千穗峰究竟在哪里，后世有宫崎县的高千穗和雾岛山的高千穗峰等不同说法，《古事记》本身没有给出更多线索。','Ninigi left the heavenly rock-seat, pushed apart the eight-fold heavenly clouds, and with mighty way-dividing made his way down by the Floating Bridge of Heaven to the peak of Kuji-furu-take of Takachiho in Himuka in Tsukushi. Ame-no-oshi-hi and Ama-tsu-kume went before him carrying heavenly rock-quivers, girded with mallet-headed swords, holding heavenly bows and true-deer arrows; the text names them as ancestors of the Ōtomo and Kume clans. Ninigi said: ''This place faces Kara; a straight road runs through to the cape of Kasasa; it is a land where the morning sun shines straight and the evening sun shines bright. This is an exceedingly good place.'' There he set his palace pillars deep in the bedrock, raised its cross-beams to the Plain of High Heaven, and dwelt. So the road from High Heaven to the human world was travelled. Where the peak of Takachiho in Himuka lies is disputed — later traditions point to Takachiho in Miyazaki or to the Takachiho peak of the Kirishima mountains — and the Kojiki itself gives no further clue.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.04','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十三至三十四节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.5','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',5,'猿女君与海参','The Duchesses of Saru and the sea-slug','迩迩艺命对天宇受卖命说：你送那位为我们引路的猿田毘古神回去吧。是你把他的名字问出来的，你就承受他的名字，侍奉他吧。所以，书中说，猿女君一族的女子都带着猿田毘古这位男神的名字，被称为“猿女”。后来，猿田毘古神在阿邪诃打鱼，手被比良夫贝夹住，沉进海里淹死了：他沉到海底时，生出了底度久御魂；海水冒泡时，生出了都夫多都御魂；水泡破开时，生出了阿和佐久御魂。天宇受卖命送猿田毘古回去以后返回，把大大小小的鱼都召集起来，问它们：你们愿意侍奉天神的儿子吗？鱼们都说：愿意侍奉。只有海参一声不吭。天宇受卖命说：这张嘴就是不肯回答的嘴！就用小刀把海参的嘴割开了。所以，书中说，海参的嘴至今仍是裂开的。书中还说，此后每一代天皇在位的时候，志摩国进献的海产初物都要分一份给猿女君。','Ninigi said to Ame-no-Uzume: ''Escort Sarutahiko, who served as our guide, back home. Since it was you who learned his name, take his name and serve him.'' Therefore, says the text, the women of the Saru-me clan bear the name of the male deity Sarutahiko and are called the Duchesses of Saru. Later, while fishing at Azaka, Sarutahiko had his hand caught by a hirabu shell and sank and drowned in the sea; as he sank to the bottom, as the water bubbled, and as the bubbles burst, three deities were born. Ame-no-Uzume, coming back from escorting him, gathered all the fishes broad of fin and narrow of fin and asked: ''Will you serve the child of the heavenly deities?'' All said they would — except the sea-slug, which said nothing. ''This is a mouth that gives no answer!'' said Ame-no-Uzume, and slit its mouth with her small knife. That, says the text, is why the sea-slug''s mouth is slit to this day. The text adds that in every reign a share of the first fruits of the sea sent from Shima is granted to the Saru-me.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.05','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十五至三十六节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.6','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',6,'木花之佐久夜毘卖与石长比卖','Konohana-sakuya-hime and Iwanaga-hime','迩迩艺命在笠沙的岬角遇见一位美丽的少女，问她是谁的女儿。少女说：我是大山津见神的女儿，名叫神阿多都比卖，又叫木花之佐久夜毘卖——意思是“像树上的花一样灿烂盛开的公主”。迩迩艺命说：我想娶你，你看怎样？她说：我不能回答，我的父亲大山津见神会回答。迩迩艺命派人去求婚，大山津见神非常高兴，让她的姐姐石长比卖和她一同出嫁，还带上了满满一百桌的礼物。可是石长比卖长得非常丑陋，迩迩艺命一见就害怕，把她送了回去，只留下了妹妹木花之佐久夜毘卖，与她共度一夜。大山津见神因为石长比卖被送回而深感羞耻，说：我把两个女儿一起献上，是有用意的：有石长比卖在，天神子孙的寿命就会像岩石一样长久，不论风雪；有木花之佐久夜毘卖在，他们就会像树上的花一样繁荣。如今你只留下了木花之佐久夜毘卖，天神子孙的寿命就会像树上的花一样短暂。书中说，所以直到今天，历代天皇的寿命都不长久。','At the cape of Kasasa, Ninigi met a beautiful maiden and asked whose daughter she was. She said: ''I am the daughter of the great mountain deity Ōyamatsumi; my name is Kamu-ata-tsu-hime, also Konohana-sakuya-hime'' — the Princess Blossoming like the Flowers of the Trees. He said: ''I wish to marry you. What do you say?'' She answered: ''I cannot say; my father Ōyamatsumi will answer.'' When Ninigi sent to ask, Ōyamatsumi was delighted, and sent her elder sister Iwanaga-hime with her, together with a hundred tables of gifts. But Iwanaga-hime was very ugly; Ninigi took fright at the sight of her and sent her back, keeping only the younger sister, with whom he spent one night. Ōyamatsumi, ashamed at Iwanaga-hime''s return, said: ''I sent both daughters for a purpose: with Iwanaga-hime, the lives of the heavenly deity''s descendants would endure like the rocks through snow and wind; with Konohana-sakuya-hime, they would flourish like the blossoms of the trees. Since you have kept only Konohana-sakuya-hime, their lives will be as brief as the blossoms of the trees.'' That, says the text, is why the lives of the Heavenly Sovereigns down to this day are not long.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.06','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十七节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.7','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',7,'火中生子','Children born in fire','过了些日子，木花之佐久夜毘卖来对迩迩艺命说：我怀孕了，就要生产了。天神的孩子不能私下里生，所以来告诉你。迩迩艺命却说：只过了一夜就怀孕了？这一定不是我的孩子，一定是哪个国津神的孩子。木花之佐久夜毘卖说：如果我怀的是国津神的孩子，生产时就不会平安；如果是天神的孩子，就一定平安。她建起一座八寻宽、没有门的大殿，走进去以后用泥土把四面封死，到生产的时候，就在殿里放起火来。在熊熊大火之中，她生下了三个儿子：火势正旺时生的叫火照命，书中说他是阿多隼人的祖先；接着生的叫火须势理命；最后生的叫火远理命，又名天津日高日子穗穗手见命。张伯伦把火照命和火远理命分别译作“火之照耀”和“火之消退”。三个孩子都平安降生，母亲用这场大火证明了自己的清白。','After some time Konohana-sakuya-hime came to Ninigi and said: ''I am with child and my time is near. The child of a heavenly deity must not be born in secret, so I have come to tell you.'' But Ninigi said: ''With child after a single night? It cannot be mine. It must be the child of some earthly deity.'' She answered: ''If the child I carry is an earthly deity''s, the birth will not go well; if it is the heavenly deity''s, it will surely go well.'' She built a doorless hall eight fathoms wide, went inside, sealed it all round with clay, and when her time came set fire to the hall. In the midst of the flames she bore three sons: the first, born when the fire blazed brightest, Hoderi, whom the text names ancestor of the Hayato of Ata; next Hosuseri; and last Hoori, also called Ama-tsu-hi-daka-hiko-ho-ho-de-mi. Chamberlain renders Hoderi and Hoori as Fire-Shine and Fire-Subside. All three were born safely, and the mother proved her truth by fire.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.07','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十八节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.8','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',8,'海幸与山幸','The luck of the sea and the luck of the mountains','火照命成了海幸彦，用鱼钩捕捞海里大大小小的鱼；火远理命成了山幸彦，用弓箭猎取山里大大小小的野兽。“幸”在这里指的是猎具和收获，张伯伦说英语里没有合适的词，只好译作“运气”。有一天，火远理命对哥哥说：我们交换一下猎具用吧。他请求了三次，哥哥都不答应，最后才勉强换了。火远理命拿着哥哥的鱼钩去钓鱼，一条鱼也没有钓到，还把鱼钩弄丢在海里了。哥哥火照命来要回自己的鱼钩，说：山的猎具是山的，海的猎具是海的，我们还是各自换回来吧。弟弟说：你的鱼钩我钓鱼时丢了。哥哥一定要他还。弟弟就把自己佩带的十拳剑打碎，做成五百个鱼钩来赔，哥哥不要；又做了一千个鱼钩来赔，哥哥还是不要，说：我只要我原来的那个鱼钩。三兄弟的名字都与火有关：火照、火须势理、火远理，像是记下了他们在火中出生时火势的起落。','Hoderi became the Luck of the Sea, catching fish broad of fin and narrow of fin with his hook; Hoori became the Luck of the Mountains, taking beasts rough of hair and soft of hair with his bow. The word sachi here means both the hunting gear and its yield, and Chamberlain says English has no satisfactory equivalent, so he renders it luck. One day Hoori asked his brother to exchange their gear. Three times he asked and was refused, and at last the elder reluctantly agreed. Hoori took the fish-hook to sea, caught not a single fish, and lost the hook in the water. Hoderi came to ask for it back: ''Mountain luck to the mountain, sea luck to the sea — let us each have our own again.'' The younger said he had lost it fishing, but the elder insisted. So Hoori broke up his ten-grasp sword and made five hundred hooks in recompense, but his brother would not take them; he made a thousand, and still his brother refused: ''I want my own hook.'' All three brothers'' names speak of fire — Fire-Shine, Fire-Climax, Fire-Subside — as if recording the rise and fall of the flames in which they were born.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.08','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三十九节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.9','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',9,'盐椎神与海神之宫','The salt deity and the road to the sea palace','火远理命在海边哭泣，这时盐椎神来了，问他：天津日高日子，你为什么哭？他说出了鱼钩的事。盐椎神说：我来为你想个好办法。他编了一艘缝隙紧密、不透水的竹笼小船，让火远理命坐进去，对他说：我把船推出去以后，你就顺着漂一会儿，会遇到一股好的潮流，你就跟着潮流走。前面会有一座宫殿，像鱼鳞一样一层层盖起来的，那就是绵津见神——海神——的宫殿。到了海神宫门口，旁边有一口井，井边有一棵枝叶繁茂的桂树。你就坐在那棵树上，海神的女儿看见你，会替你出主意的。火远理命照着他的话，乘着小船顺着潮流漂去，果然来到了那座鱼鳞般的宫殿前，爬上井边的桂树坐了下来。张伯伦把盐椎神译作“盐之主神”；那艘小船，原文叫“无间胜间之小船”，意思是编得严密、没有一点缝隙的竹笼船。火远理命独自坐在这样的小船里，离开陆地，顺着潮流漂向大海深处。','As Hoori wept on the seashore, the salt deity Shio-tsuchi came and asked: ''Why do you weep, Sky''s Sun-Height?'' He told the story of the hook. Shio-tsuchi said: ''I will make a good plan for you.'' He wove a little boat of bamboo so tightly that no water could enter, set Hoori in it and said: ''When I push the boat off, drift for a while and you will meet a pleasant current; follow it. You will come to a palace built like the scales of fishes: the palace of Watatsumi, the sea deity. By its gate there is a well, and by the well a many-branched cassia tree. Sit in that tree, and the sea deity''s daughter will see you and counsel you.'' Hoori did as he was told, drifted with the current, came to the palace built like fish-scales, and climbed the cassia tree by the well and sat there. Chamberlain renders Shio-tsuchi as the Deity Salt-Possessor; the boat is called in the original a small boat of closely woven bamboo without interstices. Alone in it, Hoori left the land behind and drifted with the current into the depths of the sea.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.09','《古事记》张伯伦译本第四十节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.10','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',10,'井边的玉与海神之女','The jewel in the vessel and the sea deity''s daughter','海神的女儿丰玉毘卖的侍女，拿着玉做的水器来井边打水，看见井水里映出了光，抬头一看，树上坐着一位英俊的男子，觉得十分奇怪。火远理命向她要水喝，侍女就打了水，盛在玉器里献给他。他却没有喝，而是解下颈上的玉，含在嘴里，吐进玉器中。那颗玉粘在器皿上，怎么也取不下来。侍女只好把粘着玉的器皿拿去交给丰玉毘卖。丰玉毘卖觉得奇怪，亲自出门去看，一见到他就被他迷住了，两人四目相对。她回去告诉父亲：我们家门口有一位很美的人。海神亲自出来一看，说：这是天津日高的儿子虚空津日高啊。他把火远理命请进宫里，铺上八层海驴皮的垫子，上面再铺八层绸缎的垫子，请他坐下，摆上一百桌礼物宴请他，把女儿丰玉毘卖嫁给了他。火远理命就在海神之宫住了三年。','A maid of the sea deity''s daughter Toyotama-bime came to the well with a jewelled vessel to draw water. She saw a light in the water, looked up, and saw a handsome man in the tree, and wondered at it. Hoori asked her for water; she drew it and offered it in the jewelled vessel. He did not drink, but loosened a jewel from his neck, took it in his mouth and spat it into the vessel. The jewel stuck fast and could not be removed, so the maid took the vessel, jewel and all, to Toyotama-bime. Wondering, the princess went out to look, was charmed at the sight of him, and they exchanged glances. She told her father: ''There is a beautiful person at our gate.'' The sea deity went out himself and said: ''This is Sora-tsu-hi-daka, the son of Ama-tsu-hi-daka.'' He brought Hoori in, spread eight layers of rugs of sea-asses'' skins and eight layers of silk rugs over them, seated him, feasted him with a hundred tables of gifts, and gave him his daughter Toyotama-bime in marriage. Hoori lived in the sea palace for three years.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.10','《古事记》张伯伦译本第四十节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.11','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',11,'潮盈珠与潮干珠','The tide-flowing and tide-ebbing jewels','三年过去，火远理命想起往事，长长地叹了一口气。丰玉毘卖告诉父亲：他住了三年从不叹气，昨夜却长叹了一声。海神问他缘故，他就讲了鱼钩的事。海神把大大小小的鱼都召集起来，问：有没有哪条鱼拿了这个鱼钩？鱼们说：最近鲷鱼说喉咙里卡了东西，吃不下饭，一定是它。一查鲷鱼的喉咙，果然找到了鱼钩。海神把鱼钩洗干净交给火远理命，教他说：你把鱼钩还给哥哥时，要说“这是个忧郁的钩、焦躁的钩、贫穷的钩、愚蠢的钩”，背过手去交给他。哥哥如果在高处种田，你就在低处种；他在低处种，你就在高处种。我掌管着水，三年之内你哥哥一定会穷下去。他要是因此怨恨来攻打你，你就拿出潮盈珠让潮水淹没他；他要是求饶，就拿出潮干珠让潮水退去救他。海神又召来众鳄，一只长一寻的鳄鱼说一天就能送他回去。火远理命骑着它回到人间，把佩带的小刀系在它的脖子上作为谢礼。回去以后，一切都如海神所说：哥哥越来越穷，凶狠地来攻打他，被潮盈珠淹得求饶，又被潮干珠救起，最后叩头说：从今以后，我日夜做你的守卫。书中说，隼人一族至今还表演溺水的动作，就是由此而来。','After three years Hoori remembered the past and heaved a great sigh. Toyotama-bime told her father: ''In three years he never sighed, but last night he heaved a great sigh.'' The sea deity asked him why, and he told of the hook. The sea deity summoned all the fishes great and small and asked whether any had taken it. They said: ''The sea-bream has lately complained of something stuck in its throat and cannot eat; surely it is that.'' The hook was found in the bream''s throat, washed and given to Hoori, with this counsel: ''When you give it back to your brother, say: this hook is a gloomy hook, a fretful hook, a poor hook, a silly hook — and hand it to him behind your back. If he makes high fields, make yours low; if low, make yours high. I rule the waters, and in three years he will grow poor. If he attacks you in resentment, use the tide-flowing jewel to drown him; if he begs for mercy, use the tide-ebbing jewel to save him.'' Then he summoned the wani, and a one-fathom wani said it could carry Hoori home in a day; Hoori rode it home and tied his small sword to its neck in thanks. All fell out as the sea deity said: the elder grew poor, attacked fiercely, was drowned by the tide-flowing jewel until he begged, was saved by the tide-ebbing jewel, and bowed down: ''Henceforth I will be your guard day and night.'' That, says the text, is why the Hayato still perform the motions of drowning.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.11','《古事记》张伯伦译本第四十至四十一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect33_43.12','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect33_43',12,'鸬鹚羽的产屋','The birth-hut thatched with cormorant feathers','后来，丰玉毘卖亲自从海里出来，对火远理命说：我怀孕了，现在就要生了。天神的孩子不能生在海里，所以我出来了。她在海边建起一座产屋，用鸬鹚的羽毛做屋顶，可是屋顶还没有盖完，她就要生了，只好走进屋里。临产前她对丈夫说：凡是别国的人生孩子的时候，都会变回自己本来的样子。我现在也要变回本来的样子生产，请你千万不要看我。火远理命觉得奇怪，偷偷往里一看，只见妻子变成了一条八寻长的大鳄，在地上爬动扭曲。他吓得转身逃走。丰玉毘卖知道他偷看了，羞愧难当，留下孩子，说：我本想永远通过海路来往，可是你偷看了我的样子，我实在羞耻。她关闭了海与陆地之间的通路，回到了海里。孩子取名为鹈葺草葺不合命，意思是“鸬鹚羽毛还没有盖完的屋顶”。丰玉毘卖虽然怨恨丈夫偷看，却忍不住思念，便让妹妹玉依毘卖来抚养孩子，托她带去一首歌：红色的玉啊，连穿玉的线都在发光；可是像白玉一样的你的模样，更加高贵。火远理命答歌说：在野鸭飞来的海岛上，与我共寝的妻子啊，我一生都不会忘记你。火远理命住在高千穗宫五百八十年。后来，鹈葺草葺不合命娶了姨母玉依毘卖，生下四个儿子，最小的儿子神倭伊波礼毘古命，就是后来的神武天皇。《古事记》的上卷到这里结束。','Later Toyotama-bime herself came out of the sea and said to Hoori: ''I am with child and my time has come. The child of a heavenly deity must not be born in the sea, so I have come out.'' On the shore she built a birth-hut thatched with cormorant feathers, but before the roof was finished her pains came and she went inside. She said to her husband: ''All people of other lands, when they give birth, return to their native form. I too shall take my native form to bear the child. I beg you not to look at me.'' Wondering, he peeped in, and saw that his wife had become a wani eight fathoms long, crawling and writhing. He fled in fear. Knowing he had looked, she was overcome with shame, left the child and said: ''I meant always to come and go by the road of the sea, but you have seen my form, and I am ashamed.'' She closed the boundary between sea and land and went back down into the sea. The child was named Ugaya-fuki-aezu, Cormorant-Thatch-Meeting-Incompletely. Though she resented his peeping, she could not stop longing for him, and sent her younger sister Tamayori-bime to nurse the child, with a song: red jewels — even their string shines; but you, like white jewels, are nobler still. He answered: my wife, with whom I slept on the island where the wild ducks come, I shall not forget you all my life. Hoori dwelt in the palace of Takachiho five hundred and eighty years. Ugaya-fuki-aezu later married his aunt Tamayori-bime and had four sons, the youngest of whom, Kamu-yamato-ihare-biko, is the future Emperor Jimmu. Here the first volume of the Kojiki ends.','claim.v0350.kojiki_ninigi.12','《古事记》张伯伦译本第四十二至四十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.1','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',1,'小碓命与兄长','Wo-usu and his elder brother','景行天皇在缠向的日代宫统治天下，他有许多儿子，其中有一对兄弟：哥哥叫大碓命，弟弟叫小碓命，也叫倭男具那命。有一天，天皇对小碓命说：你哥哥为什么早晚都不来一起用膳？你去好好教导他。过了五天，大碓命还是没有来。天皇问小碓命：你哥哥怎么还不来？你是不是没有教导他？小碓命回答：我已经教导过了。天皇问：你是怎么教导的？小碓命说：清早他去厕所的时候，我等着把他抓住，折断了他的手脚，用草席包起来扔掉了。这段话在书中说得很直接，本篇只交代结果。天皇听了，对这个儿子凶猛暴烈的性情感到害怕。这位少年后来的名字叫倭建命，他的英雄故事，就从父亲的这份恐惧开始。书中在前面的部分还记了一件事：天皇曾派大碓命去美浓召两位少女进宫，大碓命却把她们据为己有，另找别的女子冒名献上；天皇心里明白，却一直闷闷不乐。','The Heavenly Sovereign Keikō ruled from the palace of Hishiro at Makimuku. Among his many sons were two brothers: the elder, Oho-usu, and the younger, Wo-usu, also called Yamato-woguna. One day the sovereign said to Wo-usu: ''Why does your elder brother not come to the morning and evening meals? Go and teach him.'' Five days passed and still the elder brother did not come. The sovereign asked: ''Why does your brother still not come? Have you not taught him?'' Wo-usu said he had. ''How did you teach him?'' Wo-usu answered that he had waited for his brother at dawn when he went to the privy, seized him, broken his limbs, wrapped him in matting and thrown him away. The text tells it bluntly; this retelling gives only the outcome. The sovereign was afraid of his son''s fierce and violent heart. The boy would later be called Yamato Takeru, and his hero''s story begins with his father''s fear. An earlier part tells that the sovereign had sent Oho-usu to summon two maidens of Mino to court, and that Oho-usu took them for himself and sent other women under their names; the sovereign knew and was troubled.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.01','《古事记》张伯伦译本第七十九节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.2','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',2,'少女装扮','Disguised as a girl','天皇说：西方有两个熊曾建，他们不肯归顺，没有礼数，你去把他们杀了。就派小碓命去了西方。那时小碓命还很年轻，头发在额前束成少年的发髻。他向姑母倭比卖命讨来她的上衣和裙子，又把一把短剑藏在怀里，出发了。到了熊曾建的家，只见房子四周围着三重军士，熊曾兄弟正在新建一座石室，准备举行落成的宴会，到处都是人声。小碓命在附近转了转，等着宴会的日子。到了那一天，他把束起的头发放下来梳成少女的样子，穿上姑母的衣裙，完全变成了一个少女，混在女人们中间，走进了石室。熊曾建兄弟一眼看中了这个少女，就让她坐在两人中间，一起饮酒作乐。倭比卖命是天皇的妹妹，在伊势侍奉天照大神；熊曾大致在今天九州的南部。一个束着少年发髻的皇子，就这样穿着姑母的衣裳，独自走进了敌人的宴席。','The sovereign said: ''In the west there are two Kumaso bravoes who will not submit and have no respect. Go and kill them,'' and sent Wo-usu west. He was still young, his hair bound up at the forehead in a boy''s knot. He begged from his aunt Yamato-hime her upper robe and skirt, hid a short sword in his bosom, and set out. At the Kumaso bravoes'' house he found it surrounded by three rings of warriors; the brothers were building a new cave-dwelling for a housewarming feast, and the place was full of noise. Wo-usu walked about nearby, waiting for the day of the feast. When it came he let down his hair like a girl''s, put on his aunt''s robe and skirt, became in every way a young girl, mingled with the women and went into the dwelling. The two Kumaso bravoes were taken with the maiden at once, seated her between them, and feasted. Yamato-hime was the sovereign''s sister, serving Amaterasu at Ise; Kumaso lay roughly in the south of present-day Kyushu. So a prince with a boy''s hair-knot walked alone into his enemies'' feast in his aunt''s clothes.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.02','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.3','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',3,'倭建的名号','The name Yamato Takeru','宴会正热闹的时候，小碓命从怀里拔出短剑，抓住哥哥熊曾建的衣领，一剑刺穿了他的胸膛。弟弟熊曾建吓得转身就逃，小碓命追到石室的台阶下，从背后抓住他，把剑刺了进去。弟弟熊曾建说：不要动这把剑，我有话要说。小碓命就按住他，听他说。熊曾建问：您是谁？小碓命说：我是统治大八岛国、住在缠向日代宫的大带日子淤斯吕和气天皇的儿子，名叫倭男具那王。天皇听说你们熊曾建两个不肯归顺、没有礼数，派我来杀你们。熊曾建说：确实是这样。在西方，没有比我们兄弟两个更勇猛强悍的人了；可是在大倭国，却有比我们更勇猛的男子。我要献给你一个名号：从今以后，你就叫倭建御子吧。说完，小碓命就把他杀了。从此，人们称他为倭建命。“建”在这里是勇猛的意思，“倭建”就是“大和的勇士”。他一生最有名的这个名字，竟是一位敌人临死前送给他的赞许。','When the feast was at its height, Wo-usu drew the sword from his bosom, seized the elder Kumaso bravo by the collar and thrust the sword through his chest. The younger bravo fled in terror; Wo-usu pursued him to the foot of the steps of the dwelling, caught him from behind and thrust the sword into him. The younger bravo said: ''Do not move the sword. I have something to say.'' Holding him down, Wo-usu listened. ''Who is your augustness?'' ''I am the son of the Heavenly Sovereign Ōtarashi-hiko-oshiro-wake, who dwells in the palace of Hishiro at Makimuku and rules the Land of the Eight Great Islands; my name is King Yamato-woguna. The sovereign heard that you two Kumaso bravoes would not submit and had no respect, and sent me to kill you.'' The bravo said: ''It is true. In the west there are none so brave and strong as we two; but in great Yamato there is a man braver than we. I will offer you a name: henceforth be called the August Child Yamato Takeru.'' When he had spoken, Wo-usu killed him. From then on he was called Yamato Takeru. Takeru here means brave: Yamato Takeru is the brave one of Yamato. The name he is best known by was a tribute from a dying enemy.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.03','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.4','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',4,'出云建与木刀','The Izumo bravo and the wooden sword','倭建命回京的路上，把山神、河神和海峡之神一一平定，然后进入出云国，想要杀死出云建。他先和出云建结为朋友，又暗中用赤梼木做了一把假刀，佩在身上。有一天，两人一起在肥河里洗澡。倭建命先从河里出来，把出云建解下的刀佩在自己身上，说：我们来交换刀吧。出云建从河里出来，就佩上了倭建命的那把木刀。倭建命说：来，我们比比刀法吧。两人各自拔刀，出云建拔不出那把木刀，倭建命就拔出真刀，把出云建杀了。他唱了一首歌，大意是：出云建佩的那把刀，刀鞘上缠着一层又一层的藤蔓，看起来很好，里面却没有刀身，真是可怜啊。他平定了西方，回到京城，向天皇复命。这两件事，一靠扮成少女，一靠交换刀剑，都是用计取胜。同样的木刀计和同一首歌，在《日本书纪》里被放进另一个故事：出云的一对兄弟之间。两部书在这里各有各的讲法。','On his way back Yamato Takeru subdued the deities of the mountains, the rivers and the straits, and then went into Izumo intending to kill the Izumo bravo. First he made friends with him, and secretly made a false sword of ichii wood, which he wore. One day they bathed together in the river Hi. Yamato Takeru came out first, girded on the sword the Izumo bravo had taken off, and said: ''Let us exchange swords.'' The Izumo bravo came out and girded on the wooden sword. ''Come,'' said Yamato Takeru, ''let us cross swords.'' Each drew; the Izumo bravo could not draw the wooden sword, and Yamato Takeru drew the real one and killed him. He sang a song whose sense is: the sword the Izumo bravo wore, its scabbard wound with creepers many times — fine to look at, but with no blade inside, alas. Having subdued the west, he returned to the capital and reported to the sovereign. Both deeds, the girl''s disguise and the exchange of swords, were won by trickery. The same wooden-sword trick and the same song appear in the Nihon shoki in a different story, between two brothers of Izumo; the two books tell it each in their own way.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.04','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.5','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',5,'伊势的姑母','The aunt at Ise','倭建命刚刚回来，天皇又命令他说：你去把东方十二道那些凶暴的神和不肯归顺的人平定了。天皇派吉备臣的祖先御锄友耳建日子跟他同行，又赐给他一支柊木做的八寻长矛。倭建命出发去东方，先到了伊势，参拜神宫，对在那里侍奉的姑母倭比卖命说：天皇是不是想让我死？他派我去打西方那些凶暴的人，我回来还没有多久，又不给我军队，就要派我去平定东方十二道的凶暴之人。这样想来，天皇一定是想让我死啊。他一边诉说，一边哭泣。临走时，倭比卖命把草薙剑交给他——这就是须佐之男从八岐大蛇尾中得到、天孙降临时带到人间的那把剑——又给了他一个袋子，说：如果遇到紧急的事情，就打开这个袋子。那支长矛，原文叫“比比罗木之八寻矛”，比比罗木就是柊树。至于袋子里装着什么，他当时并不知道。一个刚刚立下大功的皇子在姑母面前哭泣，这是《古事记》里少有的、写英雄软弱的一幕。','No sooner had Yamato Takeru returned than the sovereign commanded him again: ''Go and subdue the savage deities and unsubmissive people of the twelve roads of the East.'' He sent with him Mi-suki-tomo-mimi-take-hiko, ancestor of the Kibi lords, and gave him a holly-wood spear eight fathoms long. On his way east Yamato Takeru went first to Ise, worshipped at the shrine, and said to his aunt Yamato-hime, who served there: ''Does the sovereign wish me dead? He sent me to strike the savage people of the west, and I have hardly come back before, without giving me any troops, he sends me to subdue the savage people of the twelve roads of the East. Thinking of this, I can only believe he wishes me to die.'' He wept as he spoke. When he left, Yamato-hime gave him the Kusanagi sword — the sword Susanoo took from the serpent''s tail and the August Grandchild brought down from heaven — and a bag, saying: ''If anything urgent happens, open the mouth of this bag.'' The spear is called in the original an eight-fathom spear of hihiragi, the holly. What the bag held he did not yet know. A prince fresh from great deeds weeping before his aunt is one of the rare scenes in the Kojiki that show a hero''s weakness.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.05','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十二节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.6','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',6,'相武的野火','The fire on the moor of Sagamu','倭建命到了尾张国，进了尾张国造的祖先美夜受比卖的家，本想娶她为妻，转念一想，等回来时再娶吧，就和她立下婚约，继续往东去了。他把山河间凶暴的神和不肯归顺的人一一平定。到了相武国，那里的国造骗他说：这片原野里有一个大沼泽，住在沼泽里的神非常凶暴。倭建命就走进原野去看那位神。国造在原野上四面放火。倭建命知道上了当，打开姑母给的袋子，里面是一块打火石。他先用草薙剑割开身边的草，再用打火石点起一圈逆火，把烧来的大火挡了回去，平安地走了出来。他回过头来，把那些国造全部杀死，放火烧掉，所以那个地方至今叫作烧津。《日本书纪》说这把剑因为割草而得名“草薙”，而《古事记》在更早的大蛇故事里就已经这样称呼它了。书中把这件事记在相武国，而“烧津”这个地名今天在骏河，也就是静冈县，这一点历来被注释家讨论。','In Owari, Yamato Takeru entered the house of Miyazu-hime, ancestress of the rulers of Owari, and thought to marry her; then, deciding to marry her on his return, he pledged himself to her and went on east. He subdued the savage deities and unsubmissive people of the mountains and rivers one by one. In Sagamu the local ruler deceived him: ''In this moor is a great marsh, and the deity of the marsh is very violent.'' Yamato Takeru went into the moor to see the deity, and the ruler set fire to the moor all round. Knowing he had been deceived, he opened his aunt''s bag and found a fire-striker. First he mowed the grass around him with the Kusanagi sword, then lit a counter-fire with the striker and drove back the oncoming fire, and came out safe. He turned back, killed all those rulers and burned them, so that the place is still called Yaizu, Burning Ford. The Nihon shoki says the sword was named Kusanagi, Grass-Mower, from this deed; the Kojiki already calls it so in the earlier serpent story. The text places this in Sagamu, while the name Yaizu belongs today to Suruga, in Shizuoka — a discrepancy long discussed by commentators.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.06','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.7','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',7,'弟橘比卖投海','Oto-tachibana-hime enters the sea','倭建命从那里出发，渡过走水海。海峡的神掀起大浪，船在原地打转，无法前进。这时，倭建命的妃子弟橘比卖命说：我代替皇子进入海中吧。皇子应当完成被派来做的事，回去向天皇复命。她要入海的时候，在浪上铺了八层菅草垫子、八层皮垫子和八层绸缎垫子，坐在上面，沉入了海中。汹涌的波浪自己平息下来，船又能前进了。弟橘比卖临走前唱了一首歌，大意是：在相武那群山耸立的小原野上，熊熊大火之中，你站在火里还在问我是否平安——啊，你啊。七天以后，她的梳子漂到了岸边。倭建命拾起那把梳子，为她修了陵墓，把梳子安放在里面。这首歌让人知道，在相武原野的大火里，她一直在丈夫的身边。走水海大致是今天东京湾口的浦贺水道一带，水流湍急，所以叫作“走水”。','From there Yamato Takeru crossed the sea of Hashirimizu. The deity of the strait raised great waves; the boat spun round and could not go forward. Then his consort Oto-tachibana-hime said: ''I will enter the sea in the prince''s stead. The prince must accomplish the task he was sent on and report to the sovereign.'' When she was about to enter the sea, she spread eight layers of sedge rugs, eight layers of skin rugs and eight layers of silk rugs on the waves, sat on them, and sank into the sea. The raging waves calmed of themselves and the boat could go on. Before she went she sang a song whose sense is: on the little moor of Sagamu among the standing peaks, in the midst of the burning fire, you stood and asked after me — ah, you. Seven days later her comb drifted ashore. Yamato Takeru took up the comb, made her a tomb and laid the comb in it. Her song shows that in the fire on the moor of Sagamu she had been at her husband''s side. The Hashirimizu sea is roughly the Uraga channel at the mouth of Tokyo Bay, its current so swift that it was called Running Water.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.07','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十四节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.8','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',8,'足柄坂的白鹿','The white deer on the Ashigara pass','倭建命继续深入东方，把凶暴的虾夷人都平定了，又平定了山河间凶暴的神，然后踏上了回程。来到足柄坂下，他正在吃干粮，那座山坡的神变成一只白鹿，走过来站在他面前。倭建命拿起吃剩的野蒜，等白鹿走近时扔过去，正好打中了它的眼睛，把它打死了。他登上足柄坂，回望来路，想起沉入海中的弟橘比卖，长叹了三声：吾妻哉！——“啊，我的妻子！”所以，书中说，从此人们就把那片土地叫作“阿豆麻”，也就是“东国”。这一声叹息，成了一个地方的名字。倭建命一路征战，从不诉苦，只在这里流露了一次深深的悲伤。虾夷指当时东方不服从朝廷的人们；足柄坂在今天神奈川与静冈交界的山上。白鹿被野蒜打中眼睛，这样细小的一笔，让这位山神的死显得格外突然。','Yamato Takeru went on deep into the East, subdued all the savage Emishi, pacified the savage deities of the mountains and rivers, and set out to return. At the foot of the Ashigara pass, as he was eating his dried rice, the deity of the pass changed into a white deer and came and stood before him. He took up the leftover end of the wild chive he had been eating, waited for the deer to come near, threw it, struck it in the eye and killed it. Then he climbed the pass, looked back the way he had come, remembered Oto-tachibana-hime who had sunk into the sea, and sighed three times: ''Azuma haya!'' — ''Alas, my wife!'' Therefore, says the text, the land has been called Azuma, the East. A sigh became the name of a land. Through all his fighting Yamato Takeru never complained; only here does he show his deep grief. The Emishi were the peoples of the East who did not submit to the court; the Ashigara pass lies in the mountains on the border of present-day Kanagawa and Shizuoka. The small detail of the wild chive striking the deer''s eye makes the mountain deity''s death all the more sudden.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.08','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十五节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.9','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',9,'酒折宫的火守老人','The old fire-keeper at Sakaori','倭建命越过足柄坂，进入甲斐国，住在酒折宫里。有一天，他唱了一句歌：越过新治和筑波，已经睡了几夜？——意思是说，离开东方的新治与筑波以来，已经过了多少个夜晚了？身边的人没有一个能接下去。这时，一个在旁边看守火堆的老人接着唱道：数一数日子，夜是九夜，日是十日。倭建命十分赞赏这个老人，就封他为东国造。这一问一答的两句歌，后世被看作日本连歌的起源之一。在征战的长途中，这是少有的安静时刻：一位疲倦的皇子数着离家的日子，一位卑微的老人替他把日子数了出来。新治与筑波都在今天的茨城县，甲斐就是今天的山梨县，酒折宫的旧址在今天的甲府。身边的随从没有一个答得上来，答出这一句的，却是一个看火的老人。','Crossing from the pass into Kai, Yamato Takeru stayed at the palace of Sakaori. One day he sang a line: since passing Niibari and Tsukuba, how many nights have I slept? — that is, how many nights since leaving Niibari and Tsukuba in the East? None of his followers could answer it. Then an old man who was tending the fire took it up: counting the days, of nights there have been nine, of days ten. Yamato Takeru praised the old man and made him ruler of the Eastern Land. This exchange of two lines has been seen in later times as one of the beginnings of linked verse in Japan. On the long campaign it is a rare quiet moment: a weary prince counting the days away from home, and a humble old man counting them for him. Niibari and Tsukuba are in present-day Ibaraki, Kai is present-day Yamanashi, and the site of Sakaori is in today''s Kofu. None of his attendants could answer; it was the old man watching the fire who did.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.09','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十六节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.10','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',10,'美夜受比卖与伊吹山','Miyazu-hime and Mount Ibuki','倭建命从甲斐越过科野，平定了科野坂的神，回到尾张，进了美夜受比卖的家，履行当初的约定。两人以歌相答，结为夫妻。随后，他把草薙剑留在美夜受比卖的身边，空着手去讨伐伊服岐能山——伊吹山——的神，说：这座山的神，我空手就能把他抓住。他登上山去，在山坡上遇见一头白色的野猪，大得像一头牛。倭建命大声说：这头变成白猪的，是山神的使者吧。现在不杀它，等我回来的时候再杀。说完就继续往上走。可是那头白猪并不是使者，正是山神本人。因为他说了这样的大话，山神降下了猛烈的冰雹，打得倭建命神志昏迷。这把一路保护他的剑，此刻已经不在他的身边。书中还记下，宴会上倭建命看见她外衣的下摆沾着月事的血迹，便以歌相问，她以歌相答，说日月流转，等他等得月亮都已升起。伊吹山在今天滋贺与岐阜的交界处。后世注释家认为，倭建命对着山神口出狂言——原文称为“言举”——正是招来灾祸的原因。','From Kai Yamato Takeru crossed Shinano, subdued the deity of the Shinano pass, and came back to Owari, to Miyazu-hime''s house, to keep his pledge. They exchanged songs and were married. Then he left the Kusanagi sword with Miyazu-hime and went bare-handed to take the deity of Mount Ibuki, saying: ''As for the deity of this mountain, I will take him with my bare hands.'' Climbing the mountain, he met on its slope a white boar as big as an ox. He said aloud: ''This one that has turned into a white boar must be the messenger of the mountain deity. I will not kill it now; I will kill it on my way back,'' and went on climbing. But the white boar was no messenger; it was the deity himself. Because of these boastful words the deity sent down a great storm of hail that struck Yamato Takeru senseless. The sword that had protected him all the way was no longer at his side. The text also tells that at the feast he saw the stain of her monthly blood on the hem of her robe and questioned her in song, and she answered in song that months had passed and the moon had risen while she waited for him. Mount Ibuki lies on the border of present-day Shiga and Gifu. Later commentators see his boastful speech against the deity — kotoage, speaking out — as what brought the disaster on him.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.10','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十七至八十八节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.11','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',11,'三重的腿','Legs like a triple-bent rice cake','倭建命下了山，来到玉仓部的清泉边休息，神志稍稍清醒了一些，所以那眼泉被叫作居寤清水。他继续往前走，到了当艺野，说：我的心本来总想在天空中飞翔，可现在我的脚却走不动了，变得蹒跚踉跄。所以那里叫作当艺。他又拄着手杖慢慢地走，那段坡就叫杖衡坂。到了尾津岬的一棵孤松下，他发现以前在这里吃饭时忘下的一把刀，竟然还在原处。他唱道：尾津岬那棵孤松啊，我的兄长，你若是人，我就给你佩上刀，给你穿上衣服。到了三重村，他说：我的腿像三重弯曲的年糕一样，累极了。所以那里叫作三重。书中一路记下他留下的地名，像是记下了他越来越沉重的脚步。当艺在今天的岐阜一带，尾津岬在伊势的桑名附近，据说今天三重县的名字也与这里有关。那棵孤松下的刀，原来一直在等着它的主人。','Coming down the mountain, he rested by the clear spring of Tamakurabe and his mind cleared a little, so the spring is called the Awakening Spring. He went on to the moor of Tagi and said: ''My heart always longed to fly through the sky, but now my feet will not walk; they stagger.'' So the place is called Tagi. Leaning on a staff he walked slowly on, and the slope is called the Staff Slope. At the single pine on Cape Otsu he found a sword he had left there once while eating, still lying where he had left it, and he sang: single pine of Cape Otsu, my elder brother — if you were a man, I would gird you with a sword and clothe you. At the village of Mie he said: ''My legs are like a rice cake bent three times; I am utterly weary.'' So the place is called Mie. The text records the place-names he left behind, as if marking his ever heavier steps. Tagi lies in present-day Gifu and Cape Otsu near Kuwana in Ise; the name of Mie Prefecture is said to come from this place. The sword under the lone pine had been waiting all along for its master.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.11','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十九节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect79_90.12','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect79_90',12,'思国之歌与白鸟','The songs of home and the white bird','到了能烦野，倭建命思念故乡，唱道：大和是国中最好的地方，层层叠叠的青山像垣墙一样环绕，大和啊，多么美好。又唱：愿那些性命完好的人，把平群山上的熊白梼叶插在头上。又唱：啊，从我家那边升起的云啊。病势沉重的时候，他唱了最后一首歌：我把那把大刀留在了少女的床边——那把刀啊！唱完就死了。使者把消息报告给京城。他的妃子和孩子们赶来，在那里修了陵墓，在周围的稻田里爬来爬去，哭着唱歌。这时，倭建命化作一只八寻长的白鸟，飞上天空，向海边飞去。妃子和孩子们在后面追，赤脚被竹子的残茬划破也不觉得痛，一边追一边唱。书中说，这四首歌都在他的葬礼上唱过，所以直到今天，天皇的大葬还唱这些歌。白鸟飞到河内的志几停了下来，人们在那里修了陵墓，叫作白鸟御陵。可是白鸟又从那里飞上天空，飞走了。','At the moor of Nobo, longing for his home, he sang: Yamato is the best of lands; the green mountains fold round it like a fence; Yamato, how lovely. And: let those whose lives are whole put leaves of the bear-oak of Mount Heguri in their hair. And: ah, the clouds rising from the direction of my home. When his sickness grew grave he sang a last song: by the maiden''s bed I left my great sword — that sword! — and when he had sung it he died. Messengers carried the news to the capital. His consorts and children came down, made a tomb there, and crawled about the rice fields around it, weeping and singing. Then Yamato Takeru turned into a great white bird eight fathoms long, rose into the sky and flew towards the shore. His consorts and children followed, their bare feet cut on bamboo stubble, feeling no pain, singing as they went. The text says these four songs were all sung at his funeral, and so they are sung at the great funerals of the Heavenly Sovereigns to this day. The white bird stopped at Shiki in Kawachi, and a tomb was made there called the Tomb of the White Bird. But from there it rose into the sky again and flew away.','claim.v0350.kojiki_yamato_takeru.12','《古事记》张伯伦译本第八十九至九十节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.1','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',1,'大气之女与鸭蛋','The daughter of the air and the duck''s eggs','在最古老的时候，有一位少女，她是大气的女儿，名叫伊尔玛塔尔。她独自在空旷的天空中住了很久很久，渐渐觉得寂寞，就从天上降下来，落到了无边无际的大海上。狂风吹起了海浪，海浪使她怀了孕，她成了“水之母”，在海上漂流了七百年，却始终生不出孩子来。她向至高的天神乌戈哀求，请他来帮助她摆脱痛苦。不久，一只美丽的鸭子在海面上飞来飞去，想找一个地方筑巢，却到处找不到陆地。伊尔玛塔尔从水里抬起膝盖，鸭子以为那是一座小岛，就落在她的膝盖上筑了巢，下了六个金蛋，第七个是铁蛋。鸭子孵着蛋，伊尔玛塔尔觉得膝盖越来越烫，像要被火烧着一样，忍不住动了一下，蛋就滚进海里，摔碎了。可是蛋的碎片并没有消失：下半部的蛋壳变成了大地，上半部变成了高高的天穹，蛋黄变成了太阳，蛋白变成了月亮，蛋壳上的斑点变成了星星，黑色的碎片变成了天上的云。','In the oldest times there was a maiden, daughter of the air, named Ilmatar. She lived alone for ages in the empty spaces of the sky until she grew weary of it, and came down to the boundless sea. The wind raised the waves, and the waves made her with child; she became the Water-mother and floated on the sea for seven hundred years without giving birth. She cried to Ukko, the highest god, to free her from her pain. Soon a beautiful duck came flying over the waters looking for a place to nest, but there was no land anywhere. Ilmatar raised her knee from the water; the duck took it for a little island, built her nest on it and laid six golden eggs and a seventh of iron. As the duck brooded, Ilmatar felt her knee grow hotter and hotter until it seemed to burn, and she moved it; the eggs rolled into the sea and broke. But the pieces did not perish: the lower halves of the shells became the earth, the upper halves the high vault of heaven, the yolk the sun, the white the moon, the specks on the shells the stars, and the dark fragments the clouds of the sky.','claim.v0350.kal_1.01','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.2','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',2,'万奈莫宁的诞生','The birth of Väinämöinen','世界有了形状，伊尔玛塔尔又在海上漂了许多年，开始塑造大地的样子：她把手一指，就出现了岬角；用脚一踩，就有了鱼儿栖息的深坑；侧身一躺，就形成了平滑的海岸；她脚踏过的地方成了海湾，头枕过的地方成了港口。可是她的儿子还没有出生。万奈莫宁在母亲的腹中待了三十个夏天，又待了同样多的冬天，在黑暗中渐渐长大。他向太阳、月亮和大熊星祈求：请给我打开一条道路，让我走出这狭小的地方，去看看天空的太阳和月亮。可是没有回应。于是他自己用无名指推开了城堡的门，用左脚的脚趾拨开了骨头的门闩，从母亲的身体里爬了出来，一头扎进了海里。他在海浪中漂了好几年，终于来到一片无名的海岬，爬上了岸。这位后来被称为“永恒的歌者”的万奈莫宁，一出生就已经是一个老人了。','With the world given shape, Ilmatar floated on for many years and began to form the land: where she pointed her hand, headlands rose; where she pressed with her foot, deep pools for the fish were made; where she lay on her side, smooth shores appeared; where her feet touched, bays were formed, and where her head rested, harbours. But her son had not yet been born. Väinämöinen stayed in his mother''s womb thirty summers and as many winters, growing in the dark. He prayed to the sun, the moon and the Great Bear: open a way for me out of this narrow place, that I may see the sun and moon in the sky. No answer came. So he pushed open the castle gate with his ring finger, slid back the bony bolt with the toes of his left foot, crawled out of his mother''s body and plunged headlong into the sea. He drifted on the waves for years until he came to a nameless headland and climbed ashore. Väinämöinen, later called the eternal singer, was already an old man at his birth.','claim.v0350.kal_1.02','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.3','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',3,'播种与大橡树','The sowing and the great oak','万奈莫宁上了岸，看见大地光秃秃的，什么也不长。他请来播种者桑普萨·佩勒沃伊宁，让他在大地上撒下种子：山上种松树，山丘种冷杉，沼泽长桦树，低地长赤杨，湿地长柳树，河岸长稠李。树木都长起来了，只有橡树不肯发芽。后来一位从海里来的巨人帮忙，把一颗橡子种了下去，这棵橡树长得太大了，树冠遮住了天上的云，挡住了太阳和月亮，大地一片黑暗。万奈莫宁祈求母亲派人来砍倒它。海里走上来一个小小的男人，只有拇指那么高，穿着铜甲；可一转眼，他就长成了顶天立地的巨人，挥斧三下，把大橡树砍倒了，阳光重新照到大地上。接着，万奈莫宁开始种大麦。一只山雀告诉他：要先烧林开荒。他砍倒了整片森林，只留下一棵白桦，让鸟儿有地方栖息；一只鹰为此感谢他，替他打火烧林。他撒下麦种，向乌戈祈雨，大麦就长了出来。','Coming ashore, Väinämöinen saw that the land was bare and nothing grew. He called the sower Sampsa Pellervoinen to scatter seed over the earth: pines on the hills, firs on the knolls, birches in the swamps, alders in the lowlands, willows in the wet places, bird-cherries by the rivers. Every tree grew except the oak. Then a giant from the sea planted an acorn, and the oak grew so great that its crown hid the clouds and blocked the sun and moon, and the earth was dark. Väinämöinen prayed to his mother to send someone to fell it. A little man rose from the sea, no taller than a thumb, armoured in copper; in a moment he grew into a giant reaching the sky, and with three strokes of his axe he felled the oak, and the sunlight returned. Then Väinämöinen began to sow barley. A titmouse told him to burn the forest first. He felled the whole forest but left one birch for the birds to rest in; an eagle, thankful, struck fire to burn the clearing for him. He sowed the grain, prayed to Ukko for rain, and the barley grew.','claim.v0350.kal_1.03','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第二章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.4','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',4,'约卡海宁的挑战','Joukahainen''s challenge','万奈莫宁的歌声和智慧传遍了四方。北方有一个年轻的歌手，名叫约卡海宁，听说有人唱得比他好，心里很不服气，不顾父母的劝阻，赶着雪橇去找万奈莫宁比试。两架雪橇在路上迎面相撞，缰绳缠在一起。约卡海宁开始炫耀自己的学问，说起鱼儿在哪里产卵、山上长着什么树、天地是怎样造成的，甚至说世界是他造的。万奈莫宁说：这些都是孩子和女人的学问，你所说的，你都不在场。约卡海宁恼羞成怒，要和他拔剑决斗。万奈莫宁不屑与他相斗，就唱起歌来。随着他的歌声，约卡海宁的雪橇变成了湖边的灌木，马变成了河边的石头，弓变成了水上的彩虹，箭变成了天上的鹰，他自己则一点点沉进了沼泽，泥水没过了腰，没过了胸，一直没到了下巴。这是《卡勒瓦拉》里有名的“唱歌比赛”：两位歌手比的不是力气，而是谁知道万物的起源；而在这部史诗里，知道一样东西的来历，就意味着拥有支配它的力量。','The fame of Väinämöinen''s songs and wisdom spread far and wide. In the north lived a young singer named Joukahainen, who could not bear to hear that anyone sang better than he. Against his parents'' warnings he drove off in his sledge to challenge Väinämöinen. Their sledges met head-on on the road and their reins tangled. Joukahainen began to boast of his learning — where the fish spawn, what trees grow on the hills, how heaven and earth were made — and even claimed that he had made the world. Väinämöinen answered that this was the lore of children and women, and that Joukahainen had been present at none of it. Stung, the young man challenged him to fight with swords. Scorning to fight, Väinämöinen began to sing. As he sang, Joukahainen''s sledge became bushes by a lake, his horse a stone by the river, his bow a rainbow over the water, his arrows hawks in the sky, and he himself sank little by little into a swamp, the mire rising to his waist, to his breast, to his chin. This is the Kalevala''s famous singing contest: the two singers compete not in strength but in knowing the origins of things, and in this epic to know where a thing came from is to have power over it.','claim.v0350.kal_1.04','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.5','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',5,'用妹妹换回性命','A sister for a life','约卡海宁陷在沼泽里，吓坏了，开始求饶。他说：放了我吧，我给你两张好弓；万奈莫宁不要，继续往下唱。他又说：我给你两条船；我给你两匹快马；我给你满盔的金子和满帽的银子；我给你所有的田地和庄稼。万奈莫宁都不要，泥水越升越高。最后，约卡海宁说：我把妹妹艾诺许给你，让她给你打扫屋子、烤面包、织布，做你的妻子。万奈莫宁听了，心里非常欢喜，就坐在一块喜悦的石头上，把刚才的歌倒过来唱了一遍，约卡海宁从沼泽里升了上来，马、雪橇和弓箭也都恢复了原样。约卡海宁垂头丧气地回到家，告诉母亲他把妹妹许给了老万奈莫宁。母亲却高兴地说：这正是我一直盼望的事，能有那位大智者做女婿。只有艾诺听了，坐在台阶上哭了起来。一个少女的婚事，就这样在两个男人的较量中被定了下来，而她本人一句话也没有被问过。','Sunk in the swamp, Joukahainen was terrified and began to beg. ''Let me go, and I will give you two fine bows.'' Väinämöinen would not take them and sang on. ''I will give you two boats... two swift horses... a helmet full of gold and a hat full of silver... all my fields and harvest.'' Väinämöinen refused them all, and the mire rose higher. At last Joukahainen said: ''I will give you my sister Aino, to sweep your house, bake your bread and weave your cloth, and be your wife.'' At this Väinämöinen rejoiced; he sat down on a stone of joy and sang his song backwards, and Joukahainen rose out of the swamp, and his horse, sledge and bow returned to their own shapes. Joukahainen went home crestfallen and told his mother he had promised his sister to old Väinämöinen. But his mother was glad: it was what she had always hoped for, to have the great sage as her son-in-law. Only Aino, hearing it, sat down on the steps and wept. So a maiden''s marriage was settled in a contest between two men, and no one had asked her a single word.','claim.v0350.kal_1.05','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三至四章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.6','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',6,'艾诺的命运','The fate of Aino','艾诺到林中去折桦树枝做浴帚，在那里遇见了万奈莫宁。老人说：少女啊，不要为别人戴珠子和十字架，只为我戴吧。艾诺扯下胸前的十字架、手上的戒指、颈上的珠子和头上的发带，扔在地上，哭着跑回家去。母亲劝她不要哭，叫她打开阁楼上的箱子，穿上最好的衣服，戴上金银首饰——那都是母亲年轻时织的、留给她出嫁用的。艾诺却说：我宁愿嫁给大海里的鲑鱼，也不愿做老人的拐杖。她穿上最好的衣服，离开家，一连走了好几天，来到海边。她看见三个少女在海里洗澡，就脱下衣服，游到海中的一块彩色岩石上。岩石却沉进了海底，艾诺也跟着沉了下去，再也没有回来。一只野兔把消息带回了她的家。母亲哭了很久，她的眼泪流成了三条河，河边长出了三棵白桦，桦树上落下三只杜鹃，一只唱着“爱啊”，一只唱着“新郎啊”，一只唱着“欢乐啊”。诗中母亲后来悔恨地说：做母亲的人啊，千万不要逼女儿嫁给她不喜欢的人。','Aino went into the woods to break birch twigs for a bath-whisk and met Väinämöinen there. ''Maiden,'' said the old man, ''wear your beads and cross for no one but me.'' Aino tore the cross from her breast, the rings from her fingers, the beads from her neck and the ribbons from her hair, threw them on the ground, and ran home weeping. Her mother told her not to weep, but to open the chest in the loft and put on her finest clothes and her gold and silver — things her mother had woven and kept for her wedding when she was young. Aino said she would rather be the bride of the salmon in the sea than the staff of an old man. She put on her finest clothes, left home, walked for days, and came to the sea. Seeing three maidens bathing, she took off her clothes and swam out to a bright-coloured rock. The rock sank to the bottom, and Aino sank with it and never came back. A hare carried the news home. Her mother wept so long that her tears became three rivers; three birches grew by them, and on the birches sat three cuckoos, one calling love, one calling bridegroom, one calling joy. The mother later says in her grief: mothers, never force a daughter to marry a man she does not want.','claim.v0350.kal_1.06','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.7','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',7,'鱼与失去的新娘','The fish and the lost bride','万奈莫宁听说艾诺死了，日夜悲伤。他到海边去钓鱼，钓了很久，钓上来一条奇怪的鱼，它不像鲑鱼，不像鳟鱼，也不像任何他见过的鱼。他拿出刀来，想把它剖开做早饭，那条鱼却从他手里一跃而起，跳回了海里，在离船不远的水面上抬起头来说：我不是给你做早饭的鲑鱼。我就是艾诺，约卡海宁的妹妹，你想娶的那个少女。我本想做你的妻子，为你铺床、做饭，可你没有认出我来。说完，它就沉入了深海。万奈莫宁拼命撒网、垂钓，再也找不到那条鱼了。他悲伤地回到家里，觉得自己老了，再也没有欢乐了。这时，他已故的母亲从海浪下的坟墓里醒来，对他说：不要为约卡海宁的妹妹悲伤，到北方的波赫约拉去吧，那里有更美丽、更聪明的少女。','Hearing that Aino was dead, Väinämöinen grieved day and night. He went to the sea to fish and after long fishing caught a strange fish — not a salmon, not a trout, not like any fish he had seen. He took out his knife to cut it up for breakfast, but the fish leapt from his hand back into the sea, raised its head from the water near the boat, and said: ''I am no salmon for your breakfast. I am Aino, Joukahainen''s sister, the maiden you sought. I would have been your wife, made your bed and cooked your food, but you did not know me.'' Then it sank into the depths. Väinämöinen cast his nets and lines again and again but never found that fish. He went home in sorrow, feeling old and joyless. Then his dead mother woke in her grave beneath the waves and said: ''Do not grieve for Joukahainen''s sister. Go north to Pohjola, where there are maidens fairer and wiser.''','claim.v0350.kal_1.07','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第五章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.8','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',8,'约卡海宁的箭','Joukahainen''s arrows','万奈莫宁骑上一匹轻得像豆秆一样的马，踏着海浪向北方的波赫约拉出发。约卡海宁却一直记恨着沼泽里的耻辱。他做了一张钢弓，配上蛇筋一样的弓弦，削好了箭，蘸上了毒，埋伏在路边等着。母亲劝他不要射，说万奈莫宁是大地的歌者，他死了，世上的欢乐和歌声都会消失。约卡海宁不听。他射出第一支箭，飞得太高，射进了云里；第二支箭飞得太低，钻进了地下；第三支箭射中了万奈莫宁胯下的马。马倒下了，万奈莫宁跌进了大海，被风浪卷走了。约卡海宁回家得意地告诉母亲：我射死了老万奈莫宁。母亲说：你做了一件坏事，你射死了大地上最好的歌者。约卡海宁的母亲说得没错：在《卡勒瓦拉》里，歌声就是力量，也是世间欢乐的来源。好在这位“永恒的歌者”并没有死，大海托着他，像当年他出生时一样，把他带向远方。万奈莫宁在海浪中漂流，无依无靠，这时他已经离家很远，离北方的波赫约拉却越来越近了。','Väinämöinen mounted a horse as light as a pea-stalk and set off over the waves for northern Pohjola. But Joukahainen had never forgotten his shame in the swamp. He made a bow of steel, strung it with a string like a serpent''s sinew, shaped his arrows and dipped them in poison, and lay in wait by the way. His mother begged him not to shoot: Väinämöinen was the singer of the land, and if he died, joy and song would vanish from the world. He would not listen. His first arrow flew too high, into the clouds; his second too low, into the earth; his third struck the horse beneath Väinämöinen. The horse fell, Väinämöinen tumbled into the sea, and the wind and waves carried him away. Joukahainen went home and boasted to his mother that he had killed old Väinämöinen. ''You have done an evil thing,'' she said. ''You have shot the best singer on earth.'' Joukahainen''s mother was right: in the Kalevala song is power and the source of the world''s joy. But the eternal singer did not die; the sea bore him up, as it had at his birth, and carried him far away. Drifting helpless on the waves, he was far from home and drawing ever nearer to the north.','claim.v0350.kal_1.08','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第六章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.9','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',9,'鹰与洛希','The eagle and Louhi','万奈莫宁在海上漂了许多天，又冷又饿，看不到岸。这时，一只巨大的鹰从天边飞来，它记得当年万奈莫宁烧林时为鸟儿留下了一棵白桦，就对他说：你曾经为鸟儿留下了栖息的树，现在我来报答你。它让万奈莫宁骑在背上，一直把他驮到北方的波赫约拉海边，放下他就飞走了。万奈莫宁在陌生的海岸上哭泣，哭声传到了波赫约拉的女主人洛希那里。缺了牙的洛希坐船来找到他，把他带回家里，给他吃喝，让他恢复了力气。万奈莫宁说他想回家。洛希说：我可以送你回去，还把女儿嫁给你，只要你为我锻造一件三宝：用天鹅的羽毛尖、不生育的母牛的奶、一粒大麦和夏天母羊的一缕羊毛把它造出来。万奈莫宁说：我不会锻造三宝，可是我家乡有一位铁匠伊尔马里宁，他锻造过天空的穹顶，他一定能做到。洛希答应了，给了他一架雪橇，叮嘱他回家的路上千万不要抬头看天。北方的波赫约拉在史诗里是一片寒冷而强大的土地，它的女主人洛希会魔法、有心计，后来成了卡勒瓦拉英雄们最难对付的对手。','Väinämöinen drifted on the sea for many days, cold and hungry, with no shore in sight. Then a great eagle came flying from the edge of the sky. Remembering that when Väinämöinen burned the forest he had left a birch for the birds, it said: ''You left the birds a tree to rest in; now I will repay you.'' It took him on its back and carried him to the shore of northern Pohjola, set him down and flew away. Väinämöinen wept on the strange shore, and his weeping reached Louhi, the mistress of Pohjola. The gap-toothed Louhi came in her boat, found him, took him home, fed him and restored his strength. He said he longed to go home. Louhi said: ''I will send you home and give you my daughter too, if you will forge me a Sampo from the tip of a swan''s feather, the milk of a barren cow, a grain of barley and the wool of a summer ewe.'' Väinämöinen answered: ''I cannot forge the Sampo, but in my land there is a smith, Ilmarinen, who forged the vault of the sky; he can.'' Louhi agreed, gave him a sledge, and warned him not to look up at the sky on his way home. Pohjola, the north, is in the epic a cold and mighty land, and its mistress Louhi, skilled in magic and cunning, becomes the hardest adversary of the heroes of Kalevala.','claim.v0350.kal_1.09','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第七章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.10','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',10,'彩虹上的少女与斧伤','The maiden on the rainbow and the axe','万奈莫宁赶着雪橇往南走，忽然听见头顶传来织布机的声音。他忘了洛希的叮嘱，抬头一看，波赫约拉的美丽少女正坐在彩虹上，用金梭银线织着布。万奈莫宁请她坐到自己的雪橇上来，跟他回家。少女说，要她跟他走，他得先做几件事：用一把没有刃的刀劈开一根马鬃，把一个鸡蛋打成看不出的结；剥下石头的皮，从冰上砍下木桩而不溅起一点碎屑。万奈莫宁一件件都做到了。少女又说：你还要用我纺锤的碎片造一艘船，不用手推、不用膝顶，把它推下水去。万奈莫宁在一块蓝色的石头上造船，可是恶灵希西和勒姆波暗中作怪，让他的斧头一滑，砍进了自己的膝盖。鲜血像河水一样涌出来，淹没了草地和山丘。万奈莫宁会许多咒语，却唯独想不起止住铁伤的那一首，因为他不知道铁的起源。','Driving south in his sledge, Väinämöinen heard the sound of a loom above him. Forgetting Louhi''s warning he looked up, and there sat the beautiful maiden of Pohjola on the rainbow, weaving with a golden shuttle and silver thread. He asked her to come down into his sledge and go home with him. She said he must first do certain things: split a horsehair with a knife that had no edge, and tie an egg into knots no one could see; peel the bark from a stone, and cut stakes from the ice without splintering it. He did them all. Then she said: ''Build a boat from the fragments of my spindle and launch it without pushing it with hand or knee.'' Väinämöinen set to building on a blue stone, but the evil spirits Hiisi and Lempo made his axe slip, and it cut into his knee. Blood gushed out like a river, flooding the meadows and the hills. He knew many charms, but not the one to stop a wound of iron, for he did not know the origin of iron.','claim.v0350.kal_1.10','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第八章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.11','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',11,'铁的起源','The origin of iron','万奈莫宁流着血，赶着雪橇去找能治伤的人。他走过一户户人家，最后在一座小屋里找到了一位白胡子老人。老人说：比这更大的伤我也治过。可你得先告诉我铁是怎么来的。万奈莫宁就讲起了铁的起源：最早的时候，三位少女在天上把乳汁洒向大地，黑色的乳汁变成了软铁，白色的乳汁变成了钢，红色的乳汁变成了生铁。铁害怕火，躲进了沼泽和泉水里，藏在狼和熊的脚印下面。铁匠伊尔马里宁找到了它，把它放进熔炉里锻炼，铁哀求他不要伤害它，答应永远不伤害自己的亲人。可伊尔马里宁在给钢淬火时，希西的鸟——一只胡蜂——飞来，把毒蛇的毒液、蚂蚁的酸液和蛤蟆的毒汁偷偷放进了淬火的水里，钢从此变得凶猛，背叛了誓言，连自己的兄弟都要砍伤。老人听完，大声斥责铁的罪恶，念起了止血的咒语，又派儿子去熬制药膏，替万奈莫宁包扎了伤口。伤口愈合了，万奈莫宁感谢乌戈，赶着雪橇回到了家乡。','Bleeding, Väinämöinen drove on in search of a healer. He passed house after house until, in a small hut, he found a white-bearded old man, who said: ''I have stopped greater floods of blood than this. But first tell me the origin of iron.'' So Väinämöinen told it: in the beginning three maidens sprinkled their milk from the sky over the earth; the black milk became soft iron, the white became steel, the red became hard iron. Iron, afraid of fire, hid in the swamps and springs, under the footprints of the wolf and the bear. The smith Ilmarinen found it and put it into his furnace, and the iron begged him not to harm it, promising never to wound its kin. But as Ilmarinen tempered the steel, Hiisi''s bird, a hornet, came and slipped the venom of serpents, the acid of ants and the poison of toads into the tempering water, so the steel became fierce, broke its oath and would cut even its own brothers. When the old man had heard it, he loudly rebuked iron for its wickedness, spoke the charms that stop blood, and sent his son to boil a salve, and bound the wound. It healed; Väinämöinen thanked Ukko and drove home.','claim.v0350.kal_1.11','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第九章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_1_10.12','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_1_10',12,'锻造三宝','The forging of the Sampo','万奈莫宁回到家，劝铁匠伊尔马里宁到北方去锻造三宝、娶波赫约拉的少女。伊尔马里宁说：我不去那个吃人的阴冷地方。万奈莫宁就唱出一棵开着花的冷杉，树梢上挂着月亮，枝间停着大熊星，骗伊尔马里宁爬上去摘月亮；等他爬到树顶，万奈莫宁唱起一阵狂风，把伊尔马里宁连人带树卷走，一直吹到了波赫约拉。洛希热情地款待他，问他能不能锻造三宝。伊尔马里宁找到一块石头砌起熔炉，叫奴隶们拉风箱，把天鹅的羽毛尖、母牛的奶、大麦和羊毛放进火里。第一天，炉中出来一张金弓，可它每天都要一颗人头，他把它折断了；第二天出来一艘红船，它专要去打仗；第三天出来一头金角母牛，脾气太坏；第四天出来一把金犁，专犁别人的田地，都被他扔回了火里。他唤来四方的风，风吹了三天三夜，三宝终于成形了，它有一个五彩的盖子，一边磨出吃的粮食，一边磨出卖的粮食，一边磨出存的粮食，每天各磨一大斗。洛希非常高兴，把三宝锁进波赫约拉的石山里，上了九道锁，三宝的根扎进地下九寻深。可是少女说她还不想离开母亲的家，伊尔马里宁只好垂头丧气地坐上洛希给的船，回到了家乡。','Back home, Väinämöinen urged the smith Ilmarinen to go north, forge the Sampo and win the maiden of Pohjola. Ilmarinen refused to go to that cold land that eats men. So Väinämöinen sang up a flowering fir with the moon in its crown and the Great Bear in its branches, and coaxed Ilmarinen to climb up and fetch the moon; when he reached the top, Väinämöinen sang up a whirlwind that swept Ilmarinen away and carried him to Pohjola. Louhi welcomed him and asked whether he could forge the Sampo. Ilmarinen built a furnace on a rock, set thralls to work the bellows, and put the swan''s feather tip, the milk, the barley and the wool into the fire. On the first day a golden bow came out, but it wanted a head every day, so he broke it; on the second a red boat that wanted only war; on the third a heifer with golden horns, too ill-tempered; on the fourth a golden plough that ploughed other men''s fields — all thrown back into the fire. He called the four winds, which blew for three days and nights, and at last the Sampo took shape, with a many-coloured lid; on one side it ground grain for eating, on another for selling, on a third for storing, a great measure each day. Louhi rejoiced and locked the Sampo in the stone mountain of Pohjola behind nine locks, its roots sunk nine fathoms deep. But the maiden said she would not yet leave her mother''s house, and Ilmarinen went home crestfallen in the boat Louhi gave him.','claim.v0350.kal_1.12','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.1','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',1,'萨里岛的花','The flower of Saari','勒明凯宁又叫阿赫蒂、考科米耶利，是一个英俊的年轻人，脸颊红润，头发漂亮，可是性情放荡不羁，眼睛总是在少女们身上打转。他听说萨里岛上有一位美丽的少女，名叫基利基，被称为“萨里岛之花”，许多求婚者——太阳的儿子、月亮的儿子、星星的儿子，甚至遥远国度的王子——都被她拒绝了。勒明凯宁不顾母亲的劝阻，赶着雪橇来到萨里岛。岛上的少女们嘲笑他，说他是个穷小子，连马都喂不饱。勒明凯宁并不生气，他留在岛上，白天和少女们一起玩耍，晚上和她们一起跳舞，很快就和岛上所有的少女都成了朋友，只有基利基一个人对他不理不睬，从不正眼看他。可是勒明凯宁已经下定了决心：非娶到这朵萨里岛之花不可。他的另一个名字考科米耶利，意思是“心在远方的人”，正好说明了他的性情：他永远不肯安安稳稳地待在家里。母亲曾警告他，萨里岛的人会看不起一个外乡来的穷小子，可他偏偏要去试一试。','Lemminkäinen, also called Ahti and Kaukomieli, was a handsome young man with ruddy cheeks and fine hair, but wild and reckless, his eyes always roving after the maidens. He heard that on the island of Saari lived a beautiful maiden named Kyllikki, called the flower of Saari, who had refused many suitors — the son of the sun, the son of the moon, the son of the stars, even princes from distant lands. Against his mother''s warnings he drove his sledge to Saari. The island maidens mocked him as a poor fellow who could not even feed his horse. He did not take offence; he stayed on the island, played with the maidens by day and danced with them by night, and soon was friends with every maiden there — except Kyllikki, who would not even look at him. But Lemminkäinen had made up his mind: he would have the flower of Saari. His other name, Kaukomieli, means far-minded, and it fits him: he could never stay quietly at home. His mother had warned him that the people of Saari would look down on a poor stranger, but he had to try.','claim.v0350.kal_2.01','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.2','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',2,'雪橇上的新娘','The bride in the sledge','有一天傍晚，少女们在林间空地上跳舞，基利基跳在最前面。勒明凯宁赶着雪橇冲过来，一把抱起基利基，把她扔进雪橇，用鞭子一甩，飞驰而去。基利基又哭又喊，求他放她回家，说：放我回去吧，不然我就喊我的兄弟们来，他们会追上你，把你的头砍下来。勒明凯宁说：我的心是勇敢的，我的剑是锋利的，我不怕你的兄弟。基利基哭着说，他是一个只会打仗的穷汉。勒明凯宁就向她许诺：我发誓，从今以后再也不去打仗，哪怕金银堆成山也不去。你也要发誓，从今以后不再到村子里去跳舞，不管多么想去。基利基答应了。两人在乌戈面前立下誓约，回到勒明凯宁的家。母亲欢喜地迎接了这位美丽的儿媳。这两个誓言把两个性情相反的人拴在一起：一个是好斗的英雄，一个是爱热闹的少女。誓言立下了，危险却也一起埋下了。','One evening the maidens were dancing in a forest glade with Kyllikki leading them. Lemminkäinen drove up in his sledge, seized Kyllikki, threw her into the sledge, cracked his whip and sped away. Kyllikki wept and cried out, begging to be let go home: ''Let me go, or I will call my brothers; they will overtake you and cut off your head.'' He answered: ''My heart is brave and my sword is sharp; I do not fear your brothers.'' She wept that he was a poor man who only knew how to fight. So he promised her: ''I swear never again to go to war, not even for mountains of gold and silver. And you must swear never again to go dancing in the village, however much you long to.'' Kyllikki agreed. They swore their oaths before Ukko and went to Lemminkäinen''s home, where his mother joyfully welcomed her beautiful daughter-in-law. The two vows bound together two people of opposite nature, a quarrelsome hero and a maiden who loved merrymaking; with the vows, the danger too was laid down.','claim.v0350.kal_2.02','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.3','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',3,'破誓','The broken vow','日子一天天过去，勒明凯宁常常出门去打鱼。有一天他出海很晚还没有回来，基利基一个人在家闷得慌，就违背誓言，偷偷跑到村子里去跳舞了。勒明凯宁的妹妹艾尼基看见了，等哥哥一回来，就把这件事告诉了他。勒明凯宁又气又恼，说：基利基破了她的誓言，那我也不必再守我的誓言了。我要去打仗，我要到北方的波赫约拉去，向那里的少女求婚。基利基哭着请求原谅，他却已经铁了心。他对母亲说：快把我的战衣拿来，把我的剑拿来，我要到波赫约拉去，那里有美丽的少女，还有许多巫师等着我去较量。一个誓言的破灭，就这样把这位英雄重新推上了危险的道路。艾尼基告诉哥哥的话很简单：基利基到别人的村子里去了，在那里和人一起跳舞。这一句话，就让勒明凯宁觉得自己也被誓言解脱了。母亲劝他说，誓言是两个人一起立的，破了一半也还剩一半，可他已经听不进去了。','The days went by, and Lemminkäinen often went out fishing. One day he stayed out late on the water, and Kyllikki, weary of being alone at home, broke her vow and slipped off to the village to dance. Lemminkäinen''s sister Ainikki saw her and told her brother as soon as he came home. Angry and hurt, he said: ''Kyllikki has broken her oath, so I need not keep mine. I will go to war; I will go north to Pohjola and woo a maiden there.'' Kyllikki wept and begged forgiveness, but his mind was made up. He said to his mother: ''Bring my war-coat, bring my sword. I am going to Pohjola, where there are beautiful maidens and many sorcerers waiting for me to match my strength against them.'' So the breaking of one vow set the hero back on the road of danger. Ainikki''s words to her brother were simple: Kyllikki has gone to a strange village and danced there. That one sentence was enough for Lemminkäinen to think himself released from his own oath. His mother said that the vow had been made by two and half of it still stood, but he would not hear her.','claim.v0350.kal_2.03','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十二章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.4','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',4,'流血的梳子','The bleeding brush','母亲苦苦劝他：不要去波赫约拉，那里的拉普兰巫师会用歌声把你唱进火里、唱进冰里，你一个人去会丢掉性命的。勒明凯宁说：拉普兰的巫师们早就对我唱过歌了，我一点也不怕。母亲说：你有一个美丽的妻子在家，还要去别处找什么新娘？他不听，梳好了头，把梳子往墙上——一说是往炉边——一扔，说：当这把梳子流出血来的时候，就是我遭到不幸的时候。母亲和妻子哭着看他穿上铁甲，佩上宝剑，套好了马，赶着雪橇向北方出发了。他一路上念着咒语，祈求乌戈和森林的神灵保佑他，给他的剑和马增添力量。那把留在家中的梳子，从此成了母亲日夜守望的信号。在整部史诗里，勒明凯宁的母亲是最坚定、最勇敢的人物之一。儿子离开的那一刻，她就开始守望；后面的故事会证明，她的爱比亡者之河还要深。勒明凯宁却头也不回，他的心思已经全在北方。','His mother begged him: ''Do not go to Pohjola. The Lapland sorcerers will sing you into fire and ice; alone you will lose your life.'' He answered: ''The Lapland sorcerers have sung against me before; I do not fear them.'' ''You have a beautiful wife at home,'' she said. ''Why seek a bride elsewhere?'' He would not listen. He combed his hair and flung the brush against the wall — or by the stove, in some tellings — and said: ''When this brush drips blood, misfortune has befallen me.'' His mother and wife wept as he put on his iron mail, girded his sword, harnessed his horse and drove off north. All the way he chanted charms, praying Ukko and the spirits of the forest to guard him and give strength to his sword and horse. The brush left at home became the sign his mother watched day and night. In the whole epic Lemminkäinen''s mother is one of the most steadfast and courageous figures. From the moment her son left she began to watch; the story will show that her love runs deeper than the river of the dead. Lemminkäinen never looked back; his mind was already in the north.','claim.v0350.kal_2.04','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十二章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.5','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',5,'巫师与盲牧人','The sorcerers and the blind herdsman','勒明凯宁来到波赫约拉，走进洛希的大厅，那里坐满了巫师和歌者，弹琴的弹琴，唱歌的唱歌。他们看见这个陌生人，都不理睬他。勒明凯宁就站在门口唱起歌来，歌声像火一样从他口中喷出，他把那些巫师一个个唱走了：有的唱进了荒野，有的唱进了湖里，有的唱进了激流，有的唱到了冰封的远方。最后，大厅里只剩下一个又老又瞎、戴着湿帽子的牧人，叫马尔卡哈图。勒明凯宁没有对他唱歌，反而嘲笑他说：你这个可怜的老头，我才不屑对你唱，你又瞎又丑，连自己的姐妹都敢欺负，连牛都看不好。老牧人受了这番羞辱，心里怀恨，悄悄走出大厅，来到图奥内拉的黑河边，等着勒明凯宁总有一天会从这里经过。马尔卡哈图这个名字，意思是“湿帽子”。勒明凯宁唱走了所有强大的对手，却偏偏轻视了这个最不起眼的老人，这一次轻视，后来要了他的命。','Arriving in Pohjola, Lemminkäinen went into Louhi''s hall, which was full of sorcerers and singers playing and singing; they ignored the stranger. He stood in the doorway and began to sing, and song poured from his mouth like fire. He sang the sorcerers away one by one: some into the wilderness, some into the lakes, some into the rapids, some to icebound distant places. At last only one was left in the hall, an old, blind herdsman in a wet hat, named Märkähattu. Lemminkäinen did not sing against him but mocked him: ''You wretched old man, I would not stoop to sing against you; you are blind and ugly, you have wronged your own kin and cannot even tend the cattle.'' Shamed and full of hatred, the old herdsman slipped out of the hall, went to the black river of Tuonela, and waited there for the day Lemminkäinen would pass that way. The name Märkähattu means Wet-hat. Lemminkäinen sang away all his mighty rivals but slighted the humblest old man of all, and that slight would cost him his life.','claim.v0350.kal_2.05','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十二章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.6','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',6,'希西的麋鹿','The elk of Hiisi','勒明凯宁向洛希求娶她的女儿。洛希说：你已经有妻子了，可要是你能滑雪捉住希西的麋鹿，我就把女儿给你。勒明凯宁找到一个造雪橇板的巧匠，请他做了一副好滑雪板，一边做一边夸口：森林里没有一只野兽能逃过我的滑雪板。恶灵希西听见了，就用朽木、柳枝、草根和沼泽里的东西造出一头麋鹿，让它奔向拉普兰。勒明凯宁滑着雪追了上去，滑雪板在雪地上嘶嘶作响，像火一样飞快。那头麋鹿一路狂奔，冲进拉普兰人的家里，踢翻了锅子，弄得煮好的肉都掉进了灰里，拉普兰的妇女和孩子又哭又笑。勒明凯宁用力过猛，一脚踩下去，滑雪板啪的一声折断了，滑雪杖也断了，麋鹿逃得无影无踪。他站在雪地里，心里懊恼极了。为他做滑雪板的巧匠，原诗里叫考皮，又叫吕利基。希西是史诗里的恶灵，常常和另一个恶灵勒姆波一起，给英雄们制造麻烦。','Lemminkäinen asked Louhi for her daughter. Louhi said: ''You have a wife already, but if you can catch the elk of Hiisi on skis, my daughter shall be yours.'' He went to a skilled maker of skis and had a fine pair made, boasting as they were made that no beast in the forest could escape his skis. The evil spirit Hiisi heard, and made an elk out of rotten wood, willow twigs, roots and things of the swamp, and set it running towards Lapland. Lemminkäinen sped after it on his skis, which hissed over the snow as swift as fire. The elk ran wild, burst into the Lapps'' huts and kicked over their kettles so that the meat fell into the ashes, and the Lapp women and children wept and laughed. Lemminkäinen thrust down so hard that his ski snapped, and his ski-pole too, and the elk vanished. He stood in the snow, bitterly vexed. The ski-maker is called in the poem Kauppi, also Lyylikki. Hiisi is an evil spirit of the epic, often paired with another, Lempo, in making trouble for the heroes.','claim.v0350.kal_2.06','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十三章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.7','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',7,'森林之神','The gods of the forest','勒明凯宁修好了滑雪板，这一次他不再夸口，而是向森林之神祈祷。他向森林之王塔皮奥和森林女主人米耶利基唱起祈祷的歌：森林的主人啊，美丽的森林之母啊，请打开你的金库，把那头麋鹿赶到我的路上来，我会用金子和银子报答你。他唱森林的美丽，唱森林之母穿着金色的衣裙，唱树木像盛装的人一样站立。他一连祈祷了三天，森林之神被他的歌声感动了，把那头麋鹿赶到了他面前。勒明凯宁终于捉住了希西的麋鹿，把它带回波赫约拉交给洛希，说：我已经做到了，把女儿给我吧。可是洛希又提出了第二个难题。在原诗里，这些祈祷占了很长的篇幅，保留了古代芬兰猎人向森林神灵祈求猎物的祷词；塔皮奥的国度叫塔皮奥拉，是森林中一个富足的王国。这一次，勒明凯宁靠的不是夸口，而是谦卑的祈求。','Lemminkäinen mended his skis, and this time, instead of boasting, he prayed to the forest gods. He sang to Tapio, king of the forest, and Mielikki, its mistress: ''Master of the forest, fair mother of the woods, open your treasure-house and drive that elk into my path, and I will repay you with gold and silver.'' He sang of the beauty of the forest, of its mother in golden garments, of the trees standing like people in festive dress. For three days he prayed, and the forest gods, moved by his song, drove the elk to him. At last he caught the elk of Hiisi, brought it to Pohjola and gave it to Louhi: ''I have done it; give me your daughter.'' But Louhi set him a second task. In the poem these prayers run long, preserving the words with which old Finnish hunters asked the forest spirits for game; Tapio''s realm, Tapiola, is a rich kingdom in the forest. This time Lemminkäinen succeeded not by boasting but by humble prayer.','claim.v0350.kal_2.07','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十四章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.8','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',8,'火焰之马','The fire-breathing horse','洛希说：你去把希西那匹棕色的骟马套上笼头，它在希西的原野上奔跑，鼻孔里喷着火，嘴里冒着烟。勒明凯宁带着金笼头和银马勒出发了。那匹马奔过来，鼻孔里喷出的火焰让人无法靠近。勒明凯宁向乌戈祈求：至高的乌戈啊，请降下冰雹和雪雨，落在这匹烈马的身上，浇灭它的火焰。乌戈降下了铁一样的冰雹，那匹马被冰雹浇灭了身上的火，勒明凯宁趁机走上前去，用温和的话哄它：好马啊，不要伤害我，低下你的头来，套上这金色的笼头吧，我不会用鞭子抽你。马低下了头，他给它套上了笼头，骑着它回到了波赫约拉。可洛希还是不肯把女儿给他，又说出了第三个难题，这一次的地方，比前两次都要可怕得多。这一次，他又一次学会了不靠蛮力，而是靠祈求和温和的话语。','Louhi said: ''Go and bridle the brown gelding of Hiisi, which runs on Hiisi''s meadows breathing fire from its nostrils and smoke from its mouth.'' Lemminkäinen set out with a golden halter and a silver bridle. The horse came galloping, and the flames from its nostrils let no one near. Lemminkäinen prayed: ''Highest Ukko, send down hail and sleet on this wild horse and quench its fire.'' Ukko sent down hail like iron, the fire on the horse was quenched, and Lemminkäinen stepped up and coaxed it gently: ''Good horse, do not harm me; bow your head into this golden halter. I will not strike you with the whip.'' The horse bowed its head; he bridled it and rode back to Pohjola. Still Louhi would not give him her daughter, and named a third task, in a place far more terrible than the first two. Once again he succeeded not by force but by prayer and gentle words.','claim.v0350.kal_2.08','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十四章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.9','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',9,'图奥内拉的天鹅','The swan of Tuonela','洛希说：你去图奥内拉的黑河上，只射一箭，射死那里的天鹅，我就把女儿给你。图奥内拉是亡者之地，那条黑河就在亡者之王图奥尼的国度里。勒明凯宁背着弓箭，来到黑河边的瀑布旁，等着天鹅游过来。可是那个被他嘲笑过的盲眼牧人马尔卡哈图早就埋伏在河边了。他看见勒明凯宁走来，就从河水里抓起一条水蛇，像投矛一样投过去，水蛇穿过了勒明凯宁的左腋，刺穿了他的心脏和右肩。勒明凯宁倒在地上，痛苦地说：我真是疏忽啊，我没有问过母亲，没有学会对付水蛇的咒语。我知道三条咒语，却唯独不知道这一条。如果母亲在身边，她一定会告诉我该怎么办。这只在亡者之河上游动的天鹅，后来成了芬兰艺术中有名的形象，作曲家西贝柳斯的交响诗《图奥内拉的天鹅》就取材于此。','Louhi said: ''Go to the black river of Tuonela, and with a single arrow shoot the swan there; then my daughter shall be yours.'' Tuonela was the land of the dead, and the black river lay in the realm of Tuoni, lord of the dead. Lemminkäinen took his bow and arrows and went to the falls of the black river to wait for the swan. But the blind herdsman Märkähattu, whom he had mocked, lay in wait by the river. Seeing Lemminkäinen come, he drew a water-serpent from the river and hurled it like a spear; it passed under Lemminkäinen''s left arm and through his heart and out at his right shoulder. Lemminkäinen fell and said in his agony: ''I was careless: I never asked my mother, never learned the charm against the water-serpent. I knew three charms but not this one. If my mother were here, she would tell me what to do.'' The swan swimming on the river of the dead became a famous image in Finnish art; Sibelius''s tone poem The Swan of Tuonela takes its subject from here.','claim.v0350.kal_2.09','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十四章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.10','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',10,'黑河里的碎片','The pieces in the black river','盲眼牧人把奄奄一息的勒明凯宁扔进了图奥内拉的黑河里，河水把他卷向汹涌的瀑布。亡者之王图奥尼的儿子——一个满身血污的人——站在河里，拿着刀把勒明凯宁砍成了好几块，扔进了图奥内拉深深的河水里，说：你就在这里躺着吧，带着你的弓和箭，去射河上的天鹅吧。一位英雄就这样消失在亡者之河的深处，没有坟墓，也没有人知道他的下落。远方的家中，母亲和妻子还在等着他回来。这一章短促而阴暗，诗人只用几句话就交代了勒明凯宁的死，仿佛连诗歌本身也不愿在这黑河边多停留一刻。可是在《卡勒瓦拉》里，死亡并不总是故事的终点。原诗把图奥尼的儿子称为“血红的图奥尼之子”。那只天鹅仍在黑河上静静地游着，没有人射中它。','The blind herdsman threw the dying Lemminkäinen into the black river of Tuonela, and the current swept him towards the roaring falls. The son of Tuoni, lord of the dead — a man stained with blood — stood in the river with a knife, hewed Lemminkäinen into pieces and threw them into the deep waters of Tuonela, saying: ''Lie there with your bow and arrows, and shoot the swan on the river.'' So the hero vanished into the depths of the river of the dead, with no grave, and no one knowing where he lay. Far away at home his mother and wife still waited for him. The rune is brief and dark; the singer tells Lemminkäinen''s death in a few lines, as if the song itself would not linger by that black river. But in the Kalevala death is not always the end of the story. The poem calls him the bloody son of Tuoni. The swan still swam quietly on the black river; no one had shot it.','claim.v0350.kal_2.10','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十四章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.11','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',11,'母亲的寻找','The mother''s search','在勒明凯宁的家里，母亲每天都看着那把梳子。有一天，梳子上忽然渗出了血。母亲大哭起来，立刻提起裙子，一路奔向北方。她来到波赫约拉，逼问洛希：我的儿子在哪里？洛希先说给了他一匹马，又说让他去打猎了。母亲说：你在撒谎！快说实话，不然我就毁掉你的仓库，打碎你的三宝。洛希这才说出，她派勒明凯宁去射图奥内拉的天鹅。母亲到处寻找，她问树木，树木说：我们自己的苦已经够多了，没有看见你的儿子；她问道路，道路说：我被人踩、被车轧，没有看见；她问月亮，月亮也说不知道。最后她问太阳，太阳说：你的儿子在图奥内拉的黑河里，他被砍成了碎块，沉在河底。太阳高高在天上，什么都看得见，所以只有它知道真相。母亲听了，不再哭泣，而是转身去找能帮她把儿子捞上来的人。','At home, Lemminkäinen''s mother watched the brush every day. One day blood suddenly oozed from it. Weeping, she gathered up her skirts and ran all the way north. In Pohjola she demanded of Louhi: ''Where is my son?'' Louhi said first that she had given him a horse, then that she had sent him hunting. ''You lie!'' said the mother. ''Tell the truth, or I will destroy your storehouses and smash your Sampo.'' Then Louhi admitted she had sent Lemminkäinen to shoot the swan of Tuonela. The mother searched everywhere. She asked the trees, and the trees said: ''We have troubles enough of our own; we have not seen your son.'' She asked the road, and the road said: ''I am trodden and crushed by wheels; I have not seen him.'' She asked the moon, and the moon did not know. At last she asked the sun, and the sun said: ''Your son is in the black river of Tuonela, hewn to pieces, lying at the bottom.'' The sun, high in the sky, sees everything, and so it alone knew the truth. Hearing it, the mother stopped weeping and turned to find someone who could help her bring her son up from the river.','claim.v0350.kal_2.11','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十五章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_11_15.12','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_11_15',12,'铜耙与蜜蜂','The copper rake and the bee','母亲哭着去找铁匠伊尔马里宁，请他打一把耙子：铜的耙齿要一百寻长，耙柄要五百寻长。伊尔马里宁为她打好了铜耙。母亲来到图奥内拉的黑河边，请太阳让亡者之地的人们都睡去，然后用铜耙在河里一遍遍地耙，耙出了儿子的衬衫，耙出了他的靴子和帽子，最后在河底耙出了儿子的身体，可还少了一只手、半个头和许多块骨头。她又耙了一遍又一遍，终于把所有的碎块都找齐了。她把碎块拼在一起，请掌管血脉的女神苏奥内塔尔用纺锤把断开的血管一根根接起来，把骨头接上骨头，把肉接上肉。可儿子还是不能说话。母亲就派一只小蜜蜂去取蜜膏，蜜蜂先在草地上采来蜜，没有用；又飞过海去取，还是不够；最后飞过了九重天，从造物主的屋子里取来了神的蜜膏。母亲把蜜膏涂在儿子身上，勒明凯宁醒了过来，说：我睡得多么久啊。母亲问他为什么还想去波赫约拉，他说那个少女还在等他。母亲劝他放弃，带着他回到了家乡。','Weeping, the mother went to the smith Ilmarinen and asked for a rake with copper teeth a hundred fathoms long and a handle five hundred fathoms long, and Ilmarinen forged it. At the black river of Tuonela she asked the sun to put the people of the land of the dead to sleep, then raked the river again and again. She raked up her son''s shirt, his boots and his hat, and at last his body from the bottom — but a hand was missing, half the head and many bones. She raked again and again until she had found every piece. She laid the pieces together and called on Suonetar, goddess of the veins, to join the severed veins with her spindle, bone to bone and flesh to flesh. Still her son could not speak. So she sent a little bee for honey. The honey it gathered from the meadows did not help; what it brought from over the sea was not enough; at last it flew beyond the nine heavens and brought the Creator''s own honey from his house. The mother anointed her son, and Lemminkäinen woke and said: ''How long I have slept!'' When she asked why he still wanted to go to Pohjola, he said the maiden was waiting for him; she persuaded him to give it up and took him home.','claim.v0350.kal_2.12','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第十五章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.1','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',1,'两兄弟的仇恨','The brothers'' feud','从前，有一位母亲养大了一窝小鸡，一只被带到了俄罗斯，一只被带到了卡累利阿，还有一只留在家里——诗人用这样的比喻开始讲述两兄弟的故事。哥哥温塔莫和弟弟卡勒沃住得很近，却总是合不来。他们为了渔场、为了田地和羊群，为了一连串鸡毛蒜皮的小事争吵不休，怨恨越积越深。终于，温塔莫召集了一群武士，带着刀剑去攻打卡勒沃。卡勒沃的族人全被杀光，房屋被烧成灰烬，只剩下一个怀了孕的女子活了下来，被温塔莫带回家去，做了打扫屋子、劈柴担水的奴隶。在温塔莫看来，卡勒沃一族已经从世上消失了。可是那个女子腹中的孩子，正是后来让温塔莫一族灭亡的人。在隆洛特编排的《卡勒瓦拉》里，库勒沃的这几章最为阴暗，它们主要取材于卡累利阿和英格里亚一带的民间唱本。二十世纪的作家托尔金也深受这个故事的触动，写下了《库勒沃的故事》。','Once, the poem begins, a mother raised a brood of chicks: one was carried to Russia, one to Karelia, and one stayed at home — with such an image the singer opens the tale of two brothers. The elder, Untamo, and the younger, Kalervo, lived near each other but could never agree. They quarrelled over fishing-grounds, over fields and flocks, over a run of petty matters, and their grudge grew deeper and deeper. At last Untamo gathered a band of warriors and attacked Kalervo with swords. Kalervo''s people were all slain and his houses burned to ashes; only one woman, who was with child, survived, and Untamo took her home as a slave to sweep the house, split wood and carry water. To Untamo, Kalervo''s line had vanished from the earth. But the child in that woman''s womb would one day destroy Untamo''s own clan. In Lönnrot''s Kalevala these runes are the darkest; they draw chiefly on songs from Karelia and Ingria. In the twentieth century J. R. R. Tolkien was moved by the tale to write his own Story of Kullervo.','claim.v0350.kal_3.01','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.2','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',2,'摇篮里的复仇者','The avenger in the cradle','那个女子在温塔莫家里生下了一个男孩，母亲给他取名叫库勒沃，温塔莫的人则叫他“战争之子”。孩子还在摇篮里的时候，就显出了惊人的力气。到了第三个月，他一脚踢开了包着自己的襁褓，把摇篮的底都蹬破了，大家都说：这孩子将来一定会成为一个强壮的人。可是库勒沃一天天长大，心里只有一个念头。有一次，温塔莫听见这个摇篮里的孩子说：等我长大了，有了力气，我要为父亲报仇，为母亲流过的眼泪报仇。温塔莫听了，心里一惊，对家人说：这孩子留着，将来就是我们一族的祸害。他决定趁孩子还小，把他除掉。这个出生在仇人家里的孩子，从一开始就被仇恨包围着。在芬兰语的原诗里，他常被连名带姓地称为“卡勒沃之子库勒沃”，仿佛他的名字本身就背负着父亲一族的命运。这样的话出自一个还在吃奶的孩子之口，温塔莫一家都不寒而栗。','In Untamo''s house the woman bore a son. His mother named him Kullervo, and Untamo''s people called him the son of war. While still in the cradle he showed astonishing strength: in his third month he kicked off his swaddling clothes and broke the bottom of the cradle, and everyone said he would grow to be a mighty man. But as Kullervo grew, one thought filled his heart. Once Untamo heard the child in the cradle say: ''When I am grown and strong, I will avenge my father and the tears my mother has shed.'' Untamo was startled and said to his household: ''If this child lives, he will be the ruin of our clan.'' He resolved to be rid of him while he was still small. Born in his enemy''s house, the child was surrounded by hatred from the start. In the Finnish poem he is often called by his full name, Kullervo son of Kalervo, as though his very name carried the fate of his father''s people. Such words from a child still at the breast chilled Untamo''s whole household.','claim.v0350.kal_3.02','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.3','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',3,'杀不死的孩子','The child who could not be killed','温塔莫先叫人做了一只木桶，把库勒沃装进去，扔进了湖里。过了三个晚上，人们去看，只见木桶浮在水面上，库勒沃坐在桶上，手里拿着一根铜钓竿，正在钓鱼。温塔莫又叫人砍来一大堆桦木和松木，堆成一座高高的柴堆，把库勒沃扔进火里烧。过了三天，人们去看，只见柴堆已经烧成了灰，库勒沃坐在灰烬里，拿着一根拨火棍，正在拨弄余火，头发一根也没有烧焦。温塔莫又叫人把孩子吊在一棵橡树上。过了三天三夜，人们去看，库勒沃没有死，他坐在树枝上，正在用小刀在树干上刻画，刻的全是手拿刀剑的武士。温塔莫无可奈何，只好说：这孩子杀不死，那就让他做奴隶，替我们干活吧。水、火和绳索都没能杀死他，像是在说这个孩子身上有一种无法消灭的力量；可这股力量后来带给他和身边的人的，却只有灾难。','First Untamo had a barrel made, shut Kullervo inside and threw him into the lake. After three nights his men went to look: the barrel was floating on the water, and Kullervo sat on top of it with a copper rod in his hand, fishing. Next Untamo had a great pile of birch and pine logs heaped up and threw Kullervo into the fire. After three days they went to look: the pile had burned to ashes, and Kullervo sat in the ashes with a poker, stirring the embers, not a hair singed. Then Untamo had the child hanged from an oak. After three days and nights they went to look: Kullervo was not dead but sat on a branch carving pictures on the trunk with a little knife — all of warriors with swords in their hands. Helpless, Untamo said: ''If this child cannot be killed, let him be a slave and work for us.'' Water, fire and rope could not kill him, as if the child held a strength nothing could destroy; yet that strength would bring only disaster to him and to those around him.','claim.v0350.kal_3.03','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.4','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',4,'无用的仆人','The useless servant','温塔莫给库勒沃派活。先让他照看一个婴儿，他却把婴儿弄伤、弄死了，还把摇篮烧掉；诗中这一段说得很直接，本篇只交代结果。温塔莫又让他去砍林开荒，他把整片森林都砍倒了，然后诅咒那块地永远不长庄稼、不长树木。温塔莫让他筑篱笆，他把整棵整棵的松树和冷杉竖起来，筑起一道高到天上、没有缝隙也没有门的围墙，谁也进不去出不来。温塔莫让他打黑麦，他把黑麦打成了粉末和糠皮。温塔莫说：这个孩子什么活都干不好，留着他只会带来灾祸。他就把库勒沃卖到了卡累利阿，卖给了铁匠伊尔马里宁，作价不过是几口旧锅和一些破旧的农具。库勒沃就这样从仇人的奴隶，变成了陌生人家的奴隶。库勒沃并不是懒惰，他力大无穷，只是心里装满了怨恨，每一件活计到了他手里，都变成了破坏。伊尔马里宁并不知道，自己买回家的是怎样一个人。','Untamo set Kullervo to work. First he was told to mind a baby; he hurt it and it died, and he burned the cradle — the poem tells it bluntly, and this retelling gives only the outcome. Next he was sent to clear the forest; he felled the whole forest and cursed the land so that neither grain nor tree would ever grow there. Told to build a fence, he set up whole pines and firs into a wall reaching the sky, with no gap and no gate, so that no one could get in or out. Told to thresh the rye, he beat it to dust and chaff. Untamo said: ''This boy can do nothing right; keeping him will only bring misfortune.'' So he sold Kullervo into Karelia, to the smith Ilmarinen, for no more than a few worn-out kettles and some battered farm tools. So Kullervo passed from slave in his enemy''s house to slave in a stranger''s. Kullervo was not idle; he was enormously strong, but so full of resentment that every task in his hands turned to destruction. Ilmarinen did not know what kind of man he had brought home.','claim.v0350.kal_3.04','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.5','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',5,'藏着石头的面包','The bread with a stone in it','伊尔马里宁的妻子，就是当年那位波赫约拉的少女，如今是铁匠家的女主人。她看着这个新来的奴隶，心里想：让他去放牛吧。她要给库勒沃准备路上吃的干粮，就烤了一块面包：上面是燕麦，下面是小麦，中间却藏了一块石头。她把面包交给库勒沃，叮嘱他：等牛群进了树林，你再吃这块面包。然后，她向乌戈、森林的主人和森林之母念了很长的祈祷，请他们保佑牛群平安，不受狼和熊的伤害，又念起对熊的咒语，请熊不要伤害她的牛。库勒沃赶着牛群出了门，在沼泽和林间放牧，一路上抱怨自己的命运：别人吃的是白面包，喝的是麦酒，而他这个奴隶，只能吃干硬的面包，在荒野里放牛。原诗里，她的祈祷占了这一章的大半篇幅，保存了古代芬兰牧人祈求牲畜平安、驱赶猛兽的咒语。读者也就更明白，后面她的牛群为什么会在狼和熊的手里遭殃。','Ilmarinen''s wife — the maiden of Pohjola of old, now mistress of the smith''s house — looked at the new slave and thought: let him herd the cattle. To provide for him she baked a loaf: oats on top, wheat below, and in the middle a stone. She gave it to Kullervo and told him not to eat it until the herd had gone into the woods. Then she spoke long prayers to Ukko and to the master and mother of the forest, asking them to keep the herd safe from wolf and bear, and chanted the charms to the bear, begging it to spare her cattle. Kullervo drove the herd out and pastured them through swamp and wood, lamenting his lot as he went: others ate white bread and drank ale, while he, a slave, had only hard bread and the cattle in the wilderness. In the poem her prayers fill most of the rune, preserving the charms with which old Finnish herders asked safety for their cattle and kept off wild beasts — which makes plain why her herd later comes to grief by wolf and bear.','claim.v0350.kal_3.05','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十二章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.6','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',6,'断刀','The broken knife','到了中午，库勒沃坐在山坡上，拿出主妇给的面包，用刀一切，刀刃碰到了中间的石头，啪的一声折断了。这把刀是他父亲留下的唯一遗物，是他与被杀光的卡勒沃一族之间仅剩的联系。库勒沃看着断刀，哭了起来，说：这是我唯一的珍宝，是我父亲的刀，如今竟断在一个女主人的石头上。一只乌鸦从树上对他说：你这卡勒沃的孩子，为什么这样悲伤？把牛群赶进沼泽吧，让狼和熊把它们都吃掉，再把狼和熊召来，让它们变成牛的样子，傍晚赶回家去。库勒沃照着乌鸦的话做了：他把牛群赶进沼泽，让狼和熊吃掉了它们，又用咒语把狼变成了牛，把熊变成了母牛，赶着这群野兽，一路吹着号角，向主人家走去。这一刀断下去，断的不只是一把刀，也是库勒沃心中最后的一点忍耐。他把断刀收好，心里只想着怎样报复那个女主人。','At noon Kullervo sat down on a hillside, took out the mistress''s loaf and cut into it; the blade struck the stone in the middle and snapped. The knife was the only keepsake left by his father, his last link with the slaughtered people of Kalervo. Kullervo looked at the broken knife and wept: ''This was my only treasure, my father''s knife, and it has broken on a mistress''s stone.'' A crow called to him from a tree: ''Child of Kalervo, why so sad? Drive the herd into the swamp and let the wolves and bears devour them; then call the wolves and bears, give them the shape of cattle, and drive them home at evening.'' Kullervo did as the crow said: he drove the herd into the swamp to be devoured, then by his charms turned the wolves into oxen and the bears into cows, and drove the beasts towards his master''s house, blowing his horn. With the blade broke the last of Kullervo''s patience. He put the broken knife away, thinking only of how to repay the mistress.','claim.v0350.kal_3.06','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十三章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.7','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',7,'主妇之死','The death of the mistress','傍晚，库勒沃把那群“牛”赶进了牛栏，号角声响彻四方。伊尔马里宁的妻子高兴地说：感谢乌戈，牛群平安回来了。她提着奶桶去挤奶，俯下身去，那头“母牛”却原来是一只熊，扑上来把她撕咬。她惊叫着向库勒沃求救：好心的牧人啊，救救我，拿开这些野兽吧，我会给你最好的衣服，最好的面包。库勒沃说：你给我吃石头做的面包，现在你自己尝尝苦头吧。她又向乌戈祈求，请他用箭射死库勒沃。可是没有人来救她，伊尔马里宁的妻子就这样死在了牛栏里。这位曾经在彩虹上织布、让万奈莫宁和伊尔马里宁都为之倾倒的少女，就因为一块藏在面包里的石头，丢掉了性命。库勒沃吹起号角，离开了铁匠的家，逃进了森林。伊尔马里宁后来失去了妻子，悲伤不已。他为她哭了许多日夜；后面的诗章会讲到，他甚至想用金子和银子为自己打造一个新的妻子。','At evening Kullervo drove his ''cattle'' into the byre, the horn sounding far and wide. Ilmarinen''s wife said joyfully: ''Thanks be to Ukko, the herd has come home safe.'' She took her milking pail and bent to milk, but the ''cow'' was a bear, which sprang on her and tore her. She shrieked to Kullervo for help: ''Good herdsman, save me, drive off these beasts, and I will give you the best clothes and the best bread.'' Kullervo answered: ''You fed me bread made of stone; now taste your own bitterness.'' She prayed to Ukko to shoot Kullervo with his arrows. But no one came to save her, and Ilmarinen''s wife died in the byre. The maiden who once wove on the rainbow, for whom Väinämöinen and Ilmarinen had both longed, lost her life over a stone hidden in a loaf. Kullervo blew his horn, left the smith''s house and fled into the forest. Ilmarinen was left to grieve for his wife. He wept for her many nights; a later rune tells how he even tried to forge himself a new wife of gold and silver.','claim.v0350.kal_3.07','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十三章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.8','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',8,'找到父母','Finding his parents','库勒沃在森林里游荡，心中充满了悲伤和愤怒。他想：我该到哪里去？我是谁的孩子？我从来没有一个家。他决定去找温塔莫报仇。在林中，他遇见一个穿着蓝衣的老妇人，她对他说：卡勒沃的孩子，你的父亲并没有死，他还活着，你的母亲也活着，他们就住在拉普兰的边上，靠近一片湖。库勒沃半信半疑地出发了，走了很久，终于在湖边找到了一座房子，见到了自己的父亲和母亲。母亲哭着说：我的儿子，我以为你早就死了，还为你流了许多眼泪。我还以为你是被卖到远方去了。他们告诉他，他还有一个姐姐，可是很多年前，她到林子里去采浆果，就再也没有回来，谁也不知道她去了哪里。这是库勒沃一生中少有的温暖时刻：他终于有了一个家，有了父亲和母亲。可那个失踪的姐姐，却像一道阴影，留在了故事里。','Kullervo wandered in the forest, full of grief and anger. ''Where shall I go? Whose child am I? I have never had a home.'' He resolved to go and take vengeance on Untamo. In the woods he met an old woman in a blue robe, who said: ''Child of Kalervo, your father is not dead. He lives, and your mother lives too; they dwell on the borders of Lapland, near a lake.'' Half believing, Kullervo set out, and after long wandering found a house by a lake and saw his father and mother. His mother wept: ''My son, I thought you long dead and shed many tears for you; I thought you had been sold far away.'' They told him that he had an elder sister, but that years ago she had gone into the woods to pick berries and never come back, and no one knew where she had gone. It is one of the few warm moments of Kullervo''s life: at last he had a home, a father and a mother. But the lost sister lingers in the story like a shadow.','claim.v0350.kal_3.08','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十四章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.9','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',9,'路上的少女','The maiden on the road','回到家里，库勒沃想帮父母干活，却什么也干不好：父亲让他划船，他把船桨划断了，把船底都划穿了；让他撒网捕鱼，他把水搅成了泥浆，把渔网撕成了碎片。父亲只好派他去交税。库勒沃交完了税，赶着雪橇回家，在路上遇见一个少女，请她坐上自己的雪橇，她不肯；又遇见一个少女，也不肯。第三次，他遇见一个在荒野里赶路的少女，就用银子和漂亮的布料引诱她，把她拉上了雪橇。两人在雪橇上过了一夜。第二天早晨，少女问他是哪一家的人。库勒沃说：我是卡勒沃的儿子。少女听了，脸色大变，说：我也是卡勒沃的女儿，就是多年前在林子里采浆果时迷了路的那个女儿。他们谁也不知道对方是谁，两个失散多年的孩子，就在这样的误会里相遇了。这一夜，成了两个人一生中最大的不幸。','Back home, Kullervo tried to help his parents but could do nothing right. Set to row, he broke the oars and drove the boat''s bottom through; set to fish, he churned the water to mud and tore the net to shreds. So his father sent him to pay the taxes. Driving home in his sledge after paying them, he met a maiden on the road and asked her into his sledge; she refused. He met another, who also refused. The third time he met a maiden travelling across the wilds, and with silver and fine cloth he lured her and drew her into the sledge. They spent the night together. In the morning the maiden asked him what family he came from. Kullervo said: ''I am the son of Kalervo.'' At this her face changed, and she said: ''I too am Kalervo''s daughter — the daughter who lost her way picking berries in the woods long ago.'' Neither knew who the other was; two children lost for years met in this terrible ignorance. That night became the greatest misfortune of both their lives.','claim.v0350.kal_3.09','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十五章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.10','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',10,'急流','The rapids','少女明白了一切，悲痛欲绝。她从雪橇上跳下来，奔向河边那奔腾的急流，说：我宁愿让急流把我卷走，也不愿再活在这世上。她投身于激流之中，被汹涌的河水吞没了。库勒沃站在河边，悔恨交加，他说：我做了什么样的事啊！我毁了我的妹妹，我毁了我自己，我这一生只给别人带来不幸。他一度想就此结束自己的生命。他回到家里，把这件事告诉了母亲。母亲劝他说：不要死，我的儿子，你可以远远地走开，到一个没有人认识你的地方去，等时间慢慢平息你的悲伤。可是库勒沃说：在我死之前，还有一件事要做。我要去找温塔莫，为我父亲的族人报仇。诗中说她在投水之前留下了悲伤的话，哀叹自己生来就不幸。库勒沃的一生，从仇人家的摇篮开始，到这里，已经只剩下了复仇和悔恨。','The maiden understood everything and was overcome with grief. She leapt from the sledge and ran to the roaring rapids of the river, crying that she would rather be swept away by the torrent than live on in the world. She threw herself into the rapids and was swallowed by the raging waters. Kullervo stood by the river, torn with remorse: ''What have I done! I have ruined my sister and ruined myself; all my life I have brought only misfortune on others.'' For a while he thought of ending his own life. He went home and told his mother. She pleaded: ''Do not die, my son. Go far away, to a place where no one knows you, and let time ease your grief.'' But Kullervo said: ''Before I die there is one thing left to do. I will go to Untamo and avenge my father''s people.'' The poem gives her sorrowful words before she leaps, lamenting that she was born to misfortune. Kullervo''s life, begun in an enemy''s cradle, now held nothing but vengeance and remorse.','claim.v0350.kal_3.10','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十五章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.11','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',11,'出征与复仇','War and vengeance','库勒沃准备出征，他一个个地问家人：我死了，你们会为我哭泣吗？父亲说：不会，我会再生一个比你更好的儿子。哥哥说：不会，我会找一个比你更好的兄弟。姐妹说：不会，我会找一个比你更好的兄弟。只有母亲说：我会为你哭泣，我会在屋里哭，在田里哭，在每一个地方哭，哭到我的眼泪流干。库勒沃上了路。打仗的途中，一个个使者跑来告诉他：你的父亲死了，你的哥哥死了，你的姐妹死了。他都不肯回去，只说：让他们安葬吧。最后使者说：你的母亲死了。他才流下了眼泪。他向乌戈祈求一把好剑，乌戈给了他。库勒沃来到温塔莫的家，把温塔莫的族人全部杀死，烧毁了他们的房屋，只留下一片灰烬和石头。父亲的仇，终于报了。全家人里，只有母亲的爱从未改变。可是对库勒沃来说，复仇并没有带来安慰，回家时迎接他的只有冷清。','As he made ready for war, Kullervo asked each of his family: ''When I die, will you weep for me?'' His father said: ''No; I will beget a better son.'' His brother said: ''No; I will find a better brother.'' His sister said: ''No; I will find a better brother.'' Only his mother said: ''I will weep for you, in the house and in the fields and everywhere, until my tears run dry.'' Kullervo set out. On the way messengers came one after another: your father is dead, your brother is dead, your sister is dead. He would not turn back, saying only: let them be buried. At last a messenger said: your mother is dead. Then he wept. He prayed to Ukko for a good sword, and Ukko gave it. Coming to Untamo''s house, Kullervo slew all of Untamo''s people and burned their dwellings, leaving only ashes and stones. His father''s death was avenged at last. Of all his family, only his mother''s love had never changed. Yet vengeance brought Kullervo no comfort; only emptiness awaited him at home.','claim.v0350.kal_3.11','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十六章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_31_36.12','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_31_36',12,'卡勒沃之子的结局','The end of Kalervo''s son','库勒沃回到家里，家中空无一人，炉灶冰冷，父亲、母亲、兄弟和姐妹都已经死去。他在母亲的坟前哭泣，母亲在坟墓里对他说：带上我们的黑狗穆斯蒂，到森林里去吧，向森林的神灵求一个安身的地方。库勒沃带着狗走进森林，不知不觉来到了当年他诱骗妹妹的那片荒野。那里的草不再生长，树叶凋零，仿佛大地也在为那件事哀悼。库勒沃拔出剑来，问它：我的剑啊，你愿意饮一个有罪之人的血吗？剑回答说：我连无辜者的血都饮过，为什么不能饮你这有罪之人的血？库勒沃把剑柄插在地上，剑尖朝上，扑倒在剑上死去了。诗的最后，老万奈莫宁听说了这一切，说：以后的人们啊，不要用错误的方式抚养孩子，不要让孩子在陌生人的摇篮里长大，否则他永远学不会正直。卡勒沃之子库勒沃的故事，就这样结束了。','Kullervo came home to find the house empty and the hearth cold; father, mother, brother and sister were all dead. He wept at his mother''s grave, and from the grave his mother said: ''Take our black dog Musti and go into the forest, and ask the spirits of the forest for a place to rest.'' Kullervo went into the forest with the dog and came, without knowing it, to the very wilds where he had wronged his sister. The grass there no longer grew and the leaves had withered, as if the earth itself mourned what had happened. Kullervo drew his sword and asked it: ''My sword, will you drink the blood of a guilty man?'' The sword answered: ''I have drunk the blood of the innocent; why should I not drink yours, who are guilty?'' Kullervo set the hilt in the ground, point upward, and fell upon it and died. At the end of the rune old Väinämöinen hears of it all and says: people of the future, never raise a child wrongly, never let him grow up in a stranger''s cradle, or he will never learn to be upright. So ended the story of Kullervo, son of Kalervo.','claim.v0350.kal_3.12','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十六章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.1','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',1,'宝剑与哭泣的船','The sword and the weeping boat','伊尔马里宁失去了妻子，悲伤了很久。万奈莫宁对他说：我们到北方的波赫约拉去吧，去把三宝夺回来。你为他们锻造了三宝，他们却只给我们带来不幸；三宝在那里磨出粮食、磨出财富，让北方富足，我们却一无所有。伊尔马里宁说：三宝锁在波赫约拉的石山里，有九道锁，根扎进地下九寻深，很难拿到。可他还是答应了，并且为万奈莫宁打造了一把宝剑：剑刃上有月亮和太阳，剑柄上有星星，剑身上有一匹马在嘶鸣，剑柄的圆头上有一只猫在叫，剑鞘上有一只狗在吠。两位英雄骑马出发，走到海边，听见岸边有一艘船在哭泣。那是一艘战船，它哭着说：别的船都去打仗，带回金银财宝，只有我被丢在这里，在木屑堆里一年年地腐烂。万奈莫宁说：不要哭，我会带你去打仗的。他唱起歌来，船自己滑进了水里，两位英雄登上船，向北方驶去。','Ilmarinen grieved long for his lost wife. Väinämöinen said to him: ''Let us go north to Pohjola and win back the Sampo. You forged it for them, yet they bring us only misfortune; there it grinds grain and wealth and makes the north rich, while we have nothing.'' Ilmarinen said: ''The Sampo is locked in the stone mountain of Pohjola behind nine locks, its roots nine fathoms deep; it will be hard to take.'' But he agreed, and forged Väinämöinen a sword: on its blade the moon and the sun, on its hilt the stars, on its side a horse neighing, on the knob of the hilt a cat mewing, on the scabbard a dog barking. The two heroes rode to the sea and heard a boat weeping on the shore. It was a war-boat, and it wept: ''The other boats go to war and bring home gold and silver; only I am left here, rotting year after year among the wood-chips.'' ''Do not weep,'' said Väinämöinen, ''I will take you to war.'' He sang, the boat slid into the water of itself, and the heroes boarded it and sailed north.','claim.v0350.kal_4.01','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十九章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.2','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',2,'勒明凯宁加入','Lemminkäinen joins them','船经过一座岬角时，岸上有人在喊：是谁在海上航行？原来是勒明凯宁。他一个人住在海边，正因为没有了打仗的机会而闷闷不乐，看见两位英雄的船驶过，就大声问他们要到哪里去。万奈莫宁说：我们要到北方的波赫约拉去，去夺回三宝。勒明凯宁说：让我也一起去吧，我会是第三个英雄，我的剑也很锋利，我能在打仗的时候帮上你们的忙。万奈莫宁答应了。勒明凯宁拿着一块木板跳上了船，船身沉了一沉，又平稳地向前驶去。万奈莫宁坐在船尾掌舵，伊尔马里宁和勒明凯宁划桨，船儿像飞鸟一样在海浪上前进。三位卡勒瓦拉最有名的英雄——歌者、铁匠和战士——第一次一同出发，去完成一件谁也没有做到过的事。这位战士在前面的诗章里曾在波赫约拉闯下大祸，不得不逃到海岛上躲了三年，如今他又一次站到了冒险的船上。','As the boat passed a headland, someone on the shore called out: ''Who is sailing on the sea?'' It was Lemminkäinen. Living alone by the sea and sullen for lack of a war to fight, he saw the heroes'' boat go by and shouted to ask where they were going. Väinämöinen said: ''We are going north to Pohjola, to win back the Sampo.'' Lemminkäinen said: ''Let me come too. I will be the third hero; my sword is sharp too, and I can help you in the fighting.'' Väinämöinen agreed. Lemminkäinen leapt aboard with a board under his arm; the boat dipped and then sailed steadily on. Väinämöinen sat at the stern steering, Ilmarinen and Lemminkäinen rowed, and the boat sped over the waves like a bird. The three most famous heroes of Kalevala — the singer, the smith and the warrior — set out together for the first time, to do what no one had ever done. In earlier runes this warrior had brought disaster on himself in Pohjola and had to hide on an island for three years; now he stood once more aboard a boat bound for adventure.','claim.v0350.kal_4.02','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第三十九章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.3','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',3,'大梭鱼','The great pike','船驶进一段湍急的河道，在急流和瀑布之间穿行。万奈莫宁念着咒语，请急流的女神和水中的精灵让船平安通过。可就在急流中央，船忽然停住了，任凭怎么划桨也纹丝不动，好像被什么东西紧紧托住。勒明凯宁探身往水里一看，说：船下面不是礁石，也不是树干，而是一条巨大的梭鱼，我们的船正卡在它的背上。他拔剑向水里砍去，可是用力太猛，自己差点掉进河里，剑也没能伤到梭鱼。伊尔马里宁抓住他的头发，把他拉了回来。伊尔马里宁也拔剑去砍，剑却折断了，梭鱼仍然一动不动。最后，万奈莫宁拔出自己的宝剑，猛地刺进水里，从梭鱼的身下一挑，把这条巨大的鱼刺穿，挑出水面，劈成了几段。他们把梭鱼拖上船，在岸边煮熟，三位英雄美美地吃了一顿，只剩下一堆鱼骨。','The boat ran into a stretch of wild river, threading between rapids and falls. Väinämöinen chanted charms to the goddess of the rapids and the spirits of the water to let them pass safely. But in the middle of the rapids the boat suddenly stopped, and however they rowed it would not move, as if something held it fast. Lemminkäinen leaned over and looked: ''It is no rock and no tree-trunk beneath us, but a giant pike; our boat is stuck on its back.'' He drew his sword and struck into the water, but struck so hard that he nearly fell in, and did the pike no harm; Ilmarinen caught him by the hair and pulled him back. Ilmarinen struck too, and his sword broke, and the pike did not stir. At last Väinämöinen drew his own sword, thrust it into the water and up under the fish, pierced the great pike, lifted it out and cut it in pieces. They hauled it aboard, cooked it on the shore, and the three heroes ate their fill, leaving only a heap of bones.','claim.v0350.kal_4.03','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.4','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',4,'第一把康特勒琴','The first kantele','万奈莫宁看着那堆鱼骨，心想：这些骨头要是做成一件东西，会是什么样子？他拿起梭鱼的颚骨，说：如果有一位巧手的工匠，就能用它做成一件乐器。可是谁也不会做，万奈莫宁就自己动手。他用梭鱼的颚骨做成琴身，用梭鱼的牙齿做成调弦的琴钉，又从恶灵希西那匹骟马的身上取来马鬃，做成了琴弦。这样，世界上第一把康特勒琴就造成了。伊尔马里宁和勒明凯宁都想试一试，他们拨动琴弦，发出的却是刺耳难听的声音。他们把琴带到波赫约拉，那里的人们也争着去弹，有年轻人，有老人，有少女，可没有一个人能弹出好听的曲子，琴声粗哑，人们捂着耳朵，说这琴毫无用处，还是扔进水里算了。可是琴自己哭了起来，说它不愿被扔进水里，它只等着一位真正懂得它的人来弹奏。','Looking at the heap of bones, Väinämöinen wondered what they might become. He took up the pike''s jawbone and said: ''A skilled craftsman could make an instrument of this.'' No one knew how, so he set to it himself. From the jawbone he made the body of the harp, from the pike''s teeth the pegs, and for strings he took hair from the gelding of the evil spirit Hiisi. So the first kantele in the world was made. Ilmarinen and Lemminkäinen both tried it, but when they plucked the strings the sound was harsh and ugly. In Pohjola people crowded to play it — young men and old, maidens too — but no one could draw a fair tune from it; the sound was rough, people covered their ears, and said the harp was useless and had better be thrown into the water. But the kantele wept, saying it would not be thrown away; it was waiting for one who truly understood it.','claim.v0350.kal_4.04','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.5','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',5,'万物倾听','All creatures listen','万奈莫宁洗净双手，坐在一块歌唱的石头上，把康特勒琴放在膝上，用手指拨动琴弦。第一声响起，四周就安静了下来；琴声越来越美，传遍了山林和湖海。森林里的松鼠从这根树枝跳到那根树枝，跑来倾听；白鼬、麋鹿、山猫都来了，狼从沼泽里走出来，熊爬上了篱笆。森林之王塔皮奥和他的家人穿着盛装来到山岗上。天上的鸟儿也飞来了：鹰离开了巢，鹞子从高空落下，天鹅从湖上飞来，就连小小的燕雀也聚在他的肩头。水里的鱼儿都游到岸边，梭鱼、鲑鱼和白鲑把头伸出水面。海王阿赫托也从海底升起，坐在一朵睡莲上倾听。天上的少女们——月亮的女儿和太阳的女儿——正在纺金线、织银布，听见琴声，手里的纺锤都掉了下来。万奈莫宁一连弹了许多天，所有的生灵都被琴声迷住了。','Väinämöinen washed his hands, sat down on a stone of singing, set the kantele on his knee and plucked the strings with his fingers. At the first note all around fell silent; the music grew ever lovelier and spread through hills, woods, lakes and seas. The squirrels of the forest leapt from branch to branch to listen; ermine, elk and lynx came, the wolf came out of the swamp and the bear climbed onto the fence. Tapio, king of the forest, came with his household in festive dress to the hilltop. The birds of the sky came too: the eagle left its nest, the hawk dropped from the heights, the swans flew in from the lakes, and even the little finches gathered on his shoulders. The fishes swam to the shore, pike, salmon and whitefish lifting their heads from the water. Ahto, king of the sea, rose from the depths and sat on a water-lily to listen. The maidens of the sky — the daughters of the moon and of the sun, spinning golden thread and weaving silver cloth — let their spindles fall at the sound. Väinämöinen played for many days, and every creature was enchanted.','claim.v0350.kal_4.05','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.6','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',6,'泪珠','The tears that became pearls','所有听见琴声的人都哭了：年轻的人哭了，老人哭了，男人和女人都哭了，连小孩子也哭了。最后，万奈莫宁自己也被琴声打动，流下了眼泪。他的泪珠比越橘还大，比豌豆还圆，比鸡蛋还要光亮，一颗颗从他的眼里落下，落到脸颊上，从脸颊落到胸前，从胸前落到膝上，从膝上落到脚下，又从脚下滚进了大海，沉到了海底的黑泥里。万奈莫宁说：谁能把我的眼泪从海底捞上来？乌鸦来试，没有成功。一只蓝色的野鸭飞来，潜进海里，把那些泪珠一颗颗衔了上来。泪珠已经变了样：它们变成了蓝色的珍珠，美丽得可以戴在国王的头上，成为英雄们永远的装饰。这是《卡勒瓦拉》里最安静的时刻之一：在夺取三宝的长途中，歌者停下来，让音乐和眼泪说话。那只野鸭，在原诗里是一只蓝色的鸭子。','Everyone who heard the music wept: the young and the old, men and women, even the little children. At last Väinämöinen himself was moved by his playing and began to weep. His tears were larger than cranberries, rounder than peas, brighter than eggs; they fell from his eyes onto his cheeks, from his cheeks onto his breast, from his breast to his knees, from his knees to his feet, and from his feet rolled into the sea and sank into the dark mud of the bottom. ''Who will bring my tears up from the sea?'' said Väinämöinen. The raven tried and failed. A blue duck came, dived into the sea and brought the tears up one by one. They had changed: they had become blue pearls, beautiful enough to crown a king, an ornament for heroes for ever. It is one of the quietest moments in the Kalevala: on the long road to the Sampo the singer pauses, and lets music and tears speak. In the poem the duck is a blue duck.','claim.v0350.kal_4.06','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十一章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.7','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',7,'唱睡波赫约拉','Singing Pohjola to sleep','船终于到了波赫约拉。英雄们走进洛希的大厅，洛希问他们来做什么。万奈莫宁说：我们来商量三宝的事。三宝在你们这里磨出了粮食和财富，我们也想分享一份。如果你不肯分给我们，我们就把它整个拿走。洛希说：一只松鼠不能分给三个人，一个三宝也不能分给两家。三宝留在波赫约拉，在这里转动，在这里磨面。万奈莫宁说：既然如此，我们就把它全部带回卡勒瓦拉。洛希大怒，召集了波赫约拉的全部武士，手持刀剑围了上来。万奈莫宁不慌不忙，拿起康特勒琴弹奏起来。琴声一响，波赫约拉的人们都停下了脚步，一个个闭上眼睛，倒在地上沉沉睡去。万奈莫宁又从口袋里掏出“睡眠之针”，把他们的睫毛缝在一起，让他们久久不能醒来。整个北方都陷入了沉睡。在这部史诗里，琴声和歌声比刀剑更有力量。','At last the boat reached Pohjola. The heroes went into Louhi''s hall, and she asked their business. Väinämöinen said: ''We have come to speak of the Sampo. Here it grinds grain and wealth for you; we want a share. If you will not share it, we will take the whole.'' Louhi said: ''A squirrel cannot be shared among three, and one Sampo cannot be shared between two households. The Sampo stays in Pohjola, to turn here and grind here.'' ''Then we will take it all to Kalevala,'' said Väinämöinen. Furious, Louhi summoned all the warriors of Pohjola, who closed in with swords. Unhurried, Väinämöinen took up his kantele and played. At the first notes the people of Pohjola stood still, closed their eyes one by one, and fell to the ground in deep sleep. Then he took his needles of sleep from his pouch and stitched their eyelashes together so that they would not wake for a long time. All the north lay asleep. In this epic the harp and the song are mightier than the sword.','claim.v0350.kal_4.07','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十二章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.8','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',8,'挖出三宝','Digging out the Sampo','英雄们来到波赫约拉的铜山前，三宝就锁在山里的九道门后。万奈莫宁轻声唱起开锁的歌，门锁一道道松开了；伊尔马里宁在门轴上抹了黄油，让沉重的铜门打开时不会发出吱呀的响声，免得惊醒睡着的人们。门开了，三宝就在里面，它的根深深地扎进了山岩和地下。勒明凯宁说：让我来，我力气最大。他用脚抵着地，双手抱住三宝，用尽全力往上拔，三宝却纹丝不动，它的根扎得太深了。于是他们牵来波赫约拉最大的一头公牛，套上犁，用犁把三宝的根一根根犁断。三宝终于松动了，英雄们把它从山里抬出来，搬上了船，藏在船舱里。他们解开缆绳，悄悄地离开了波赫约拉的海岸，划着船，向南方的家乡驶去。原诗在第十章里说过，三宝的根有三条：一条扎进大地，一条扎进水边，一条扎进家门前的山丘；如今这三条根都被犁断了，三宝离开了它扎根多年的地方。','The heroes came to the copper mountain of Pohjola, where the Sampo was locked behind nine doors. Väinämöinen softly sang the song that opens locks, and the locks loosened one by one; Ilmarinen smeared butter on the hinges so that the heavy copper doors would open without creaking and wake the sleepers. The doors opened, and there was the Sampo, its roots sunk deep in the rock and the earth. ''Let me,'' said Lemminkäinen, ''I am strongest.'' He braced his feet, clasped the Sampo in his arms and heaved with all his strength, but it did not move; its roots went too deep. So they brought the greatest ox of Pohjola, yoked it to a plough, and ploughed through the Sampo''s roots one by one. At last the Sampo came loose; the heroes carried it out of the mountain, loaded it into the boat and hid it in the hold. They cast off and slipped quietly away from the shores of Pohjola, rowing south towards home. Rune X had told of the Sampo''s three roots, one in the earth, one at the water''s edge, one in the hill by the house; now all three were ploughed through, and the Sampo left the place where it had been rooted so long.','claim.v0350.kal_4.08','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十二章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.9','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',9,'鹤的叫声','The crane''s cry','船在海上平稳地航行，三宝藏在船舱里，三位英雄心里都很高兴。勒明凯宁按捺不住，说：我们夺得了三宝，这样的喜事，怎么能不唱歌呢？万奈莫宁劝他说：现在还不是唱歌的时候。唱歌会被人听见，会把波赫约拉的人吵醒，等我们回到家乡再唱吧。勒明凯宁忍了一天、两天，到了第三天，他再也忍不住了，张开嘴就唱了起来。可他的嗓子粗哑难听，歌声在海面上远远传开。一只鹤正站在沼泽边的草丛里，被这难听的歌声吓了一跳，扑腾着翅膀飞了起来，一路尖叫着飞向北方。它的叫声越过了山岭和海湾，飞进了波赫约拉，把那些被缝住睫毛、沉睡已久的人们全都吵醒了。洛希醒来，跑到铜山一看，三宝不见了。一次不合时宜的歌唱，就这样毁掉了万奈莫宁小心翼翼的计划，追兵很快就要来了。','The boat sailed smoothly over the sea with the Sampo hidden in the hold, and the three heroes were glad. Lemminkäinen could not contain himself: ''We have won the Sampo — how can we not sing at such joy?'' Väinämöinen warned him: ''This is no time for singing. Song will be heard and will wake the people of Pohjola. Wait until we are home.'' Lemminkäinen held back one day, two days, but on the third he could hold back no longer and began to sing. His voice was harsh and ugly, and the song carried far over the water. A crane standing in the reeds by a swamp was startled by the dreadful singing, flapped up, and flew screaming north. Its cry crossed hills and bays and reached Pohjola, waking all the people who had slept so long with their eyelashes sewn. Louhi woke, ran to the copper mountain, and found the Sampo gone. One song at the wrong moment undid all Väinämöinen''s careful plans, and the pursuit would soon be upon them.','claim.v0350.kal_4.09','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十二章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.10','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',10,'雾、海怪与风暴','Fog, the sea monster and the storm','洛希大怒，施展魔法追赶英雄们。她先派来浓雾的少女，让大雾笼罩了海面，英雄们的船在雾中迷失了方向，一连三天三夜停在原地。万奈莫宁拔出宝剑，向大雾劈去，劈开了一条路，雾气四散，船又能前进了。接着，洛希唤起了海怪伊库-图尔索。海怪从海底升起，掀起巨浪，要把船掀翻。万奈莫宁伸手抓住海怪的耳朵，把它提出水面，问它：伊库-图尔索，你为什么从海里升起来，挡在我们的路上？他命令海怪沉回海底，永远不许再在人们面前出现。海怪沉了下去。最后，洛希向乌戈求来一场狂风暴雨，海浪滔天，船身摇晃，万奈莫宁心爱的康特勒琴被一阵大浪卷下船去，沉进了海底。万奈莫宁看着大海，伤心地说：我的快乐已经沉进了海里，再也找不回来了。这把琴后来再也没有找回来，万奈莫宁不得不在后面的诗章里重新做了一把。','Louhi in her fury worked magic to pursue the heroes. First she sent the maiden of the mist, and a thick fog covered the sea; the heroes'' boat lost its way and lay still for three days and nights. Väinämöinen drew his sword and struck at the fog, cleaving a path through it; the mist scattered and the boat went on. Next Louhi called up the sea monster Iku-Turso, which rose from the deep and raised great waves to overturn the boat. Väinämöinen seized the monster by the ears, lifted it out of the water and asked: ''Iku-Turso, why have you risen from the sea to bar our way?'' He commanded it to sink back to the bottom and never again show itself to men, and it sank. Last, Louhi begged Ukko for a raging storm; the waves rose sky-high, the boat pitched and rolled, and a great wave swept Väinämöinen''s beloved kantele overboard into the depths. Gazing at the sea he said sorrowfully: ''My joy has sunk into the sea, and I shall never find it again.'' That harp was never found again; in a later rune Väinämöinen has to make another.','claim.v0350.kal_4.10','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十二章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.11','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',11,'鹰形的洛希','Louhi as an eagle','洛希造了一艘巨大的战船，让上百名武士带着刀剑和弓箭上船，亲自率领他们追赶。眼看就要追上了，她又变出了一个可怕的样子：她把镰刀装在脚上做爪子，把船身变成翅膀，变成一只巨大的鹰，一边翅膀上载着一百个武士，尾巴上载着一千个弓箭手，向英雄们的船扑来。万奈莫宁向乌戈祈祷，求乌戈保护他们。巨鹰落在船的桅杆上，船几乎被压翻。伊尔马里宁和勒明凯宁拔剑去砍，勒明凯宁砍下了鹰的几根羽毛。万奈莫宁从水里拔起船舵，用船舵狠狠地向巨鹰打去，打断了它的爪子，鹰爪纷纷掉进海里，只剩下一个小小的爪尖。洛希的武士们也纷纷从鹰的翅膀上跌落，掉进海里，就像秋天的树叶从树上落下来一样。这是《卡勒瓦拉》里最壮观的一场战斗：一边是变成巨鹰的北方女主人和她的大军，一边是一艘小船上的三位英雄。','Louhi built a great war-ship, put a hundred warriors aboard with swords, bows and arrows, and led the pursuit herself. When she was nearly upon them she took a terrible shape: she fixed scythes to her feet for talons, turned the ship into wings and became a giant eagle, carrying a hundred warriors on one wing and a thousand bowmen on her tail, and swooped down on the heroes'' boat. Väinämöinen prayed to Ukko for protection. The giant eagle settled on the mast and nearly overturned the boat. Ilmarinen and Lemminkäinen struck at it with their swords; Lemminkäinen cut away a few of its feathers. Väinämöinen pulled the rudder from the water and struck the eagle hard with it, smashing its talons, which fell into the sea until only one small claw was left. Louhi''s warriors fell from the eagle''s wings into the sea like autumn leaves falling from a tree. It is the grandest battle in the Kalevala: on one side the mistress of the north turned giant eagle with her host, on the other three heroes in one small boat.','claim.v0350.kal_4.11','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十三章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.finnish_kalevala_crawford_39_43.12','storyver.finnish.kalevala_crawford_39_43',12,'三宝的碎片','The fragments of the Sampo','洛希虽然失去了爪子，却不肯罢休。她用仅剩的那根小小的爪尖——诗中说是她的小指——勾住了三宝，想把它从船上拖走。三宝从船上滑落，跌进了大海，在海中摔成了碎片。大块的碎片沉到了海底，从此大海有了取之不尽的财富，海里的鱼和宝藏都由此而来。小块的碎片随着波浪漂流，漂到了卡勒瓦拉的海岸上。万奈莫宁在岸边一块块地拾起这些碎片，把它们种进土里，祈求它们长出黑麦和大麦，带来丰收和富足。洛希只抢回了三宝那五彩的盖子，带回了波赫约拉，从此北方日渐贫穷。洛希在空中恶狠狠地发誓：我要给你们送去瘟疫，送去冰霜，送去猛兽，我还要把太阳和月亮偷走！万奈莫宁回答说：我不怕你的诅咒。他向乌戈祈祷，请乌戈保护卡勒瓦拉的人们，保护他们的田地和庄稼，让三宝的碎片永远给这片土地带来福祉。','Though she had lost her talons, Louhi would not give up. With the one small claw she had left — her little finger, the poem says — she hooked the Sampo and tried to drag it from the boat. The Sampo slipped from the boat, fell into the sea, and was shattered. The large pieces sank to the bottom, and from them the sea has its endless riches, its fish and its treasures. The small pieces drifted on the waves to the shores of Kalevala. Väinämöinen gathered them one by one on the beach, planted them in the earth, and prayed that they would bring forth rye and barley, plenty and prosperity. Louhi took home to Pohjola only the Sampo''s many-coloured lid, and from then on the north grew poorer. From the air Louhi swore fiercely: ''I will send you plague and frost and wild beasts; I will steal the sun and the moon!'' Väinämöinen answered that he did not fear her curses, and prayed to Ukko to protect the people of Kalevala, their fields and their crops, and to let the fragments of the Sampo bless the land for ever.','claim.v0350.kal_4.12','《卡勒瓦拉》克劳福德译本第四十三章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.1','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',1,'坚战的悲叹','Yudhiṣṭhira''s lament','般度族的长兄坚战在掷骰中输掉了王国，带着四个弟弟和妻子黑公主流放到森林里，要在那里度过十二年，再隐姓埋名过一年。有一天，他的弟弟阿周那去天上求取神兵，还没有回来，坚战坐在林中，心中充满了忧愁。这时，大仙巨马来到他们的住处。坚战向他诉苦说：世上还有比我更不幸的国王吗？我被狡诈的赌徒骗走了王国，带着弟弟和妻子在森林里受苦。巨马说：大王，我给你讲一个比你更不幸的国王的故事。他也是因为掷骰子失去了王国，带着妻子流落森林，后来甚至与妻子分离，独自一人受尽了苦难。可是他终于熬过了一切，重新得到了妻子和王国。坚战说：请你讲给我听。于是巨马开始讲述尼沙陀国王那罗的故事。这个故事，就是一个国王在不幸中讲给另一个不幸的国王听的。','Yudhiṣṭhira, eldest of the Pāṇḍava brothers, had lost his kingdom at dice and gone into exile in the forest with his four brothers and their wife Draupadī, to spend twelve years there and a thirteenth in hiding. One day, while his brother Arjuna was away in heaven seeking divine weapons, Yudhiṣṭhira sat in the forest full of sorrow. Then the great sage Bṛhadaśva came to their dwelling. Yudhiṣṭhira poured out his grief: ''Is there any king in the world more unfortunate than I? Cheated of my kingdom by crafty gamblers, I suffer in the forest with my brothers and my wife.'' Bṛhadaśva said: ''King, I will tell you of a king more unfortunate than you. He too lost his kingdom at dice and went into the forest with his wife; he was even parted from her and suffered alone. Yet he came through it all and won back his wife and his kingdom.'' ''Tell me,'' said Yudhiṣṭhira. So Bṛhadaśva began the story of Nala, king of the Nishadhas: a tale told by one sage to an unfortunate king about another.','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.01','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第五十二节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.2','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',2,'天鹅传情','The swans'' message','从前，尼沙陀国有一位国王，名叫那罗，是英雄维罗森那的儿子。他英俊得像爱神一样，力量强大，精通吠陀，善于驾驭马匹，只有一个弱点：喜欢掷骰子。在毗陀婆国，国王毗摩有一个女儿，名叫达摩衍蒂，美丽得连天上的仙女都比不上。人们在那罗面前常常赞美达摩衍蒂，在达摩衍蒂面前也常常赞美那罗，两人还没有见过面，就已经彼此倾心。有一天，那罗在宫中的花园里抓住了一只金翅膀的天鹅。天鹅对他说：国王，不要杀我，我会到达摩衍蒂那里去，在她面前赞美你，让她除了你谁也不想嫁。那罗放了天鹅。天鹅飞到毗陀婆，落在达摩衍蒂和侍女们的身边，达摩衍蒂追着去抓它，天鹅就用人的语言对她说：尼沙陀国有一位国王，名叫那罗，在男子中他就像天神一样，你和他正好相配。达摩衍蒂说：你也去对他说同样的话吧。','Long ago there was a king of the Nishadhas named Nala, son of Vīrasena. He was handsome as the god of love, mighty, learned in the Vedas and skilled with horses; his one weakness was a love of dice. In Vidarbha, King Bhīma had a daughter named Damayantī, more beautiful than the nymphs of heaven. People praised Damayantī before Nala and Nala before Damayantī, so that the two loved each other before they had met. One day Nala caught a swan with golden wings in his palace garden. The swan said: ''King, do not kill me. I will go to Damayantī and praise you before her, so that she will want no one but you.'' Nala let it go. The swan flew to Vidarbha and alighted near Damayantī and her maidens; when she ran to catch it, it spoke to her in human speech: ''In Nishadha there is a king named Nala, like a god among men; you and he are made for each other.'' Damayantī said: ''Go and say the same to him.''','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.02','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第五十三节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.3','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',3,'四位天神','The four gods','达摩衍蒂从此心神不宁，日渐消瘦。父亲毗摩王看出女儿到了出嫁的年纪，就宣布为她举行选婿大会，让天下的国王都来，由她亲自挑选夫婿。消息传到了天上。天神之王因陀罗、火神阿耆尼、水神伐楼那和死神阎摩，听仙人那罗陀说起达摩衍蒂的美貌，也都想去参加选婿。四位天神在路上遇见了正赶往毗陀婆的那罗，见他英俊非凡，就停下来对他说：尼沙陀王，你是一个守信的人，请你答应为我们做一件事，做我们的使者。那罗双手合十，答应了。他问：你们是谁？要我做什么？因陀罗说：我们是天神，为了达摩衍蒂而来。你去对她说，四位天神都要娶她，请她在我们之中挑选一位做丈夫。那罗说：我也是为了她而来的，怎么能去替别人求婚呢？天神说：你已经答应了，就要信守诺言。','Damayantī grew restless and pale. Seeing that his daughter was of an age to marry, King Bhīma proclaimed a svayaṃvara, a gathering where she herself would choose her husband from the kings of the earth. The news reached heaven. Indra king of the gods, Agni god of fire, Varuṇa god of the waters and Yama god of death, hearing from the sage Nārada of Damayantī''s beauty, all wished to go. On the way they met Nala hurrying to Vidarbha, and seeing how handsome he was they stopped and said: ''King of the Nishadhas, you are a man true to his word; promise to do us a service and be our messenger.'' Nala joined his palms and promised. ''Who are you, and what would you have me do?'' Indra said: ''We are gods, come for Damayantī. Go and tell her that the four gods wish to wed her, and that she should choose one of us as her husband.'' Nala said: ''I have come for her myself; how can I woo her for others?'' The gods said: ''You have promised; keep your word.''','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.03','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第五十四节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.4','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',4,'使者那罗','Nala the messenger','天神让那罗隐去身形，他一路畅通无阻地走进了毗陀婆王宫的后宫。达摩衍蒂和侍女们看见一个英俊的男子忽然出现在面前，都吃了一惊，又羞又喜。达摩衍蒂问他：你是谁？怎么进来的？宫中守卫森严啊。那罗说：我叫那罗，是天神的使者。因陀罗、阿耆尼、伐楼那和阎摩四位天神都想娶你，请你在他们之中选一位做丈夫。是天神的力量让我进来，没有被人看见。达摩衍蒂向天神行了礼，然后笑着对那罗说：国王，我的心早已属于你。我愿意把自己和一切都交给你。天鹅的话一直在我心里燃烧，正是为了你，我才召集了这许多国王。如果你拒绝我，我就服毒、投火、投水，或者自缢而死。那罗说：天神们都在那里，你怎么能选一个凡人？天神是世界的主宰，我连他们脚下的尘土都比不上。达摩衍蒂哭了，然后想出了一个办法：我会当着天神的面选你，这样你就不算违背承诺。','The gods made Nala invisible, and he passed unhindered into the inner palace of Vidarbha. Damayantī and her maidens were startled, shy and delighted to see a handsome man suddenly before them. ''Who are you, and how did you come in? The palace is closely guarded.'' ''I am Nala,'' he said, ''messenger of the gods. Indra, Agni, Varuṇa and Yama wish to marry you; choose one of them as your husband. It was the gods'' power that brought me in unseen.'' Damayantī bowed to the gods, then smiled and said to Nala: ''King, my heart has long been yours; I give myself and all I have to you. The swan''s words burn in my heart; it was for you that I called these kings together. If you reject me I will take poison, or fire, or water, or the rope.'' Nala said: ''With the gods there, how can you choose a mortal? They are the lords of the world; I am not worth the dust of their feet.'' Damayantī wept, then found a way: ''I will choose you in the presence of the gods, so that you will not have broken your promise.''','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.04','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第五十五至五十六节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.5','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',5,'五个那罗','Five Nalas','选婿大会的日子到了。国王们佩着香花、戴着耳环，坐满了金柱撑起的大厅。达摩衍蒂走进来，她的美貌吸引了所有人的目光。侍从们一一念出国王们的名字，她走到那罗的座位前，却愣住了：那里并排坐着五个一模一样的那罗。原来四位天神都变成了那罗的模样，坐在他的身边。达摩衍蒂无法分辨，心中焦急，就在心里向天神祈祷：自从听了天鹅的话，我就选定了那罗做丈夫，我的心、我的言语从未离开过他；天神们啊，如果这是真的，请显出你们本来的样子，让我认出那罗。天神被她的诚心感动，显出了天神的特征：他们身上不流汗，眼睛不眨，花环鲜艳不凋，衣上没有灰尘，双脚不沾地面，也没有影子。只有一个人身上有影子，花环已经开始凋萎，额上沾着汗珠和尘土，双脚踏在地上，眼睛会眨——那就是真正的那罗。达摩衍蒂羞涩地拉住他的衣角，把花环戴在了他的颈上。','The day of the svayaṃvara came. Kings garlanded and wearing earrings filled the hall on its golden pillars. Damayantī entered, drawing every eye. Her attendants named the kings one by one, and when she came to Nala''s seat she stopped short: five Nalas sat side by side, all alike. The four gods had taken Nala''s form and sat beside him. Unable to tell them apart, she prayed in her heart: ''Ever since I heard the swan I have chosen Nala as my husband; my heart and my words have never left him. Gods, if this is true, show your own forms and let me know Nala.'' Moved by her truth, the gods showed the marks of gods: they did not sweat, their eyes did not blink, their garlands were fresh and unfaded, their clothes free of dust, their feet did not touch the ground, and they cast no shadow. Only one cast a shadow, his garland beginning to fade, sweat and dust on his brow, his feet on the ground, his eyes blinking — the real Nala. Shyly Damayantī took the hem of his garment and placed her garland round his neck.','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.05','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第五十七节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.6','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',6,'迦利的怨恨','Kali''s grudge','四位天神没有生气，他们都很高兴，各自赐给那罗两个恩惠：因陀罗让他在祭祀时能亲眼看见天神，并能到达天界；阿耆尼让他想要火的时候火就会出现，并让他拥有像火一样光明的世界；阎摩赐给他烹调的美味和对正法的坚定；伐楼那让他想要水的时候水就会出现，并赐给他永不凋谢的花环。那罗和达摩衍蒂结了婚，回到尼沙陀，生下了一儿一女，过着幸福的生活，那罗举行了许多盛大的祭祀。可是，天神们在回天上的路上，遇见了迦利和达婆波罗——争斗与混乱时代的化身——正要去参加选婿。天神告诉他们，达摩衍蒂已经选了那罗。迦利大怒，说：她竟然在天神面前选了一个凡人，我要惩罚她。天神劝他说，那罗德行完美，选他是正当的。迦利不听，对达婆波罗说：我要住进那罗的身体里，夺走他的王国，让他和达摩衍蒂分离。你要钻进骰子里帮助我。','The four gods were not angry but pleased, and each gave Nala two boons: Indra, that he would see the gods with his own eyes at sacrifices and reach the heavenly worlds; Agni, that fire would appear whenever he wished and that he would have worlds bright as fire; Yama, skill in fine cooking and steadfastness in dharma; Varuṇa, that water would appear whenever he wished, and garlands that never faded. Nala and Damayantī married, went home to Nishadha, and lived happily; they had a son and a daughter, and Nala performed many great sacrifices. But on their way back to heaven the gods met Kali and Dvāpara, personifications of the ages of strife and confusion, who were on their way to the svayaṃvara. The gods told them Damayantī had chosen Nala. Kali flew into a rage: ''She chose a mortal in the presence of the gods; I will punish her.'' The gods said Nala was perfect in virtue and the choice was just. Kali would not listen and said to Dvāpara: ''I will enter Nala''s body, take his kingdom and part him from Damayantī. You enter the dice and help me.''','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.06','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第五十七至五十八节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.7','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',7,'掷骰失国','The kingdom lost at dice','迦利来到尼沙陀，等着那罗犯下一个过失。他足足等了十二年。终于有一天，那罗小解之后，没有洗脚就去做黄昏的祈祷。迦利趁机钻进了他的身体。接着，迦利去找那罗的弟弟补湿迦罗，对他说：去和那罗掷骰子吧，有我的帮助，你一定能赢得他的王国。补湿迦罗就来向那罗挑战，那罗被迦利迷住了心窍，无法拒绝。两人掷起了骰子，那罗把金子、银子、车辆、马匹、衣服一样样地输了出去，越输越赌，赌了好几个月。大臣和百姓都来劝他，达摩衍蒂也一次次请他停下，可他像着了魔一样，一句话也听不进去。达摩衍蒂知道大势已去，就叫来车夫瓦尔施奈耶，让他驾车把两个孩子送回毗陀婆的娘家。最后，那罗输掉了全部王国，只剩身上的一件衣服。补湿迦罗笑着问他：你还有什么可以赌的？只剩下达摩衍蒂了，把她押上吧。那罗心如刀绞，一言不发，脱下身上所有的饰物，只穿着一件衣服走出了城，达摩衍蒂也穿着一件衣服跟在他身后。','Kali came to Nishadha and waited for Nala to commit some fault. He waited twelve full years. At last one day Nala, after answering a call of nature, said his evening prayers without washing his feet, and Kali entered his body. Then Kali went to Nala''s brother Pushkara: ''Play dice with Nala; with my help you will win his kingdom.'' Pushkara challenged Nala, and Nala, his mind clouded by Kali, could not refuse. They played, and Nala lost his gold, his silver, his chariots, his horses, his clothes, staking more as he lost more, for months on end. His ministers and people came to beg him to stop, and Damayantī pleaded again and again, but he was as one possessed and would hear nothing. Knowing all was lost, Damayantī called the charioteer Vārṣṇeya and had him drive their two children to her father''s house in Vidarbha. At last Nala lost the whole kingdom and had nothing but the cloth he wore. Pushkara laughed: ''What have you left to stake? Only Damayantī — stake her.'' Heartbroken, Nala said nothing, took off all his ornaments, and left the city in a single garment, Damayantī following in a single garment behind him.','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.07','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第五十九至六十一节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.8','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',8,'森林中的分离','Parting in the forest','补湿迦罗下令：谁要是收留那罗，就处死谁。那罗和达摩衍蒂只好离开都城，在城外住了三天，只靠喝水度日，然后走进了森林。那罗饥饿难忍，看见几只金色的鸟，就脱下身上唯一的衣服去罩它们，鸟儿却叼起衣服飞上天空，说：我们就是骰子，我们来是为了夺走你最后的一件衣服。那罗赤着身子，羞愧万分，对达摩衍蒂说：这里有许多条路，这条通往阿般提，那条通往毗陀婆，你父亲的国家。他一再指给她看去毗陀婆的路。达摩衍蒂哭着说：你是要丢下我吗？我不会离开你。两人共披着达摩衍蒂的一件衣服，来到一间破旧的歇脚处，躺在地上睡着了。半夜，那罗醒来，心中被迦利搅得翻腾不止。他想：如果我离开她，她也许会回到父亲那里，过上安稳的日子。他找到一把刀，割下了达摩衍蒂衣裳的一半披在自己身上，看了熟睡的妻子一眼又一眼，走开又回来，回来又走开，最后还是被迦利驱使着，丢下她独自走进了森林。','Pushkara proclaimed that anyone who sheltered Nala would die. So Nala and Damayantī left the capital, lived three days outside the city on water alone, and went into the forest. Faint with hunger, Nala saw some golden birds and took off his only garment to throw over them, but the birds carried it off into the sky, crying: ''We are the dice! We came to take even your last garment.'' Naked and ashamed, Nala said to Damayantī: ''Here many roads part; this goes to Avanti, that to Vidarbha, your father''s land,'' and pointed out the road to Vidarbha again and again. Weeping, she said: ''Do you mean to leave me? I will not leave you.'' Sharing her single garment, they came to a ruined rest-house and lay down on the ground to sleep. At midnight Nala woke, his mind tossed by Kali. ''If I leave her, perhaps she will go to her father and live in peace.'' Finding a knife, he cut away half of Damayantī''s garment to cover himself, looked again and again at his sleeping wife, went away and came back and went away again, and at last, driven by Kali, left her and went alone into the forest.','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.08','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第六十一至六十二节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.9','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',9,'达摩衍蒂的漂泊','Damayantī''s wanderings','达摩衍蒂醒来，不见了丈夫，哭喊着在森林里四处寻找。她走着走着，被一条巨大的蟒蛇缠住了身子，正在呼救，一个猎人赶来，砍死了蟒蛇救了她。可这个猎人见她美丽，起了邪念，想要侵犯她。达摩衍蒂愤怒地诅咒他，猎人当即倒地而死。她继续在森林中游荡，向山、向树、向苦行者打听丈夫的下落，苦行者们安慰她说，她终会与那罗重逢，说完就连同净修林一起消失了。后来她遇见一支商队，跟着他们赶路，夜里在湖边宿营，一群野象冲进营地，把商人们踩死了许多。幸存的人说她是个不祥的女人，达摩衍蒂只好又独自上路，衣衫褴褛，蓬头垢面，来到了车底国的都城。城里的孩子们跟在她身后看热闹。王太后从宫楼上看见了她，觉得她虽然落魄却气质不凡，就把她接进宫里，让她做公主的侍女。达摩衍蒂提出条件：不吃剩饭，不替人洗脚，不和别的男人说话。王太后答应了。','Damayantī woke to find her husband gone and searched the forest weeping and calling. As she went, a huge python coiled around her; she cried for help, and a hunter came and killed the snake. But the hunter, seeing her beauty, meant to assault her. In her anger Damayantī cursed him, and he fell dead. She wandered on, asking the mountain, the trees and some ascetics where her husband was; the ascetics comforted her that she would meet Nala again, and then vanished with their hermitage. Later she joined a caravan of merchants, but as they camped by a lake at night a herd of wild elephants stampeded through the camp and trampled many of them. The survivors called her a woman of ill omen, and she went on alone, ragged and dishevelled, until she came to the capital of the Chedis, with children trailing after her. The queen mother saw her from the palace terrace, sensed nobility beneath the rags, and took her in as a companion to the princess. Damayantī made conditions: she would not eat leavings, would not wash anyone''s feet, and would speak with no other men. The queen mother agreed.','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.09','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第六十三至六十五节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.10','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',10,'迦尔郭吒迦','Karkoṭaka','那罗离开妻子以后，在森林里看见一场大火，火中有声音喊道：那罗，快来救我！他走进火里，看见一条巨大的蛇盘成一团，那是蛇王迦尔郭吒迦。迦尔郭吒迦说：我被仙人那罗陀诅咒，动弹不得，只有你能救我。我会做你的朋友，给你忠告。它把身子缩小到像拇指那么大，那罗把它拿起来，走出了火焰。蛇王说：你一边走一边数步子，我要报答你。那罗数到第十步——“十”这个词在梵语里听起来也像“咬”——蛇王就咬了他一口。那罗的模样立刻变了，变得矮小丑陋。迦尔郭吒迦说：我改变你的模样，是为了让人们认不出你。附在你身上的迦利会被我的毒液折磨。从此你不会受任何毒物和敌人的伤害。你到阿逾陀去，投奔国王利图帕尔纳，自称车夫巴胡迦，他精通骰子，会把骰子的学问教给你，你教他驾驭马匹的学问。等你想恢复原形时，就想起我，穿上这件衣服。它给了那罗两件神衣，就消失了。','After leaving his wife, Nala saw a great fire in the forest, and from it a voice cried: ''Nala, come and save me!'' He went into the fire and found a huge serpent coiled there, the nāga king Karkoṭaka, who said: ''Cursed by the sage Nārada, I cannot move; only you can save me. I will be your friend and counsel you.'' He made himself as small as a thumb, and Nala picked him up and carried him out of the flames. ''Walk on, counting your steps,'' said the nāga, ''and I will repay you.'' At the tenth step — the Sanskrit word for ten also sounds like ''bite'' — Karkoṭaka bit him, and at once Nala''s form changed, short and misshapen. Karkoṭaka said: ''I have changed your form so that no one will know you. Kali, who dwells in you, will be tormented by my venom. From now on no poison and no enemy will harm you. Go to Ayodhyā, to King Ṛtuparṇa, and call yourself Bāhuka, a charioteer. He is a master of dice and will teach you that science in return for your knowledge of horses. When you wish to resume your form, think of me and put on this garment.'' He gave Nala two divine garments and vanished.','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.10','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第六十六节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.11','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',11,'寻找那罗','The search for Nala','那罗以巴胡迦的名字在阿逾陀做了利图帕尔纳的车夫，掌管马厩。每天傍晚，他都独自吟诵一句诗：那个受饥渴折磨、疲惫不堪的女人，如今躺在哪里，还在想着那个蠢人吗？另一边，毗陀婆的毗摩王派出许多婆罗门，到四方寻找女儿和女婿。婆罗门须提婆来到车底国，在宫中看见一个穿着破衣、面容憔悴的女子，认出她眉间那颗像莲花一样的红痣，知道她就是达摩衍蒂。王太后这才知道，她原来是自己的外甥女，就派人把她送回了毗陀婆。达摩衍蒂回到父母身边，又请婆罗门们出去寻找那罗，让他们在各地的集会上念出这样几句话：赌徒啊，你割下你妻子的一半衣服，把熟睡的她丢在森林里，你去了哪里？她仍然穿着那半件衣服，日夜为你哭泣。后来，婆罗门帕尔纳达回来报告：在阿逾陀，利图帕尔纳的一个车夫，名叫巴胡迦，听了这几句话，叹着气回答说：好女子即使遭到抛弃，也会守护自己的贞节。','As Bāhuka, Nala served Ṛtuparṇa at Ayodhyā as his charioteer and master of the stables. Every evening he would recite a single verse alone: ''Where does she lie now, tormented by hunger and thirst, weary — does she still think of that fool?'' Meanwhile King Bhīma of Vidarbha sent many brahmins out in all directions to search for his daughter and son-in-law. The brahmin Sudeva came to the Chedi court, saw a woman in worn clothes with a wasted face, recognised the lotus-like red mole between her brows, and knew her for Damayantī. Only then did the queen mother learn she was her own niece, and she sent her home to Vidarbha. Back with her parents, Damayantī sent the brahmins out again to seek Nala, telling them to recite these words at every gathering: ''Gambler, where have you gone, having cut away half your wife''s garment and left her asleep in the forest? She still wears that half garment and weeps for you day and night.'' At last the brahmin Parṇāda came back and reported that at Ayodhyā a charioteer of Ṛtuparṇa named Bāhuka, hearing the words, had sighed and answered: ''A good woman, even when abandoned, guards her own virtue.''','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.11','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第六十七至七十节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.12','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',12,'百由旬的车程与骰子的学问','The hundred-yojana drive and the science of dice','达摩衍蒂听了帕尔纳达的报告，心里明白了，就想出一个办法。她请婆罗门须提婆到阿逾陀去，对利图帕尔纳说：毗陀婆的达摩衍蒂要举行第二次选婿，就在明天日出的时候，你若想去，就要赶快。从阿逾陀到毗陀婆有一百由旬远，只有一天的时间。利图帕尔纳召来巴胡迦，巴胡迦听了，心如刀割，以为妻子真要改嫁，可还是挑选了几匹瘦弱却有好血统的马，驾着车飞驰起来，快得像鸟儿在天上飞。途中，利图帕尔纳的上衣被风吹落，他想停车去拾，巴胡迦说：已经跑出一由旬远了。路过一棵毗毗陀迦树，利图帕尔纳为了炫耀自己的计数本领，说出了树上的叶子和果实的数目。巴胡迦停下车，把树砍倒一数，果然分毫不差。他说：请把这门学问教给我，我把驾驭马匹的学问教给你。利图帕尔纳把骰子的学问传给了他。就在那一刻，迦利从那罗的身体里出来，吐出了迦尔郭吒迦的毒液，钻进了那棵毗毗陀迦树。','Hearing Parṇāda''s report, Damayantī understood and devised a plan. She sent the brahmin Sudeva to Ayodhyā to tell Ṛtuparṇa that Damayantī of Vidarbha would hold a second svayaṃvara at sunrise the next day, and that if he wished to go he must hurry. From Ayodhyā to Vidarbha was a hundred yojanas, with only a day to cover them. Ṛtuparṇa summoned Bāhuka, who was cut to the heart, thinking his wife meant to remarry, yet chose some lean horses of good breed and drove so fast that the chariot flew like a bird through the sky. On the way Ṛtuparṇa''s upper garment blew off, and he wanted to stop for it; ''We are already a yojana past it,'' said Bāhuka. Passing a Vibhītaka tree, Ṛtuparṇa, to show off his skill with numbers, told the number of its leaves and fruits. Bāhuka stopped, felled the tree and counted, and the number was exact. ''Teach me this science,'' he said, ''and I will teach you the science of horses.'' Ṛtuparṇa taught him the science of dice. At that moment Kali came out of Nala''s body, vomiting Karkoṭaka''s venom, and entered the Vibhītaka tree.','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.12','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第七十一至七十二节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.13','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',13,'相认','Recognition','利图帕尔纳的车隆隆地驶进毗陀婆的都城，车声震动了宫殿，达摩衍蒂一听就知道，只有那罗能把车驾得这样快。可是走出来的车夫却矮小丑陋。达摩衍蒂不敢确定，就派侍女盖希妮去试探他。盖希妮回来说：这个车夫很奇怪：他走到低矮的门前，门会自己升高；水罐会自己装满水；他把草拿在手里，草会燃起火来；他碰过的花朵，反而开得更加鲜艳。达摩衍蒂又让人把巴胡迦做的饭菜拿来尝了一口，正是那罗做菜的味道。她又让侍女带着两个孩子去见他，巴胡迦一见孩子，就抱着他们痛哭起来。达摩衍蒂终于把他请进屋里。她穿着那半件衣服，哭着问他：你为什么在森林里丢下熟睡的妻子？那罗说：那是迦利做的，如今他已经离开了我。可你为什么又要举行第二次选婿？达摩衍蒂说：那只是为了把你找回来。天上传来风神的声音，证明她一直忠贞不二。那罗想起迦尔郭吒迦，穿上那件神衣，恢复了本来的模样。夫妻二人紧紧相拥。','Ṛtuparṇa''s chariot thundered into the capital of Vidarbha, the noise shaking the palace, and Damayantī knew at once that only Nala drove like that. But the charioteer who stepped down was short and misshapen. Unsure, she sent her maid Keśinī to test him. Keśinī came back and said: ''This charioteer is strange. When he comes to a low doorway it rises for him; water-jars fill themselves; grass in his hand bursts into flame; flowers he touches bloom brighter.'' Damayantī had food that Bāhuka had cooked brought and tasted it: it was Nala''s cooking. She sent her maid with their two children to him, and Bāhuka, seeing them, clasped them and wept. At last she called him in. Wearing the half garment, she asked him weeping: ''Why did you leave your sleeping wife in the forest?'' Nala said: ''That was Kali''s doing, and now he has left me. But why are you holding a second svayaṃvara?'' ''Only to bring you back,'' said Damayantī. A voice from the sky — the wind god — bore witness that she had been faithful throughout. Nala thought of Karkoṭaka, put on the divine garment and resumed his true form, and husband and wife held each other close.','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.13','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第七十三至七十六节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_nala.14','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_nala',14,'赢回王国','The kingdom won back','利图帕尔纳知道了真相，向那罗道歉，那罗也把驾驭马匹的学问教给了他，两人友好地分别。那罗在毗陀婆住了一个月，然后带着一辆车、十六头大象、五十匹马和六百名步兵，回到尼沙陀，来到弟弟补湿迦罗的面前，说：我们再来掷骰子吧。我用我的一切财富和达摩衍蒂作赌注，你用你的王国作赌注；如果你不愿意掷骰子，我们就用弓箭决一胜负。补湿迦罗以为自己一定会赢，笑着说：好啊，我早就想赢得达摩衍蒂了。可这一次，那罗已经掌握了骰子的学问，只掷了一把，就赢回了全部的王国和财富。补湿迦罗吓得面无人色，以为必死无疑。那罗却对他说：这一切不是你的过错，而是迦利做的。你仍然是我的弟弟，我宽恕你，你可以平安地回到你的城里去。他把妻子儿女接回尼沙陀，重新统治了王国。巨马对坚战说：那罗遭受的苦难比你更深，而他终于等到了好日子。你也会这样。他还把骰子的学问传授给了坚战。','Learning the truth, Ṛtuparṇa asked Nala''s pardon; Nala taught him the science of horses, and they parted friends. After a month in Vidarbha, Nala went back to Nishadha with a chariot, sixteen elephants, fifty horses and six hundred foot-soldiers, and stood before his brother Pushkara: ''Let us play again. I stake all my wealth and Damayantī; you stake your kingdom. If you will not play, let us decide it with the bow.'' Sure of winning, Pushkara laughed: ''Gladly — I have long wished to win Damayantī.'' But Nala now had the science of dice, and with a single throw he won back the whole kingdom and its wealth. Pushkara went pale, expecting death. But Nala said: ''None of this was your fault; it was Kali''s doing. You are still my brother. I forgive you; go back safely to your city.'' He brought his wife and children home to Nishadha and ruled his kingdom again. Bṛhadaśva said to Yudhiṣṭhira: ''Nala suffered more deeply than you, and at last his good days came; so will yours.'' And he taught Yudhiṣṭhira the science of dice.','claim.v0350.mbh_nala.14','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》那罗故事第七十七至七十九节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.1','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',1,'马主王的祈求','King Aśvapati''s prayer','坚战在森林里问仙人摩根德耶：世上可曾有过像我们的黑公主那样贤德、那样忠于丈夫的女子？摩根德耶说：有的，大王，请听莎维德丽的故事。从前，摩德罗国有一位国王，名叫马主王。他虔诚守法，慷慨仁慈，深受百姓爱戴，只有一件心事：他已经年老，却没有一个孩子。为了求得子嗣，他严格地守戒、节制饮食，每天念诵十万遍莎维德丽祷词，向祭火献上供品，只在每天的第六个时辰进一点食物。他这样坚持了整整十八年。到了第十八年，女神莎维德丽被他的虔诚打动，从祭火中现出身形，满面喜悦地对他说：国王，我对你的苦行、你的守戒和你的虔诚都很满意。你想要什么恩惠，就说吧，只是不要违背正法。这个故事收在《森林篇》里，名为“贞妇颂”，意思是称颂忠贞妻子的篇章。坚战之所以这样问，是因为黑公主曾在俱卢族的大殿上受尽屈辱，却始终坚定地站在丈夫们一边。','In the forest Yudhiṣṭhira asked the sage Mārkaṇḍeya: ''Has there ever been a woman as virtuous and as devoted to her husband as our Draupadī?'' ''There has, king,'' said Mārkaṇḍeya; ''hear the story of Sāvitrī. Long ago in the land of Madra there was a king named Aśvapati, righteous, generous and kind, beloved of his people. He had one sorrow: he was growing old and had no child. To win offspring he kept strict vows and fasting, recited the Sāvitrī prayer a hundred thousand times a day, made offerings into the sacred fire, and ate only a little at the sixth hour. So he persevered for eighteen full years. In the eighteenth year the goddess Sāvitrī, pleased with his devotion, rose in bodily form from the sacrificial fire and said to him joyfully: King, I am pleased with your austerities, your vows and your devotion. Ask whatever boon you wish, only let it not go against dharma.'' The story stands in the Forest Book under the name Pativratā-māhātmya, the glory of the faithful wife. Yudhiṣṭhira asks because Draupadī had suffered humiliation in the Kuru hall yet stood steadfast beside her husbands.','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.01','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十一节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.2','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',2,'女神的恩赐','The goddess''s gift','马主王说：女神啊，我所求的只是子嗣，为了延续家族，我才守戒修行。婆罗门们告诉我，子嗣是最大的功德。如果你满意，就请赐给我许多儿子吧。女神说：国王，我已经知道你的心意，也已经为你向梵天说过了。由于梵天的恩典，你很快就会有一个光彩照人的女儿。这是自生者梵天的旨意，你不要再说什么了。说完，女神就消失了。不久，王后怀了孕，生下一个女儿，眼睛像莲花一样美丽。因为她是女神莎维德丽所赐，父亲和婆罗门们就给她取名叫莎维德丽。公主一天天长大，美丽得像天上的吉祥天女，人们看见她，都以为是一位天上的女神下凡来了。她的光芒就像祭火一样明亮，让人不敢直视。女神莎维德丽本是那首每天念诵的祷词的化身，公主就以她的名字为名，仿佛她本身就是一句祈祷的回应。','Aśvapati said: ''Goddess, I ask only for offspring; it was for the continuance of my line that I kept these vows. The brahmins tell me offspring is the highest merit. If you are pleased, grant me many sons.'' The goddess said: ''King, I knew your wish and have already spoken for you to Brahmā. By his grace you will soon have a radiant daughter. This is the will of the Self-born; say no more.'' With that the goddess vanished. Soon the queen conceived and bore a daughter with eyes as lovely as lotuses. Because she was the goddess Sāvitrī''s gift, her father and the brahmins named her Sāvitrī. The princess grew up as beautiful as Śrī, goddess of fortune, and those who saw her thought a goddess had come down from heaven. Her radiance was as bright as the sacrificial fire, so that people could hardly look at her. The goddess Sāvitrī is the embodiment of the prayer recited each day, and the princess bore her name as though she were herself the answer to a prayer.','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.02','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十一节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.3','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',3,'自己去选','Sent to choose for herself','莎维德丽到了出嫁的年纪，可她的美丽和光彩让所有的人都望而却步，竟然没有一个人敢来向她求婚。有一天，她斋戒沐浴，在祭火前礼拜之后，捧着供过神的花环来到父亲面前，向父亲行礼。马主王看着已经长大的女儿，心中忧愁，对她说：女儿，你出嫁的时候到了，可是没有人来向你求婚。我自己也找不到配得上你的人。圣典上说，女儿到了年纪而父亲不让她出嫁，父亲是要受责备的。所以，你自己去吧，去寻找一个与你相配的丈夫，选中了谁，就告诉我，我会按你的心意把你嫁给他。马主王派了年老的大臣陪伴她，给了她车辆和随从。莎维德丽向父亲行了礼，乘上金车，出发去拜访各地的王家仙人的净修林，在那些圣地中寻找自己的夫婿。她走遍了许多圣地，在每一处都向婆罗门布施财物，在一座座净修林之间寻访。','Sāvitrī came of age to marry, but her beauty and radiance daunted everyone, and no one dared to ask for her hand. One day, after fasting and bathing and worshipping before the sacred fire, she came to her father carrying the garland that had been offered to the gods and bowed to him. Aśvapati looked at his grown daughter with a troubled heart and said: ''Daughter, your time to marry has come, but no one comes to seek you, and I myself can find no one worthy of you. The scriptures blame a father who does not give his daughter in marriage when she is of age. So go yourself and seek a husband who is your equal; tell me whom you choose, and I will give you to him as you wish.'' He sent old ministers with her and gave her chariots and attendants. Sāvitrī bowed to her father, mounted a golden chariot, and set out to visit the hermitages of the royal sages, seeking her husband among those holy places. She travelled to many holy places, giving wealth to the brahmins at each, and went from hermitage to hermitage.','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.03','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十一节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.4','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',4,'那罗陀的预言','Nārada''s prophecy','过了一些日子，莎维德丽回来了。她走进宫殿时，仙人那罗陀正坐在马主王的身边谈话。马主王问女儿选中了谁。莎维德丽说：沙尔瓦国有一位国王，名叫迪尤摩特舍那，他双目失明以后，被宿敌夺走了王国，带着妻子和年幼的儿子流落到森林里，在那里过着苦行者的生活。他的儿子生在城里，长在净修林里，名叫萨谛梵。我心里已经选定他做我的丈夫。那罗陀听了，叹息说：哎呀，莎维德丽做了一件不幸的事。萨谛梵的父亲说话诚实，母亲说话诚实，所以婆罗门给他取名萨谛梵，意思是“说真话的人”。他光彩如太阳，智慧如祭主，勇敢如因陀罗，宽厚如大地，慷慨、守法、英俊。马主王问：那么他有什么缺点吗？那罗陀说：他只有一个缺点，没有别的：从今天算起，一年以后，萨谛梵的寿命就要终结，他会离开这个世界。','Some time later Sāvitrī returned. When she entered the palace, the sage Nārada was sitting and talking with Aśvapati. Her father asked whom she had chosen. She said: ''In the land of Śālva there was a king named Dyumatsena. When he went blind, an old enemy seized his kingdom, and he went into the forest with his wife and his young son to live as an ascetic. His son, born in the city and raised in the hermitage, is named Satyavān. In my heart I have chosen him for my husband.'' Hearing this, Nārada sighed: ''Alas, Sāvitrī has done an unhappy thing. His father speaks truth and his mother speaks truth, so the brahmins named him Satyavān, the truthful one. He is as radiant as the sun, as wise as Bṛhaspati, as brave as Indra, as patient as the earth, generous, righteous and handsome.'' ''Has he then any fault?'' asked Aśvapati. Nārada said: ''One fault only and no other: a year from today, Satyavān''s life will end and he will leave this world.''','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.04','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十二节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.5','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',5,'不改的心意','A choice made once','马主王听了，对女儿说：莎维德丽，你去另选一个丈夫吧，萨谛梵这个缺点盖过了他所有的美德，一年之后他就会死去。莎维德丽回答说：父亲，一个人一生只能分一次家产，女儿一生只能嫁一次，一个人一生只能说一次“我给你”——这三件事都只做一次。不管他是长寿还是短命，有德还是无德，我已经选定了丈夫，就绝不会再选第二个。心里决定的事，嘴里说出来，再用行动去做，我的心已经认定了他。那罗陀说：国王，你女儿的心意坚定，不会改变正法的道路。萨谛梵的美德别人都没有，就把女儿嫁给他吧。马主王说：仙人的话是不可违背的，就这样办吧。那罗陀祝福了他们，升上天空，回到了天界。马主王本想劝女儿回心转意，听了这番话，也明白再说什么都是徒劳的。一年的期限，从这一天就开始计算了。','Aśvapati said to his daughter: ''Sāvitrī, go and choose another husband. This one fault outweighs all Satyavān''s virtues: in a year he will die.'' Sāvitrī answered: ''Father, an inheritance is divided only once, a daughter is given in marriage only once, and a man says I give only once — these three things happen once. Whether he is long-lived or short-lived, virtuous or not, I have chosen my husband once, and I will not choose a second. What the mind resolves, the tongue speaks and the deed performs; my mind has fixed on him.'' Nārada said: ''King, your daughter''s mind is firm and will not stray from the path of dharma. No one else has Satyavān''s virtues; give her to him.'' ''The sage''s word cannot be set aside,'' said Aśvapati; ''so be it.'' Nārada blessed them, rose into the sky and returned to heaven. Aśvapati had hoped to change his daughter''s mind, but after these words he knew further argument was useless. The year began to run from that day.','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.05','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十二节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.6','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',6,'林中的婚姻','A marriage in the forest','马主王选了一个吉日，带着婆罗门和祭司，用车载着结婚的用品，带着莎维德丽来到迪尤摩特舍那所住的净修林。他看见那位盲眼的国王坐在一棵娑罗树下的草垫上，就上前行礼，说明了来意。迪尤摩特舍那说：我们失去了王国，住在森林里，过着苦行者的生活，你的女儿怎能忍受这里的艰苦？马主王说：我的女儿和我都知道幸福和苦难是会来去的，她不会在乎这些。我满怀希望而来，请不要拒绝我。迪尤摩特舍那答应了。于是在净修林里，当着所有婆罗门的面，莎维德丽和萨谛梵举行了婚礼。父亲走后，莎维德丽脱下了华丽的衣服和首饰，换上了树皮衣和红褐色的布衣，像苦行者一样生活。她侍奉公婆，温柔谦恭，节制言语，用她的品德让萨谛梵和他的父母都满心欢喜。','On an auspicious day Aśvapati, with brahmins and priests and chariots laden with things for the wedding, brought Sāvitrī to the hermitage where Dyumatsena lived. Finding the blind king seated on a mat of grass beneath a sal tree, he bowed and made known his purpose. Dyumatsena said: ''We have lost our kingdom and live in the forest as ascetics; how could your daughter bear the hardships here?'' Aśvapati answered: ''My daughter and I know that happiness and misery come and go; she will not mind them. I have come full of hope; do not refuse me.'' Dyumatsena agreed, and in the hermitage, before all the brahmins, Sāvitrī and Satyavān were married. When her father had gone, Sāvitrī laid aside her fine clothes and ornaments and put on bark and a garment of reddish-brown cloth, living as an ascetic. She served her husband''s parents, gentle and modest and sparing of speech, and her virtues filled Satyavān and his parents with joy.','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.06','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十三节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.7','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',7,'三夜的斋戒','The three-night fast','日子一天天过去，在快乐的净修林生活里，莎维德丽的心中却始终压着那罗陀的话，日日夜夜地数着日子。她没有把这件事告诉任何人。终于，离萨谛梵的死期只剩下四天了。莎维德丽决定守“三夜之誓”：三天三夜不吃不喝，站立着守戒。迪尤摩特舍那听说了，忧心地对她说：公主，你立下的誓太艰苦了，三夜不吃不喝，实在难以做到。莎维德丽说：父亲，不要担心，我一定能守完这个誓言，这是我用决心立下的。迪尤摩特舍那说：我不能叫你破誓，我只能说：愿你完成它。莎维德丽一动不动地站着，像一根木头一样，度过了三天三夜。到了第四天，就是萨谛梵要死去的那一天。她想：今天就是那一天了。她清早起来，献了供，向年长的婆罗门们和公婆一一行礼，接受他们“愿你永不守寡”的祝福，默默地在心里接受了这句话。','The days went by in the happy hermitage life, but Nārada''s words weighed on Sāvitrī''s heart, and day and night she counted the days, telling no one. At last only four days remained before Satyavān''s death. Sāvitrī resolved to keep the three-night vow: three days and nights without food or drink, standing. Hearing of it, Dyumatsena said anxiously: ''Princess, this vow is too hard; three nights without food or drink is almost impossible.'' ''Do not fear, father,'' she said; ''I will keep this vow; it is made with resolve.'' Dyumatsena said: ''I cannot tell you to break your vow; I can only say, may you fulfil it.'' Sāvitrī stood motionless as a post through three days and nights. The fourth day was the day Satyavān was to die. ''Today is the day,'' she thought. She rose early, made her offerings, bowed to the elder brahmins and to her husband''s parents, and received their blessing, may you never be a widow — which she silently accepted in her heart.','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.07','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十四节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.8','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',8,'最后一天','The last day','那一天，萨谛梵扛起斧头，要到林中去砍柴、采果子。莎维德丽说：今天我也跟你一起去，我不愿意离开你。萨谛梵说：你从来没有进过森林，林中的路难走，你又斋戒了三天，身体虚弱，怎么能走路呢？莎维德丽说：斋戒并没有让我疲倦，我很想去，请你不要阻止我。萨谛梵说：那你就去问问我的父母，他们同意了，我就不会怪你。莎维德丽去请求公婆，说：我的丈夫要进林去采果子，我想跟他一起去。我嫁过来已经快一年了，还从来没有看过林中开花的景象。公婆答应了。于是莎维德丽跟着丈夫走进了森林。萨谛梵一路指给她看孔雀，看清澈的溪流，看开满鲜花的树木，莎维德丽微笑着应答，心里却像被撕裂了一样，时时刻刻注视着丈夫，想着那罗陀的话，觉得他此刻就已经是一个将死的人。','That day Satyavān shouldered his axe to go into the forest for wood and fruit. ''I will come with you today,'' said Sāvitrī; ''I do not want to leave you.'' ''You have never been into the forest,'' said Satyavān; ''the paths are hard, and you are weak from three days of fasting; how can you walk?'' ''Fasting has not wearied me,'' she said; ''I long to go; do not stop me.'' ''Then ask my parents,'' said he; ''if they agree, I will not blame you.'' She went to them and said: ''My husband is going into the forest for fruit, and I wish to go with him. I have been married almost a year and have never seen the forest in flower.'' They gave their consent. So Sāvitrī went into the forest with her husband. All along the way Satyavān pointed out the peacocks, the clear streams and the trees in blossom, and Sāvitrī answered with a smile, while her heart was torn, her eyes always on her husband, thinking of Nārada''s words and seeing him already as a man about to die.','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.08','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十四节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.9','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',9,'阎摩的绳索','Yama''s noose','萨谛梵采了果子，装满了篮子，又开始砍柴。砍着砍着，他浑身冒汗，头痛得厉害，对莎维德丽说：我砍柴砍得头痛，四肢也痛，心里像被火烧一样，我好像病了，站也站不住，我想睡一会儿。莎维德丽走过去，坐在地上，把丈夫的头放在自己的膝上。她知道，那罗陀所说的时刻已经到了。就在这时，她看见一个人站在面前：他穿着红衣，头戴王冠，身躯高大，光芒像太阳一样耀眼，皮肤黝黑，眼睛发红，手里拿着一根绳索，样子令人畏惧。他站在萨谛梵身旁，注视着他。莎维德丽轻轻放下丈夫的头，站起来，双手合十，颤抖着问：您是哪一位天神？来做什么？那人说：莎维德丽，你忠于丈夫，又有苦行的功德，所以我才和你说话。我是阎摩。你丈夫的寿命已经到了，我要把他带走。他是有德之人，所以我亲自来，没有派我的使者。说完，阎摩从萨谛梵的身体里，用绳索拉出一个拇指大小的灵魂，带着他向南方走去。','Satyavān gathered fruit and filled his basket, then began to cut wood. As he worked he broke into a sweat and his head ached sorely. ''Cutting wood has given me a headache,'' he said to Sāvitrī; ''my limbs ache and my heart burns; I feel ill and cannot stand; I want to sleep.'' Sāvitrī sat down on the ground and took her husband''s head into her lap. She knew the hour Nārada had spoken of had come. Then she saw a figure standing before her, clad in red, crowned, tall, radiant as the sun, dark of skin and red of eye, holding a noose, fearful to look upon. He stood beside Satyavān, gazing at him. Sāvitrī gently laid her husband''s head down, rose, joined her palms and asked trembling: ''Which god are you, and why have you come?'' He said: ''Sāvitrī, you are devoted to your husband and rich in austerity, so I speak to you. I am Yama. Your husband''s span is ended and I must take him. Because he is virtuous I have come myself and not sent my messengers.'' Then with his noose Yama drew from Satyavān''s body a soul the size of a thumb, and set off with it towards the south.','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.09','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十五节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.10','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',10,'跟随死神','Following death','莎维德丽悲伤地跟在阎摩身后走去。阎摩回过头说：回去吧，莎维德丽，去为他举行葬礼。你已经尽了妻子的责任，你能走到的地方已经到了尽头。莎维德丽说：我的丈夫被带到哪里，我就该跟到哪里，这是永恒的正法。凭着我的苦行、我对长辈的敬重、我对丈夫的爱、我的守誓，以及你的恩典，我的路不会被阻挡。智者说，同行七步，就成了朋友。凭着这份友谊，请听我说几句话。接着，她谈起了自制、守法与善人之道。阎摩听了很高兴，说：你说得好，你可以向我求一个恩惠，除了萨谛梵的生命。莎维德丽说：我的公公双目失明，被逐出王国，住在林中。请让他恢复视力，变得像太阳一样强壮。阎摩说：我答应你。现在回去吧，你走得太累了。莎维德丽说：跟在丈夫身边，我怎么会累？丈夫去哪里，我就去哪里。','Sorrowing, Sāvitrī followed Yama. He turned and said: ''Go back, Sāvitrī, and perform his funeral rites. You have done your duty as a wife; you have come as far as you may.'' Sāvitrī said: ''Wherever my husband is taken, there I must go; that is the eternal dharma. By my austerity, my reverence for elders, my love for my husband, my vows and your grace, my way cannot be barred. The wise say that seven steps taken together make friends. By that friendship, hear a few words of mine.'' And she spoke of self-restraint, of dharma and of the way of the good. Pleased, Yama said: ''You speak well. Ask me a boon — anything except Satyavān''s life.'' Sāvitrī said: ''My father-in-law is blind and exiled from his kingdom, living in the forest. Let him regain his sight and become strong as the sun.'' ''I grant it,'' said Yama. ''Now go back; you are weary from walking.'' Sāvitrī said: ''How can I be weary at my husband''s side? Where my husband goes, there I go.''','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.10','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十五节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.11','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',11,'五个恩惠','Five boons','莎维德丽一边走，一边继续说话：与善人相处，哪怕只有一次，也是值得追求的；和善人交朋友，比什么都可贵，所以人应该和善人在一起。阎摩说：你的话对智者也有益处，再求一个恩惠吧，除了萨谛梵的生命。莎维德丽说：请让我的公公重新得到失去的王国，不再偏离正法。阎摩答应了，又让她回去。她又说起了正法与慈悲，阎摩又赐她第三个恩惠：她求父亲马主王得到一百个儿子，延续家族。阎摩答应了，说：你已经走得太远了，回去吧。她又赞美阎摩是正法之王，以公正对待众生，阎摩被打动了，说：你说出的话是我从未听过的，求第四个恩惠吧，除了萨谛梵的生命。莎维德丽说：请让我和萨谛梵生下一百个儿子，强壮而勇敢，延续我们两家的血脉。阎摩说：我答应你，你会有一百个儿子。莎维德丽说：正法之王啊，你已经许诺我会和萨谛梵生下一百个儿子，可是没有萨谛梵，这个恩惠怎能实现？我不求别的，只求萨谛梵活过来，否则我宁愿像死了一样。阎摩终于说：好吧，我解开绳索，把你的丈夫还给你。','As she walked Sāvitrī spoke on: ''To meet the good even once is to be sought; friendship with the good is above all things; so one should keep company with the good.'' ''Your words profit even the wise,'' said Yama; ''ask a second boon, except Satyavān''s life.'' ''Let my father-in-law regain his lost kingdom and never stray from dharma.'' Yama granted it and again bade her return. She spoke of dharma and compassion, and he gave her a third boon: she asked that her father Aśvapati might have a hundred sons to continue his line. ''Granted,'' said Yama; ''you have come too far; go back.'' She praised him as the king of dharma who deals justly with all beings, and Yama, moved, said: ''Such words I have never heard; ask a fourth boon, except Satyavān''s life.'' Sāvitrī said: ''Let Satyavān and me have a hundred sons, strong and brave, to continue both our lines.'' ''Granted; you shall have a hundred sons.'' Then Sāvitrī said: ''King of dharma, you have promised me a hundred sons by Satyavān; how can that boon be fulfilled without Satyavān? I ask nothing else — only that Satyavān live; without him I am as one dead.'' At last Yama said: ''So be it. I loosen the noose and give you back your husband.''','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.11','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十五节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.12','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',12,'黑夜里的归途','The way home through the night','阎摩解开了绳索，带着灵魂离去，并告诉莎维德丽，萨谛梵将活四百年，与她一起享受幸福。莎维德丽回到丈夫躺着的地方，坐在地上，又把他的头放在膝上。萨谛梵醒了过来，像一个远行归来的人，深情地看着妻子，说：我睡了好久，你为什么不叫醒我？那个拉着我的黑色的人去了哪里？莎维德丽说：你在我的膝上睡了很久，那位神就是阎摩，他已经走了。天已经黑了，明天我再把一切告诉你。萨谛梵站起来，看见四周一片漆黑，说：我的父母一定在为我担心，他们年老体弱，我晚回去一会儿，他们就会焦急万分。他一想到父母，就哭了起来。莎维德丽替他擦干眼泪，扶着他，把他的斧头扛在自己肩上，把装满果子的篮子挂在树枝上，两人借着星光，在黑夜的森林里摸索着往回走。','Yama loosened the noose and departed, telling Sāvitrī that Satyavān would live four hundred years and share happiness with her. Sāvitrī went back to where her husband lay, sat down and took his head into her lap again. Satyavān woke like one returned from a long journey, looked lovingly at his wife and said: ''I have slept a long time; why did you not wake me? Where is that dark man who was dragging me away?'' ''You slept long in my lap,'' said Sāvitrī; ''that god was Yama, and he has gone. It is night now; tomorrow I will tell you everything.'' Satyavān rose and saw darkness all around. ''My parents must be anxious,'' he said; ''they are old and frail, and if I am even a little late they grow frantic.'' Thinking of them, he wept. Sāvitrī dried his tears and supported him, put his axe on her own shoulder and hung the basket of fruit on a branch, and the two felt their way home through the dark forest by starlight.','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.12','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十六节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_savitri.13','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_savitri',13,'净修林中的光明','Light in the hermitage','就在那天，盲王迪尤摩特舍那忽然重见了光明。他和妻子却因为儿子和媳妇迟迟未归而焦急万分，到处寻找，在净修林附近的河边和树林里奔走呼唤。林中的婆罗门们安慰他们说：莎维德丽守过苦行，品德高尚，她的丈夫一定活着；你重见光明，正是吉祥的征兆。萨谛梵和莎维德丽终于在夜里回到了净修林，众人围坐在火边，问他们为什么回来得这么晚。萨谛梵说他在林中头痛睡着了。婆罗门们说：你父亲突然复明，其中的缘故你并不知道，莎维德丽应该告诉我们。莎维德丽就把那罗陀的预言、三夜的斋戒、阎摩的到来和五个恩惠一一说了出来。第二天早晨，沙尔瓦国的大臣们来到净修林，报告说：篡位的人已经被他的大臣杀死，百姓请迪尤摩特舍那回去做国王。迪尤摩特舍那回到了王国，后来马主王果然有了一百个儿子，莎维德丽也生下了一百个儿子。摩根德耶对坚战说：莎维德丽就是这样用她的德行救了自己、父母、公婆和丈夫，黑公主也会这样拯救你们。','That same day the blind king Dyumatsena suddenly regained his sight. But he and his wife were frantic because their son and daughter-in-law had not come home, and searched by the rivers and woods near the hermitage, calling. The brahmins of the forest comforted them: ''Sāvitrī is rich in austerity and virtue; her husband must be alive; your regaining your sight is an auspicious sign.'' At last, in the night, Satyavān and Sāvitrī reached the hermitage, and all sat round the fire and asked why they were so late. Satyavān said he had fallen asleep in the forest with a headache. The brahmins said: ''You do not know why your father has suddenly regained his sight; Sāvitrī should tell us.'' Then Sāvitrī told them of Nārada''s prophecy, her three-night fast, Yama''s coming and the five boons. The next morning ministers came from Śālva to say that the usurper had been killed by his own minister and the people asked Dyumatsena to return as king. Dyumatsena returned to his kingdom; in time Aśvapati had a hundred sons, and Sāvitrī bore a hundred sons. Mārkaṇḍeya said to Yudhiṣṭhira: ''So Sāvitrī by her virtue saved herself, her parents, her husband''s parents and her husband; so too will Draupadī save you.''','claim.v0350.mbh_savitri.13','《摩诃婆罗多·森林篇》贞妇颂第二百九十六至二百九十七节（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.1','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',1,'黑天的和平使命','Kṛṣṇa''s embassy of peace','般度五子在森林里过了十二年，又在毗罗吒国隐姓埋名地度过了第十三年，按照当年掷骰子时的约定，他们应该收回自己的王国。可是持国王的长子难敌不肯归还。为了避免一场亲族之间的大战，雅度族的王子黑天——他是般度五子的表兄弟，也是他们最信任的朋友——亲自乘车来到俱卢族的都城象城，为般度族斡旋。在宫廷大会上，黑天对持国王和众位长老说：战争会毁灭两族所有的人，请把王国的一半还给般度五子；如果不行，哪怕只给他们五个村庄也好。毗湿摩、德罗纳和维杜罗都劝难敌接受。难敌却说：只要我活着，连针尖大的一块土地，我也不会交给般度族。他甚至想把黑天抓起来，黑天显出了神的形象，满殿的人都惊呆了。和平的最后希望破灭了，黑天回去告诉般度五子：只有战争了。','The Pāṇḍavas had spent twelve years in the forest and a thirteenth in hiding at the court of Virāṭa; by the terms of the dice game they should now have their kingdom back. But Duryodhana, eldest son of King Dhṛtarāṣṭra, would not return it. To avert a war between kinsmen, Kṛṣṇa, prince of the Yādavas, the Pāṇḍavas'' cousin and most trusted friend, drove himself to the Kuru capital Hastināpura as their envoy. In the assembly he said to Dhṛtarāṣṭra and the elders: ''War will destroy everyone of both houses. Give the Pāṇḍavas back half the kingdom — or if not, even five villages.'' Bhīṣma, Droṇa and Vidura all urged Duryodhana to accept. But Duryodhana said: ''While I live, I will not give the Pāṇḍavas even as much land as the point of a needle can pierce.'' He even thought to seize Kṛṣṇa, who then showed his divine form, and the whole hall was struck with awe. The last hope of peace was gone; Kṛṣṇa went back and told the Pāṇḍavas that there would be war.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.01','《摩诃婆罗多·斡旋篇》黑天出使分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.2','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',2,'战车上的阿周那','Arjuna on the chariot','两军在俱卢之野——一片被称为“正法之野”的平原——上摆开了阵势：般度族七支大军，俱卢族十一支大军，旌旗蔽日，战鼓和螺号响彻天地。阿周那请黑天为他驾车，因为黑天发誓在战争中不拿武器，只做阿周那的车夫。阿周那让黑天把战车驶到两军之间，他放眼望去，对面站着的是他的祖父毗湿摩、他的老师德罗纳、他的堂兄弟、叔伯和朋友。他的手脚发软，神箭甘狄拨从手中滑落，他说：黑天啊，杀死自己的亲人，就算得到王国又有什么意义？我不愿意作战。他坐在车上，满心悲伤。于是黑天在两军之间开始对他说话，讲述行动与责任、灵魂的不朽、对神的奉献，这番对话就是后来被单独传诵的《薄伽梵歌》。阿周那听完，心中的迷惑消散了，重新拿起了弓。《薄伽梵歌》共十八章，后来成为印度最受尊崇的经典之一，至今仍被无数人诵读；本篇只讲它发生的场景，不转述它的教义。','The armies drew up on the field of Kurukṣetra, the plain called the field of dharma: seven armies of the Pāṇḍavas, eleven of the Kauravas, banners darkening the sun, drums and conches shaking heaven and earth. Arjuna asked Kṛṣṇa to drive his chariot, for Kṛṣṇa had vowed to take up no weapon in the war but to serve as Arjuna''s charioteer. Arjuna had Kṛṣṇa drive between the two armies and looked out: facing him stood his grandsire Bhīṣma, his teacher Droṇa, his cousins, uncles and friends. His limbs grew weak, the bow Gāṇḍīva slipped from his hand, and he said: ''Kṛṣṇa, what is a kingdom worth, won by killing my own kin? I will not fight.'' He sat down in the chariot, overwhelmed with grief. Then, between the two armies, Kṛṣṇa began to speak to him — of action and duty, of the undying self, of devotion to God — and this dialogue is what was later recited on its own as the Bhagavad Gītā. When Arjuna had heard it his confusion was gone, and he took up his bow again. The Gītā''s eighteen chapters became one of the most revered scriptures of India, recited by countless people to this day; this retelling gives only its setting, not its teaching.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.02','《摩诃婆罗多·毗湿摩篇》薄伽梵歌分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.3','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',3,'毗湿摩的箭床','Bhīṣma''s bed of arrows','俱卢族的第一位统帅是年老的毗湿摩。他是恒河女神的儿子，年轻时为了父亲发誓终身不娶、不争王位，因此得到了可以自己选择死期的恩惠。他一连九天在战场上所向无敌，每天杀死成千上万的士兵，般度族无法抵挡。第九天夜里，般度五子来到毗湿摩的营帐，向他请教怎样才能战胜他。毗湿摩说：我决不会向一个曾经是女子的人射箭。你们让束发站在阿周那的前面吧。第十天，束发站在阿周那的战车前面，毗湿摩看见他，放下了弓，不再还击。阿周那从束发身后放箭，箭如雨下，射满了毗湿摩的全身。毗湿摩从车上倒下，身体却被无数的箭撑着，没有碰到地面，就像躺在一张箭做的床上。他的头垂了下来，阿周那用三支箭为他做了一个枕头；他口渴，阿周那一箭射入地下，一股清泉喷涌而出。当时太阳正在向南运行，毗湿摩决定等太阳转向北行时再死去，就这样躺在箭床上，看着战争继续。','The first commander of the Kauravas was old Bhīṣma, son of the river goddess Gaṅgā, who in his youth had vowed for his father''s sake never to marry or claim the throne, and had been granted the boon of choosing the hour of his own death. For nine days he was unconquerable, slaying thousands each day, and the Pāṇḍavas could not withstand him. On the ninth night the Pāṇḍavas went to his tent and asked him how he could be overcome. Bhīṣma said: ''I will never shoot at one who was once a woman. Let Śikhaṇḍin stand before Arjuna.'' On the tenth day Śikhaṇḍin stood in front of Arjuna''s chariot; seeing him, Bhīṣma lowered his bow and would not strike back. From behind Śikhaṇḍin Arjuna loosed a rain of arrows that covered Bhīṣma''s whole body. He fell from his chariot, but so many arrows held him up that he did not touch the ground, as if lying on a bed of arrows. His head hung down, and Arjuna made him a pillow of three arrows; he thirsted, and Arjuna shot an arrow into the earth and a spring of clear water gushed up. The sun was then in its southern course, and Bhīṣma resolved to wait for its turn to the north before he died, lying on his bed of arrows and watching the war go on.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.03','《摩诃婆罗多·毗湿摩篇》毗湿摩之死分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.4','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',4,'激昂与轮阵','Abhimanyu and the circular array','毗湿摩倒下以后，德罗纳做了俱卢族的统帅。第十三天，德罗纳摆出了一个可怕的轮形阵，一层又一层的战士像车轮一样旋转。那一天，阿周那被引到了战场的另一头，般度族中只有他知道怎样攻破轮阵。阿周那的儿子激昂只有十六岁，他对坚战说：我在母亲腹中时，听父亲讲过怎样冲进轮阵，可是没有听到怎样从里面出来。坚战说：你冲进去打开缺口，我们随后跟进来保护你。激昂驾车冲进了轮阵，一路杀伤无数敌人。可是信度国王胜车凭着湿婆赐给他的恩惠，在阵口挡住了跟进的般度族众将，激昂一个人被困在了阵中。德罗纳、迦尔纳、马嘶等六位大将一起围攻他，射断了他的弓，杀死了他的马和车夫。激昂拿起车轮当武器继续战斗，最后被敌人的杵击中，倒在了战场上。按照战场的规矩，许多人围攻一个人本是不正当的，这件事让般度族悲愤不已。','After Bhīṣma fell, Droṇa took command of the Kauravas. On the thirteenth day he formed a terrible circular array, rank upon rank of warriors wheeling like a chakra. That day Arjuna had been drawn away to the far end of the field, and of the Pāṇḍavas only he knew how to break the array. His son Abhimanyu, only sixteen, said to Yudhiṣṭhira: ''While I was in my mother''s womb I heard my father tell how to break into the circular array, but I did not hear how to come out.'' ''Break in and open a gap,'' said Yudhiṣṭhira, ''and we will follow and protect you.'' Abhimanyu drove into the array, cutting down countless enemies. But Jayadratha, king of the Sindhus, by a boon from Śiva, held the following Pāṇḍava chiefs back at the breach, and Abhimanyu was trapped inside alone. Droṇa, Karṇa, Aśvatthāman and other great warriors, six together, surrounded him, cut his bow, and killed his horses and charioteer. He fought on with a chariot wheel as his weapon until he was struck down by a mace. By the rules of battle many attacking one was unjust, and the Pāṇḍavas were filled with grief and rage.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.04','《摩诃婆罗多·德罗纳篇》激昂之死分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.5','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',5,'阿周那的誓言','Arjuna''s vow','阿周那回到营地，得知儿子战死，又听说是胜车在阵口挡住了救兵，悲痛欲绝，当众立下誓言：明天日落之前，我一定要杀死胜车；如果做不到，我就投火自焚。消息传到俱卢族营中，胜车吓得想要逃走，德罗纳答应保护他，把他藏在大军的最后面，前面布下了重重阵势。第十四天，阿周那和黑天驾车冲进敌阵，一层层杀过去，可是胜车被保护得严严实实，太阳却一点点西沉。眼看就要日落，黑天用他的神通让一片黑暗遮住了太阳，俱卢族的人以为太阳已经落山，阿周那就要投火了，都欢呼起来，胜车也放松了警惕，从藏身之处探出头来张望。黑天说：阿周那，太阳还没有落下，胜车就在那里！阿周那一箭射去，射下了胜车的头，又用一连串的箭把那颗头送到了远处胜车的父亲的膝上——因为这位老国王曾诅咒说，谁让他儿子的头落在地上，谁的头就会裂成碎片。','When Arjuna returned to camp and learned that his son had fallen and that Jayadratha had held back the rescuers at the breach, he was beside himself with grief and vowed before all: ''Before sunset tomorrow I will kill Jayadratha; if I fail, I will enter the fire.'' When the news reached the Kauravas, Jayadratha in terror wanted to flee, but Droṇa promised to protect him and hid him at the very rear of the host behind array upon array. On the fourteenth day Arjuna and Kṛṣṇa drove into the enemy and cut their way through rank after rank, but Jayadratha was closely guarded and the sun sank lower and lower. When sunset was near, Kṛṣṇa by his power covered the sun with darkness; the Kauravas, thinking the sun had set and Arjuna must burn himself, cheered, and Jayadratha, off his guard, raised his head from his hiding-place to look. ''Arjuna, the sun has not set — there is Jayadratha!'' said Kṛṣṇa. Arjuna''s arrow struck off Jayadratha''s head, and with a stream of further arrows he carried it far away into the lap of Jayadratha''s father — for the old king had cursed that whoever made his son''s head fall to the ground would have his own head burst into pieces.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.05','《摩诃婆罗多·德罗纳篇》立誓与胜车之死分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.6','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',6,'瓶首之死','The death of Ghaṭotkaca','那一夜，战斗没有停止，两军举着火把在黑暗中继续厮杀。怖军的儿子瓶首是一个罗刹，母亲是罗刹女希丁芭，他在夜里力量更大，会施展各种幻术：他变得像山一样高大，从空中降下石头和兵器，俱卢族的士兵成片地倒下，连难敌也惊慌失措，大声呼喊迦尔纳去救援。迦尔纳手中有一件神兵：因陀罗赐给他的一支神矛，只能使用一次，必定能杀死目标。迦尔纳一直把它留着，准备用来对付阿周那。可是在难敌和众人的哀求下，迦尔纳不得不向瓶首投出了这支神矛。神矛穿透了瓶首的胸膛，瓶首在倒下之前把身体变得巨大无比，压死了一大片俱卢族的士兵。般度族都为瓶首痛哭，只有黑天却高兴起来，说：迦尔纳的神矛已经用掉了，阿周那得救了。瓶首是怖军在森林流亡时与罗刹女希丁芭所生的儿子，在般度族危难时总会赶来相助，他的死让怖军悲痛万分。','That night the fighting did not stop; the armies fought on in the dark by torchlight. Bhīma''s son Ghaṭotkaca was a rākṣasa, born of the rākṣasī Hiḍimbā; his strength grew at night and he worked many illusions, growing tall as a mountain and raining rocks and weapons from the sky, so that Kaurava soldiers fell in swathes and even Duryodhana cried out in panic for Karṇa''s help. Karṇa held a divine weapon: a dart given him by Indra, usable only once, certain to kill its target. He had kept it all along for Arjuna. But begged by Duryodhana and the others, he was forced to hurl it at Ghaṭotkaca. The dart pierced Ghaṭotkaca''s chest, and before he fell he made his body vast and crushed a great swathe of Kaurava soldiers beneath it. The Pāṇḍavas wept for him; only Kṛṣṇa was glad, saying: ''Karṇa''s dart is spent; Arjuna is saved.'' Ghaṭotkaca, son of Bhīma by the rākṣasī Hiḍimbā during the years in the forest, had always come to the Pāṇḍavas'' aid in danger, and his death grieved Bhīma deeply.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.06','《摩诃婆罗多·德罗纳篇》瓶首之死分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.7','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',7,'马嘶死了','''Aśvatthāman is dead''','第十五天，德罗纳在战场上无人能敌，用神兵杀死了大批般度族的士兵。黑天说：只要德罗纳手里有武器，谁也打不败他。只有让他听到儿子马嘶的死讯，他才会放下武器。于是怖军用杵打死了一头也叫马嘶的大象，大声喊道：马嘶死了！德罗纳不相信，他知道坚战从不说谎，就问坚战：真的吗？坚战心中挣扎，最后高声说：马嘶死了——然后低声补了一句：是那头大象。后半句被战场上的喧嚣淹没了。据说坚战的战车一向离地四指浮行，从这一刻起，它落到了地上。德罗纳听了，悲痛万分，放下了武器，坐在车上进入了冥想。般遮罗王子猛光冲上车去，砍下了这位老师的头。一句半真半假的话结束了德罗纳的生命，也在坚战的一生中留下了无法抹去的污点。这一节在后世引起了许多讨论：为了赢得战争，一个从不说谎的人说了半句真话，这样做是否合乎正法，书中并没有给出简单的答案。','On the fifteenth day Droṇa was invincible, slaying hosts of Pāṇḍava soldiers with his divine weapons. Kṛṣṇa said: ''While Droṇa holds his weapons no one can defeat him. Only news of his son Aśvatthāman''s death will make him lay them down.'' So Bhīma killed with his mace an elephant also named Aśvatthāman and shouted: ''Aśvatthāman is dead!'' Droṇa did not believe it; knowing Yudhiṣṭhira never lied, he asked him: ''Is it true?'' Yudhiṣṭhira struggled within himself, then said aloud: ''Aśvatthāman is dead'' — and added under his breath, ''the elephant''. The last words were lost in the din of battle. Yudhiṣṭhira''s chariot, it is said, had always moved four fingers'' breadth above the ground; from that moment it touched the earth. Grief-stricken, Droṇa laid down his weapons and sat in meditation on his chariot. The Pāñcāla prince Dhṛṣṭadyumna sprang onto the chariot and struck off the teacher''s head. A half-truth ended Droṇa''s life and left on Yudhiṣṭhira a stain that would never be erased. Later readers have debated this scene much: a man who never lied spoke half a truth to win a war, and whether that accorded with dharma the text does not answer simply.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.07','《摩诃婆罗多·德罗纳篇》德罗纳之死分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.8','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',8,'迦尔纳的车轮','Karṇa''s chariot wheel','德罗纳死后，迦尔纳做了俱卢族的统帅。他其实是贡蒂婚前与太阳神所生的长子，是般度五子的哥哥，被母亲放在河里漂走，由一个车夫抚养长大；战前黑天和贡蒂都把这个秘密告诉了他，可他宁愿忠于从小善待他的朋友难敌。第十七天，迦尔纳和阿周那终于在战场上相遇，两人射出的箭遮蔽了天空，众神都在空中观战。激战之中，迦尔纳的战车左轮陷进了泥土里——这是一个婆罗门曾经对他发出的诅咒。迦尔纳跳下车去，用双手拼命去抬车轮，对阿周那说：请你等一等，按照武士的正法，不应该攻击一个没有战车的人。黑天说：迦尔纳，你们在大殿上羞辱黑公主的时候，你们许多人围杀激昂的时候，正法在哪里？阿周那拉满了弓，一箭射下了迦尔纳的头。迦尔纳倒下时，一道光从他的身体里升起，回到了太阳之中。','After Droṇa''s death Karṇa took command of the Kauravas. He was in truth the eldest son of Kuntī, born to her by the sun god before her marriage — elder brother of the Pāṇḍavas — set adrift on a river by his mother and raised by a charioteer. Before the war Kṛṣṇa and Kuntī had both told him the secret, but he chose to stay loyal to Duryodhana, the friend who had been good to him from his youth. On the seventeenth day Karṇa and Arjuna met at last, their arrows darkening the sky while the gods watched from above. In the thick of the fight the left wheel of Karṇa''s chariot sank into the earth — the working of a brahmin''s curse upon him. Karṇa leapt down and strained with both hands to lift the wheel, crying to Arjuna: ''Wait! By the warrior''s dharma one does not strike a man without his chariot.'' Kṛṣṇa said: ''Karṇa, where was dharma when you humiliated Draupadī in the hall, when many of you together slew Abhimanyu?'' Arjuna drew his bow and with one arrow struck off Karṇa''s head. As Karṇa fell, a light rose from his body and returned into the sun.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.08','《摩诃婆罗多·迦尔纳篇》（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.9','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',9,'难敌藏身湖中','Duryodhana hides in the lake','第十八天，沙利耶做了俱卢族最后一位统帅，被坚战亲手杀死；那个在掷骰时设下骗局的沙恭尼，也被般度五子中最小的偶天杀死。俱卢族的大军彻底崩溃了。难敌看见兄弟和朋友全都战死，独自拿着他的杵逃离了战场，走到一个湖边，用幻术把湖水凝住，藏进了湖底。般度五子带着黑天循着猎人的指点找到了湖边。坚战对着湖水喊道：难敌，你让那么多人为你战死，自己却躲在水里，这是一个刹帝利应该做的吗？出来战斗吧！难敌在水下说：我已经失去了一切，你们拿走这个王国吧，我要到森林里去。坚战说：我们不接受你施舍的王国。你出来，选我们五人中的任何一个，用任何一种武器和他单独决斗；如果你赢了，王国就是你的。难敌听了，从湖里站了起来，扛着铁杵，说要用杵决斗；最后上前迎战的是怖军——他早就发誓要亲手打倒难敌。沙恭尼是难敌的舅舅，当年那场骗走般度五子王国的掷骰子，就是由他替难敌掷的。','On the eighteenth day Śalya, last commander of the Kauravas, was slain by Yudhiṣṭhira himself, and Śakuni, who had rigged the dice, was slain by Sahadeva, youngest of the Pāṇḍavas. The Kaurava host collapsed. Seeing his brothers and friends all dead, Duryodhana fled the field alone with his mace, came to a lake, solidified its waters by his magic and hid beneath them. Guided by some hunters, the Pāṇḍavas and Kṛṣṇa found the lake. Yudhiṣṭhira called over the water: ''Duryodhana, you sent so many to die for you and hide yourself in the water — is that fit for a kṣatriya? Come out and fight!'' From under the water Duryodhana said: ''I have lost everything. Take the kingdom; I will go to the forest.'' Yudhiṣṭhira said: ''We will not take a kingdom as your gift. Come out and choose any one of us five, with any weapon, in single combat; if you win, the kingdom is yours.'' At that Duryodhana rose from the lake with his iron mace on his shoulder and called for a mace fight; the one who stepped forward to meet him was Bhīma, who had long vowed to strike him down with his own hands. Śakuni was Duryodhana''s maternal uncle; in the dice game that cheated the Pāṇḍavas of their kingdom it was he who threw for Duryodhana.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.09','《摩诃婆罗多·沙利耶篇》入湖分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.10','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',10,'杵战','The mace duel','怖军和难敌拿着铁杵在湖边对战，两人的杵碰撞时迸出火花，声音像霹雳一样。黑天的哥哥大力罗摩恰好朝圣回来，他是两人共同的杵法老师，也来观战。难敌技艺更精，怖军力气更大，两人斗了很久，不分胜负。黑天对阿周那说：如果按规矩打，怖军赢不了。怖军曾经发过誓，要在战场上打碎难敌的大腿，因为当年在大殿上，难敌曾露出大腿，羞辱黑公主。阿周那就拍了拍自己的大腿，向怖军示意。怖军看见了，趁难敌跃起的时候，一杵打在他的大腿上，把大腿打碎了。难敌倒在地上。按照杵战的规矩，攻击腰部以下是不正当的，大力罗摩勃然大怒，举起犁头要责罚怖军，被黑天劝住了。难敌躺在地上，指责黑天用诡计赢得了战争。天上却降下花雨，落在这位倒下的国王身上。','Bhīma and Duryodhana fought with iron maces by the lake, the clash striking sparks and sounding like thunder. Kṛṣṇa''s elder brother Balarāma, returning from pilgrimage, came to watch; he had taught both men the mace. Duryodhana was the more skilful, Bhīma the stronger, and long they fought without either prevailing. Kṛṣṇa said to Arjuna: ''If he fights by the rules, Bhīma cannot win. But Bhīma vowed to break Duryodhana''s thigh in battle, because in the hall long ago Duryodhana bared his thigh to insult Draupadī.'' Arjuna slapped his own thigh as a sign to Bhīma. Bhīma saw it, and as Duryodhana leapt up he struck him on the thighs with his mace and shattered them. Duryodhana fell. By the rules of the mace, striking below the navel was forbidden; Balarāma in fury raised his ploughshare to punish Bhīma, and Kṛṣṇa calmed him. Lying on the ground, Duryodhana accused Kṛṣṇa of winning the war by trickery. Yet a rain of flowers fell from heaven upon the fallen king.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.10','《摩诃婆罗多·沙利耶篇》杵战分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.11','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',11,'夜袭','The night raid','俱卢族只剩下三个人还活着：德罗纳的儿子马嘶、慈悯和成铠。马嘶对父亲被骗杀、难敌被违规打倒满怀仇恨。夜里，三人躲在一棵大榕树下，马嘶睡不着，看见一只猫头鹰在黑暗中悄悄飞来，把栖在树上熟睡的乌鸦一只只杀死。马嘶说：这只猫头鹰教会了我该怎么做。他不顾慈悯的劝阻，带着两人来到般度族的营地。成铠和慈悯守在营门，马嘶独自潜入营帐。他先找到杀死自己父亲的猛光，不等他起身拿起武器，就把他活活踩死，然后又杀死了束发，杀死了黑公主熟睡的五个儿子，还杀死了许多尚在睡梦中的将士。般度五子和黑天那天夜里恰好不在营中，才逃过了这场劫难。天亮前，马嘶赶到垂死的难敌身边，告诉他这个消息。难敌听了，说了一句“我终于满意了”，就闭上了眼睛。这场夜袭违背了一切作战的规矩，它让胜利的般度族在一夜之间失去了几乎所有的儿子。','Of the Kauravas only three remained alive: Droṇa''s son Aśvatthāman, Kṛpa and Kṛtavarman. Aśvatthāman burned with hatred for the trickery that had killed his father and felled Duryodhana. That night the three lay under a great banyan tree; Aśvatthāman could not sleep, and saw an owl fly silently out of the dark and kill, one by one, the crows sleeping in the tree. ''This owl has taught me what to do,'' he said. Ignoring Kṛpa''s pleading, he led the other two to the Pāṇḍava camp. While Kṛtavarman and Kṛpa guarded the gate, Aśvatthāman went in alone. First he found Dhṛṣṭadyumna, who had slain his father, and trampled him to death before he could rise and take up a weapon; then he killed Śikhaṇḍin, and Draupadī''s five sleeping sons, and many warriors still asleep. The five Pāṇḍavas and Kṛṣṇa happened to be away from the camp that night and so escaped. Before dawn Aśvatthāman hurried to the dying Duryodhana and told him. Duryodhana said, ''At last I am content,'' and closed his eyes. The raid broke every rule of war, and in a single night it took from the victorious Pāṇḍavas nearly all their sons.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.11','《摩诃婆罗多·夜袭篇》（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.hindu_mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra.12','storyver.hindu.mahabharata_ganguli_kurukshetra',12,'战后的寂静','The silence after the war','第二天清晨，黑公主得知五个儿子都被杀死，悲痛欲绝，发誓说：如果马嘶不受惩罚，我就绝食而死。怖军和众人追上了躲在恒河边仙人身边的马嘶。马嘶走投无路，放出了毁天灭地的神兵“梵头”，阿周那也放出神兵抵挡，两件神兵在空中相撞，众仙人急忙站到中间，请他们收回。阿周那收回了自己的神兵，马嘶却不能收回，只好把它引向般度族未出生的后代。黑天大怒，诅咒马嘶在世间流浪三千年，身上流脓带血，无人理睬，孤独无依。马嘶交出了他额上那颗与生俱来、能护身的宝珠，独自离去。黑公主拿到宝珠，把它交给坚战戴上，这才平息了悲伤。十八天的战争结束了，两军十八支大军，最后只剩下般度五子、黑天、萨谛奇，以及俱卢族的马嘶、慈悯和成铠。胜利的坚战站在尸横遍野的俱卢之野上，心中没有一点喜悦。','The next morning Draupadī learned that all five of her sons were dead and, beside herself with grief, vowed to fast to death unless Aśvatthāman were punished. Bhīma and the others overtook Aśvatthāman where he sat among sages by the Ganges. Cornered, he loosed the world-destroying weapon Brahmaśiras; Arjuna loosed his own to meet it, and as the two clashed in the sky the sages stepped between them and begged them to withdraw. Arjuna withdrew his weapon, but Aśvatthāman could not, and turned his against the unborn heirs of the Pāṇḍavas. Kṛṣṇa in anger cursed him to wander the earth for three thousand years, oozing pus and blood, shunned and alone. Aśvatthāman gave up the protective jewel he was born with on his forehead and went away alone. Draupadī received the jewel and gave it to Yudhiṣṭhira to wear, and only then was her grief stilled. The eighteen days of war were over; of the eighteen armies there remained only the five Pāṇḍavas, Kṛṣṇa and Sātyaki, and of the Kauravas Aśvatthāman, Kṛpa and Kṛtavarman. Victorious, Yudhiṣṭhira stood on the field of Kurukṣetra strewn with the dead, and felt no joy.','claim.v0350.mbh_kurukshetra.12','《摩诃婆罗多·夜袭篇》芦苇分篇（甘古利译本）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.1','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',1,'白发之子','The white-haired son','在马努切赫尔国王统治伊朗的时候，扎布尔斯坦的领主萨姆是国王最英勇的勇士，他什么都不缺，只盼望有一个儿子。终于，他的妻子生下了一个男孩，孩子的脸像月亮一样美丽，身体健壮，可是满头的头发却像老人一样雪白。侍女们不敢告诉萨姆，过了好几天，才有一个侍女鼓起勇气去报喜。萨姆来看孩子，见他一头白发，心中又羞又怕，说：人们看见这个孩子，会说什么呢？他们会说这是魔鬼的孩子，会嘲笑我生了一个怪物。我怎么能把这样的儿子带到勇士们面前？他转过脸去，命令仆人把孩子抱走，丢到远离人烟的厄尔布尔士山上，让野兽去处置他。仆人们照办了，把这个刚出生的婴儿放在了荒凉的山顶上，任凭风吹日晒。《列王纪》是诗人菲尔多西在公元一千年前后写成的长篇史诗，讲述伊朗历代国王与英雄的事迹；萨姆、扎尔、鲁斯塔姆祖孙三代，是其中最有名的英雄家族，而这个家族的故事，就从一次抛弃开始。','When King Manūchehr ruled Iran, Sām, lord of Zābulistān, was the bravest of his champions; he lacked nothing except a son. At last his wife bore a boy, his face fair as the moon and his body strong, but his hair as white as an old man''s. The women dared not tell Sām, and it was days before one of them found the courage to bring him the news. Sām came to see the child, and at the sight of the white hair he was filled with shame and fear. ''What will people say when they see this child? They will say he is a demon''s child and mock me for fathering a monster. How can I bring such a son before the warriors?'' He turned away and commanded his servants to carry the child off and leave him on Mount Alborz, far from all human dwellings, for the beasts to deal with. The servants obeyed and left the newborn on the bare summit, exposed to wind and sun. The Shahnameh, the Book of Kings, is the long epic the poet Ferdowsi completed around the year 1000, telling of the kings and heroes of Iran; Sām, Zāl and Rostam, grandfather, father and son, are its most famous family of heroes — and their story begins with an abandonment.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.01','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.2','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',2,'西摩格的巢','The Simorgh''s nest','厄尔布尔士山的高处，住着一只神鸟，名叫西摩格。它的巢筑在山巅，离星星很近，巢里有它嗷嗷待哺的雏鸟。西摩格飞出去为孩子们觅食，看见山坡上躺着一个婴儿，饿得哭泣，正在吮吸自己的手指。它俯冲下来，用爪子抓起婴儿，带回巢中，本想把他喂给雏鸟。可是当它把婴儿放在巢里，看着这个哭泣的孩子时，心中忽然生出了怜悯。它不仅没有伤害他，还把他和自己的孩子们一起抚养，给他最柔软的肉吃。就这样，扎尔在西摩格的巢里一天天长大，长成了一个高大英俊的青年。路过山下的商队常常抬头看见山上有一个人影，在神鸟的巢边走动，消息渐渐传遍了四方，说厄尔布尔士山上有一个由神鸟养大的年轻人。厄尔布尔士山在伊朗北部，是伊朗传说中最高、最神秘的山，西摩格就住在那里，被人们视为一只充满智慧的神鸟。','High on Mount Alborz lived a great bird called the Simorgh. Her nest was on the summit, close to the stars, and in it were her hungry young. Flying out to find food for them, she saw a baby lying on the slope, crying with hunger and sucking his fingers. She swooped down, caught him up in her claws and carried him to her nest, meaning to feed him to her young. But when she set the baby in the nest and looked at the weeping child, pity stirred in her heart. Far from harming him, she raised him with her own young, giving him the tenderest meat. So Zāl grew up day by day in the Simorgh''s nest into a tall and handsome youth. Caravans passing below often looked up and saw a figure moving about beside the great bird''s nest, and word spread far and wide that on Mount Alborz there lived a young man raised by the Simorgh. Mount Alborz in the north of Iran is the highest and most mysterious mountain of Iranian legend, and the Simorgh who lives there is held to be a bird of great wisdom.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.02','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.3','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',3,'萨姆的梦','Sām''s dream','有一天夜里，萨姆梦见一个骑着阿拉伯骏马的人从印度方向疾驰而来，告诉他：你的儿子还活着。萨姆醒来，把梦告诉了祭司们，问他们这是什么意思。祭司们说：连野兽和飞鸟都会照料自己的幼崽，你却抛弃了自己的儿子，只因为他有白头发。这是不义的，你应当去把他找回来。萨姆心中悔恨。第二天夜里，他又做了一个梦：一个英俊的青年举着旗帜，带着大军向他走来，一位贤者在旁边责备他说：你连自己的孩子都不要，又怎么配做勇士的首领？萨姆从梦中惊醒，再也坐不住了，带着随从赶到厄尔布尔士山下。他抬头望去，看见山顶上神鸟的巢，巢边站着一个青年。可是山峰陡峭，无路可上。萨姆跪在地上，向造物主祈祷，忏悔自己的罪过，求神让他找回儿子。一个曾经因为一头白发抛弃儿子的父亲，如今跪在山下，向造物主请求宽恕。','One night Sām dreamed that a rider on an Arab horse came galloping from the direction of India and told him: ''Your son is alive.'' He woke and told the priests his dream, asking what it meant. They said: ''Even the beasts and birds care for their young, yet you cast off your own son only because his hair was white. It was unjust; you must go and find him.'' Sām was filled with remorse. The next night he dreamed again: a handsome youth came towards him with a banner at the head of an army, and a wise man beside him rebuked Sām: ''You would not keep your own child; how are you fit to lead warriors?'' Sām woke with a start and could rest no longer; with his followers he hurried to the foot of Mount Alborz. Looking up, he saw the great bird''s nest on the summit and a youth standing beside it. But the peak was sheer, with no path to the top. Sām knelt and prayed to the Creator, confessing his sin and begging to be given back his son. A father who had cast off his son for his white hair now knelt at the foot of the mountain and begged the Creator''s forgiveness.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.03','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.4','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',4,'扎尔回到人间','Zāl returns to the world of men','西摩格看见山下的萨姆，知道他是来找儿子的。它对扎尔说：你的父亲来了，他是世上最伟大的勇士，你该回到他身边去，回到人间去了。扎尔流着泪说：难道你厌倦了我吗？你的巢就是我的家，你就是我的母亲。西摩格说：我并不是要赶你走，我只是想让你得到你应有的荣耀。我给你取名叫“达斯坦”。它从自己的胸前拔下一根羽毛，交给扎尔说：带着这根羽毛，如果有一天你遇到危难，就把它烧掉，我会立刻来到你身边。说完，它用翅膀托着扎尔飞下山峰，把他放在父亲面前。萨姆抱住儿子，哭着请求他原谅。他给儿子取名为扎尔，意思是“白发老人”。父子俩一同回到扎布尔斯坦，后来又到了马努切赫尔国王的宫廷，国王召来星象家为扎尔占卜，星象家说：这个年轻人将成为伊朗最伟大的勇士之一。','The Simorgh saw Sām below and knew he had come for his son. She said to Zāl: ''Your father has come, the greatest of warriors; you must go back to him and to the world of men.'' Zāl wept: ''Are you weary of me? Your nest is my home and you are my mother.'' The Simorgh said: ''I do not send you away; I want you to have the glory that is yours. I name you Dastān.'' She plucked a feather from her breast and gave it to him: ''Keep this feather. If ever you are in danger, burn it, and I will be with you at once.'' Then she bore Zāl down from the peak on her wings and set him before his father. Sām embraced his son and wept, begging his forgiveness, and named him Zāl, meaning the white-haired old one. Father and son returned to Zābulistān and later went to the court of King Manūchehr, who had his astrologers read the youth''s stars; they said he would be among the greatest champions of Iran.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.04','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.5','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',5,'喀布尔的公主','The princess of Kābul','萨姆奉国王之命出征，把扎布尔斯坦交给扎尔治理。扎尔四处巡游，来到了喀布尔。喀布尔的国王梅赫拉布出城迎接他，两人一见如故。梅赫拉布是一个英俊而有智慧的国王，可是他的祖先是那个肩上长着两条毒蛇、以人脑为食的暴君佐哈克。扎尔的随从中有人对他说：梅赫拉布有一个女儿，名叫鲁达贝，她比太阳还要美丽，头发像黑色的麝香一样垂到脚踝，身段像柏树一样挺拔。扎尔听了，心中就念念不忘。另一边，梅赫拉布回到宫中，在妻子辛多赫特和女儿面前不住地称赞扎尔，说他英俊、勇敢、谦和，只可惜头发是白的。鲁达贝听了，也悄悄爱上了这个从未见过面的年轻人。她把心事告诉了五个侍女，侍女们先是责备她，说他是被鸟养大的白发人，后来见她心意坚决，就答应帮她。','Sent by the king to war, Sām left Zābulistān in Zāl''s charge. Zāl travelled through the land and came to Kābul. Mehrāb, king of Kābul, rode out to meet him, and the two took to each other at once. Mehrāb was a handsome and wise king, but his ancestor was Zahhāk, the tyrant with two serpents growing from his shoulders who fed them on men''s brains. One of Zāl''s company told him: ''Mehrāb has a daughter named Rūdābeh, fairer than the sun, her hair dark as musk falling to her ankles, her figure straight as a cypress.'' From then on Zāl could think of nothing else. Meanwhile Mehrāb went home and praised Zāl again and again before his wife Sindokht and his daughter — handsome, brave and gentle, if only his hair were not white. Hearing it, Rūdābeh too secretly fell in love with the young man she had never seen. She confided in her five maidens; at first they scolded her — a white-haired man raised by a bird! — but seeing her resolved, they promised to help.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.05','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔与鲁达贝”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.6','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',6,'长发之绳','The rope of hair','鲁达贝的侍女们装作去采玫瑰，来到扎尔营地附近的河边。扎尔正好在那里射猎，一箭射下一只水鸟，鸟落在侍女们身边，扎尔的仆人过来拾鸟，侍女们就和他攀谈起来，把公主的美貌和心意都告诉了他。扎尔送给她们许多礼物，约定在夜里与公主相见。到了夜里，扎尔来到鲁达贝的宫殿下面，公主站在高高的塔楼上，月光照在她的脸上。两人隔着塔楼说了许多话。鲁达贝说：英雄啊，你一路走来辛苦了，我把我的头发放下来给你，你抓着它爬上来吧。她解开发辫，把又长又黑的头发从塔上垂了下来。扎尔亲吻着她的头发，说：我怎么能让我心爱的人受这样的痛苦？他取出套索，抛上塔楼的垛口，攀着绳索爬了上去。两人相对而坐，倾诉衷情，彼此立下誓言：除了对方，谁也不嫁、谁也不娶。直到天快亮了，扎尔才依依不舍地离开。','Rūdābeh''s maidens went out as if to gather roses by the river near Zāl''s camp. Zāl happened to be hunting there and brought down a water-bird with an arrow; it fell near the maidens, and when his servant came to fetch it they fell into talk with him and told him of the princess''s beauty and her heart. Zāl sent them back with many gifts and arranged to meet the princess at night. That night he came beneath Rūdābeh''s palace, and she stood on her high tower with the moonlight on her face. They spoke long across the height. Rūdābeh said: ''Hero, you have come far and wearily; I will let down my hair to you; take hold of it and climb up.'' She unbound her braids and let her long dark hair fall down from the tower. Zāl kissed her hair and said: ''How could I cause my beloved such pain?'' He took his lasso, cast it over the battlements and climbed up by the rope. They sat together and opened their hearts, vowing that neither would marry anyone but the other, and only as dawn drew near did Zāl reluctantly leave.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.06','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔与鲁达贝”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.7','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',7,'父亲与国王的反对','The opposition of father and king','扎尔召来祭司和贤者，把心事告诉了他们，他们都沉默不语，因为谁都知道梅赫拉布是佐哈克的后裔，伊朗的英雄怎能和暴君的子孙结亲？扎尔就写信给父亲萨姆，提醒他当年抛弃自己的事，请求父亲成全这门婚事。萨姆读了信，心中十分为难：一边是自己亏欠的儿子，一边是暴君的血统。他召来星象家，让他们观察星辰。星象家们算了很久，笑着回来说：萨姆啊，这是一桩吉祥的婚姻，扎尔和鲁达贝会生下一个力大无穷的英雄，他将为伊朗征服世界，扫除一切邪恶。萨姆听了，心中宽慰，答应去向国王求情。可是马努切赫尔国王听说了这件事，非常担心，怕佐哈克的血脉和萨姆的力量结合在一起，将来会危害伊朗。他命令萨姆率军去攻打喀布尔，铲除梅赫拉布一族。佐哈克是《列王纪》里最可怕的暴君，他统治了一千年，最后被英雄法里东推翻，被锁在德马万德山中，所以他的后代总被伊朗人提防。','Zāl summoned the priests and wise men and told them his heart, but they were silent, for all knew Mehrāb descended from Zahhāk; how could a hero of Iran marry into the tyrant''s line? So Zāl wrote to his father, reminding him of how he had once cast him off and begging him to grant this marriage. Reading the letter, Sām was torn between the son he had wronged and the tyrant''s blood. He called the astrologers to read the stars. After long reckoning they came back smiling: ''Sām, this is a blessed marriage. Zāl and Rūdābeh will have a son of matchless strength who will conquer the world for Iran and sweep away all evil.'' Comforted, Sām promised to plead with the king. But King Manūchehr, hearing of it, was deeply troubled, fearing that Zahhāk''s blood joined with Sām''s might would one day threaten Iran, and he ordered Sām to march against Kābul and root out Mehrāb''s house. Zahhāk is the most terrible tyrant of the Shahnameh, who ruled a thousand years before the hero Fereydūn overthrew him and chained him in Mount Damāvand; so the Iranians always mistrusted his descendants.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.07','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔与鲁达贝”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.8','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',8,'王后的出使','The queen''s embassy','消息传到喀布尔，梅赫拉布才知道女儿的事，他勃然大怒，拔出剑来要杀死鲁达贝，说她给全族带来了灭顶之灾。王后辛多赫特拦住了他，说：不要动手，让我去想办法。辛多赫特是一位聪明而勇敢的女子，她带着无数的礼物——金银、宝石、骏马、大象和奴隶——亲自去见正在进军的萨姆。她没有说出自己是谁，只说是喀布尔王派来的使者。萨姆收下礼物，耐心地听她说话。辛多赫特说：喀布尔的人们有什么罪过，要遭受战火？如果国王有罪，就惩罚国王；百姓是无辜的。最后她才说出自己就是鲁达贝的母亲。萨姆被她的智慧和勇气打动，握住她的手，发誓说：只要我活着，就不会让喀布尔受到伤害，扎尔和鲁达贝的婚事我一定会成全。萨姆派扎尔亲自去国王的宫廷求情。','When the news reached Kābul, Mehrāb learned for the first time of his daughter''s love. In fury he drew his sword to kill Rūdābeh, saying she had brought ruin on the whole house. Queen Sindokht held him back: ''Do not strike; let me find a way.'' Wise and brave, Sindokht went herself to meet the advancing Sām with countless gifts — gold, silver, jewels, horses, elephants and slaves. She did not say who she was, only that she came as the king of Kābul''s envoy. Sām accepted the gifts and listened patiently. ''What crime have the people of Kābul committed to suffer war? If the king has sinned, punish the king; the people are innocent.'' Only at the end did she reveal that she was Rūdābeh''s mother. Moved by her wisdom and courage, Sām took her hand and swore: ''While I live, Kābul shall come to no harm, and I will bring about the marriage of Zāl and Rūdābeh.'' He sent Zāl himself to plead at the king''s court.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.08','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔与鲁达贝”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.9','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',9,'智者的谜语','The riddles of the wise men','扎尔来到马努切赫尔的宫廷，国王让宫中的智者们用谜语来考验他。第一个智者问：有十二棵高大的柏树，每棵树上长着三十根枝条，这是什么？扎尔回答：这是一年的十二个月，每个月有三十天。第二个智者问：有两匹骏马，一匹黑，一匹白，它们彼此追逐，却永远追不上，这是什么？扎尔说：这是黑夜和白昼。第三个智者问：有三十个骑手，在国王面前经过，有时少了一个，再看又是三十个，这是什么？扎尔说：这是月亮的日子，有的月份少了一天。第四个智者问：有一座美丽的花园，一个人拿着锋利的镰刀走进去，不分青的黄的，把草全都割倒，这是什么？扎尔说：花园就是这个世界，割草的人就是死亡，他不分老少，把所有的人都带走。国王和满朝的人都赞叹扎尔的智慧。第二天，扎尔又在骑射和摔跤中显示了无人能比的本领。马努切赫尔国王终于答应了这门婚事。','At Manūchehr''s court the king had his wise men test Zāl with riddles. The first asked: ''There are twelve tall cypresses, each with thirty branches; what are they?'' Zāl answered: ''The twelve months of the year, each with thirty days.'' The second asked: ''Two noble horses, one black and one white, chase each other and never overtake; what are they?'' ''Night and day.'' The third asked: ''Thirty horsemen pass before the king; sometimes one is missing, yet look again and there are thirty; what are they?'' ''The days of the moon; some months lack a day.'' The fourth asked: ''There is a lovely garden; a man enters with a sharp scythe and mows down all the grass, green and dry alike; what is it?'' Zāl said: ''The garden is this world and the mower is death, who takes all, old and young alike.'' The king and the whole court marvelled at his wisdom. The next day Zāl showed unmatched skill at riding, archery and wrestling, and King Manūchehr at last consented to the marriage.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.09','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔与鲁达贝”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.10','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',10,'婚礼','The wedding','扎尔带着国王的许可，日夜兼程回到扎布尔斯坦，又赶往喀布尔。喀布尔城里张灯结彩，到处是音乐和欢笑，街道上撒满了麝香和玫瑰水，大象背上驮着鼓手，人们用金银和珠宝迎接新郎。萨姆也来了，辛多赫特领着他去看鲁达贝。萨姆一见这位美丽的儿媳，就满心欢喜，赞叹不已。扎尔和鲁达贝按照古老的礼仪结为夫妻，坐在同一张宝座上，接受众人的祝福。婚礼的庆典一连举行了许多天。之后，扎尔带着新娘回到扎布尔斯坦，萨姆把领地交给儿子治理，自己又出征去了。曾经被父亲抛弃在山上的白发婴儿，如今有了自己的家，也有了一位为他放下长发的妻子。星象家们预言的那位英雄，很快就要降生了。两家都拿出了最贵重的礼物，庆祝这桩来之不易的婚事；伊朗和喀布尔之间的战事，也因为这场婚礼而平息了。','With the king''s consent Zāl rode day and night back to Zābulistān and on to Kābul. The city was hung with lamps and garlands and filled with music and laughter; the streets were strewn with musk and rosewater, drummers rode on elephants, and the people welcomed the bridegroom with gold, silver and jewels. Sām came too, and Sindokht led him to see Rūdābeh; at the sight of his lovely daughter-in-law he was filled with joy and admiration. Zāl and Rūdābeh were married by the ancient rites and sat together on one throne to receive the blessings of all. The feasting lasted many days. Then Zāl brought his bride home to Zābulistān, and Sām gave his son the rule of his lands and went off to war again. The white-haired baby once cast away on the mountain now had a home of his own and a wife who had let down her hair for him. The hero the astrologers had foretold would soon be born. Both houses brought out their costliest gifts to celebrate a marriage so hard won, and the threatened war between Iran and Kābul was laid to rest by the wedding.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.10','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“扎尔与鲁达贝”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.11','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',11,'难产与西摩格的羽毛','The hard labour and the Simorgh''s feather','不久，鲁达贝怀了孕。她的身体一天天沉重起来，腹中的孩子大得出奇，她日夜疼痛，脸色像枯萎的花一样苍白。到了生产的时候，孩子却怎么也生不下来，鲁达贝昏死过去，宫中一片哭声，人人都以为她要死了。扎尔想起了西摩格的话，取出那根羽毛，点起火来焚烧。天空立刻暗了下来，西摩格从云中飞落，问他为何如此悲伤。扎尔说了妻子的危难。西摩格说：不要哭，你的妻子会生下一个狮子般的儿子。去请一位手法娴熟的祭司来，先让鲁达贝喝下掺了药的酒，让她沉睡，不觉得痛，再剖开她的腹侧，把孩子取出来，然后把伤口缝好，把我给你的药草捣碎了和着牛奶敷上，再用我的羽毛轻轻抚过伤口，她就会痊愈。扎尔照着做了。孩子被取了出来，大得像一个满一岁的孩子。鲁达贝醒来，看见孩子，说：我终于解脱了！在波斯语里，“解脱”这个词的读音就像“鲁斯塔姆”，孩子就这样得了名字。','Soon Rūdābeh was with child. Her body grew heavier day by day, the child within her was wondrously large, she was in pain day and night, and her face grew pale as a withered flower. When her time came the child would not be born; Rūdābeh fainted, the palace filled with weeping, and all thought she would die. Zāl remembered the Simorgh''s words, took out the feather and set it alight. At once the sky darkened and the Simorgh came down from the clouds and asked why he grieved. He told her of his wife''s peril. The Simorgh said: ''Do not weep; your wife will bear a lion of a son. Fetch a skilled priest; first let Rūdābeh drink wine mixed with a drug so that she sleeps and feels no pain; then let him open her side and draw out the child, and sew up the wound; crush the herb I give you with milk and lay it on, and stroke the wound with my feather, and she will heal.'' Zāl did all this. The child was drawn out, as big as a year-old infant. When Rūdābeh woke and saw him she cried: ''I am delivered!'' — and because in Persian the word sounds like Rostam, so the child was named.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.11','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_zal_rostam.12','storyver.persian.zimmern_zal_rostam',12,'白象与拉赫什','The white elephant and Rakhsh','鲁斯塔姆长得飞快，十个乳母都喂不饱他，断奶以后，他一个人吃的饭比五个大人还多。他长得像柏树一样高，力气像大象一样大。有一天夜里，鲁斯塔姆正在熟睡，忽然听见外面一片喧哗：国王的一头白象挣脱了锁链，发了疯，在城里横冲直撞，人们四散奔逃。鲁斯塔姆跳起来，抓起祖父萨姆留下的铁杵冲出门去，守门人怕他出事，拦着不让走，他一拳打倒守门人，砸开了门，迎着白象冲上去，一杵就把白象打倒在地。后来，鲁斯塔姆要为自己挑一匹马。人们把扎布尔和喀布尔的马群赶到他面前，他用手按在马背上，马就压得弯下腰来，没有一匹承受得住。最后，他看见一匹母马身后跟着一匹小马，毛色像玫瑰花瓣点缀在藏红花上，四肢有力，胸膛宽阔。牧马人说：这匹马叫拉赫什，许多人想要它，可谁也不敢碰它，它的母马会像狮子一样冲上来保护它。鲁斯塔姆抛出套索套住了小马，母马冲过来，被他一声大喝吓退。鲁斯塔姆问牧马人这匹马的价钱，牧马人说：如果你是鲁斯塔姆，就骑上它去保卫伊朗吧，它的价钱就是整个伊朗。从此，鲁斯塔姆和拉赫什再也没有分开过。','Rostam grew so fast that ten nurses could not satisfy him, and once weaned he ate more than five grown men. He grew tall as a cypress and strong as an elephant. One night as he slept he heard an uproar outside: the king''s white elephant had broken its chains and run mad through the city, and people were fleeing. Rostam leapt up, seized the iron mace his grandfather Sām had left, and ran to the door; the gatekeepers, fearing for him, tried to stop him, but he knocked them down, broke the door open, charged the elephant and felled it with a single blow. Later Rostam wanted a horse of his own. The herds of Zābul and Kābul were driven before him, but when he pressed his hand on a horse''s back it sank down under him; none could bear his weight. At last he saw a colt following a mare, its coat like rose-petals scattered on saffron, its legs strong and its chest broad. The herdsman said: ''This is Rakhsh. Many have wanted him, but no one dares touch him; his mother rushes to defend him like a lion.'' Rostam cast his lasso over the colt; the mare charged, and he drove her back with a great shout. When he asked the price, the herdsman said: ''If you are Rostam, ride him and defend Iran; his price is the land of Iran.'' From then on Rostam and Rakhsh were never parted.','claim.v0350.shn_zal.12','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.1','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',1,'魔鬼歌手','The demon minstrel','凯·卡乌斯继承了伊朗的王位，坐在宝座上，看着满朝的勇士和堆积如山的财宝，以为天下再也没有人比他更强大。有一天，宫门口来了一个陌生的歌手，请求在国王面前献唱。他其实是一个魔鬼变的。他弹起琴，唱起了马赞德兰：那里四季鲜花盛开，夜莺在花园里歌唱，山上长满了郁金香，空气温暖而芬芳，城里堆满了金子和宝石，美丽的少女多得数不清。凯·卡乌斯听得入了迷，当即宣布：我要率军去征服马赞德兰，把那里的财富和美景都收归伊朗。满朝的勇士都惊呆了，他们知道马赞德兰是魔鬼居住的地方，以前的贤明君王贾姆希德和法里东都不敢去冒犯。他们不敢劝阻国王，就悄悄派人连夜赶到扎布尔斯坦，去请扎尔。','Kay Kāvus inherited the throne of Iran and, seated in state with his champions around him and treasure heaped high, thought no one in the world more mighty than he. One day a strange minstrel came to the palace gate asking to sing before the king. He was in truth a demon. He struck his lute and sang of Māzandarān: flowers bloom there in every season, nightingales sing in the gardens, tulips cover the hills, the air is warm and fragrant, the cities are heaped with gold and jewels, and its fair maidens are past counting. Kay Kāvus was enchanted and declared at once: ''I will march and conquer Māzandarān and add its wealth and beauty to Iran.'' The champions were aghast, for they knew Māzandarān was the home of demons, which even the wise kings Jamshīd and Fereydūn had not dared to attack. Not daring to oppose the king, they secretly sent a messenger riding through the night to Zābulistān to fetch Zāl.','claim.v0350.shn_seven.01','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.2','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',2,'白魔的冰雹','The White Demon''s hail','扎尔赶到宫中，恳切地劝国王：马赞德兰是魔鬼的国度，他们会用魔法迷惑人，以前的国王都不敢去，你不要拿伊朗的勇士和自己的性命去冒险。凯·卡乌斯却说：我比贾姆希德和法里东更强大，我有更多的勇士和财宝，魔鬼又能把我怎样？你若是怕，就留在家里吧。扎尔无可奈何，只好告辞。凯·卡乌斯率领大军进入马赞德兰，烧杀抢掠，掳走了许多财宝和人口。马赞德兰的国王大为惊恐，派人去请魔鬼的首领白魔。白魔连夜召来一片乌云，天地间一片漆黑，接着降下了石头和冰雹，伊朗的军队在黑暗中乱成一团。等到天亮，凯·卡乌斯和他的勇士们全都瞎了眼睛，被魔鬼们抓住，关进了地牢。凯·卡乌斯在黑暗中后悔不已，偷偷派人给扎尔送信：我没有听你的话，如今落得这样的下场，快来救我吧。','Zāl hastened to court and earnestly urged the king: ''Māzandarān is the land of demons; they bewitch men with magic. No king before you has dared to go; do not risk the champions of Iran and your own life.'' But Kay Kāvus said: ''I am mightier than Jamshīd and Fereydūn, with more champions and more treasure; what can demons do to me? If you are afraid, stay at home.'' Zāl could do nothing but take his leave. Kay Kāvus led his army into Māzandarān, burning and plundering, carrying off treasure and captives. In terror the king of Māzandarān sent for the White Demon, chief of the demons. In the night the White Demon summoned a black cloud that plunged the world into darkness, then rained down stones and hail, and the Iranian army fell into confusion in the dark. When day came Kay Kāvus and all his champions were blind, and the demons seized them and cast them into a dungeon. In the darkness Kay Kāvus repented bitterly and secretly sent a message to Zāl: ''I did not listen to you, and this is my fate; come quickly and save me.''','claim.v0350.shn_seven.02','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.3','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',3,'两条路与狮子','Two roads, and the lion','扎尔收到信，对儿子鲁斯塔姆说：国王落难了，只有你能救他。从这里到马赞德兰有两条路：一条长而安全，要走六个月；另一条短而凶险，只要两个星期，可路上有狮子、恶龙、女巫和魔鬼。鲁斯塔姆说：我选那条近路，愿神保佑我。他穿上虎皮做的战袍，带上祖父的铁杵，骑上拉赫什出发了，一天走两天的路程。第一天傍晚，他在一片芦苇荡边打了一头野驴，烤来吃了，然后摘下拉赫什的辔头，让它去吃草，自己躺在芦苇丛里睡着了。半夜，一头凶猛的狮子回到它的巢穴，看见熟睡的人和马，就向拉赫什扑过来。拉赫什像火一样跳起来，用前蹄猛击狮子的头，用牙齿咬住狮子的背，把它摔在地上踩死了。鲁斯塔姆醒来，看见死狮，却责备拉赫什说：谁让你去和狮子打？要是你被它咬死了，我怎么背着铁杵、穿着战袍走到马赞德兰去？下次遇到危险，先把我叫醒。','When Zāl received the letter he said to his son Rostam: ''The king is in peril, and only you can save him. Two roads lead to Māzandarān: one long and safe, six months'' journey; the other short and perilous, only two weeks, but beset by lions, dragons, witches and demons.'' ''I choose the short road,'' said Rostam; ''may God protect me.'' He put on his war-coat of tiger-skin, took his grandfather''s mace, mounted Rakhsh and set out, covering two days'' journey in each day. On the first evening he killed a wild ass by a reed-bed, roasted it and ate, then took off Rakhsh''s bridle to let him graze and lay down to sleep among the reeds. At midnight a fierce lion came back to its lair, saw the sleeping man and horse, and sprang at Rakhsh. Rakhsh leapt up like fire, struck the lion''s head with his forefeet, seized its back in his teeth, threw it down and trampled it to death. Waking to find the dead lion, Rostam scolded Rakhsh: ''Who told you to fight a lion? If it had killed you, how would I have walked to Māzandarān with my mace and war-coat? Next time danger comes, wake me first.''','claim.v0350.shn_seven.03','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.4','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',4,'沙漠中的公羊','The ram in the desert','第二天，鲁斯塔姆走进了一片炎热的沙漠，烈日像火一样烤着大地，四周没有一滴水，也没有一片树荫。走了很久，人和马都渴得精疲力竭，拉赫什的脚步越来越慢，鲁斯塔姆也从马上摔了下来，躺在滚烫的沙子上，舌头干裂，眼看就要渴死了。他抬起头，向造物主祈祷：神啊，你知道我是为了去救国王才走上这条路的，我不怕任何敌人，可是我不愿意渴死在这荒野里，求你救救我吧。就在这时，一只肥壮的公羊从他身边走过。鲁斯塔姆想：这只羊一定是去喝水的。他挣扎着站起来，拿着剑跟在公羊后面，走了不远，果然看见了一眼清澈的泉水。他和拉赫什痛痛快快地喝足了水，洗净了身上的尘土。鲁斯塔姆没有伤害那只公羊，而是向天空祝福它，感谢造物主派它来救了自己的性命。','The next day Rostam entered a scorching desert; the sun burned the earth like fire, and there was not a drop of water nor a patch of shade. After a long march man and horse were spent with thirst; Rakhsh slowed, and Rostam fell from his back and lay on the burning sand, his tongue cracked, close to dying of thirst. He raised his head and prayed: ''O God, you know I took this road to save the king. I fear no enemy, but I would not die of thirst in this wilderness; save me.'' At that moment a fat ram passed close by him. ''This ram must be going to drink,'' thought Rostam. He struggled to his feet and followed it, sword in hand, and not far off he found a clear spring. He and Rakhsh drank their fill and washed off the dust. Rostam did not harm the ram, but blessed it towards heaven and thanked the Creator for sending it to save his life.','claim.v0350.shn_seven.04','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.5','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',5,'恶龙','The dragon','那天夜里，鲁斯塔姆睡在泉水边，叮嘱拉赫什：不要再自己去和野兽打斗，有危险就叫醒我。半夜，一条巨大的恶龙从洞里爬了出来，它长八十腕尺，吐出的气像火一样，连大象都不敢靠近这片草地。拉赫什看见恶龙，连忙跑到鲁斯塔姆身边，用蹄子刨地、嘶叫，把他叫醒。鲁斯塔姆醒来四处张望，恶龙却用魔法隐去了身形，他什么也没看见，就骂拉赫什打扰他睡觉，又躺下了。恶龙又出现了，拉赫什又把他叫醒，恶龙又不见了，鲁斯塔姆大怒，说：你再这样无缘无故地吵我，我就砍下你的头，自己走到马赞德兰去。恶龙第三次出现，拉赫什不顾一切地又跑来叫醒他。这一次，造物主让鲁斯塔姆看见了恶龙。他拔出剑，和恶龙搏斗起来，恶龙太强大了，几乎要把他压倒。拉赫什冲上去，一口咬住恶龙的肩膀，把它的皮肉撕开，鲁斯塔姆趁机一剑砍下了恶龙的头。他向拉赫什道歉，感谢这匹忠诚的马救了他的命。','That night Rostam slept by the spring, telling Rakhsh not to fight beasts alone again but to wake him at any danger. At midnight a huge dragon crawled from its den, eighty cubits long, its breath like fire, so fearsome that not even elephants dared enter that meadow. Seeing it, Rakhsh ran to Rostam, pawing the ground and neighing to wake him. Rostam woke and looked around, but the dragon had hidden itself by magic, and he saw nothing; he cursed Rakhsh for disturbing his sleep and lay down again. The dragon came again, Rakhsh woke him again, and again it vanished. Rostam in fury said: ''Wake me again for nothing and I will cut off your head and walk to Māzandarān myself.'' The dragon came a third time, and Rakhsh, heedless of the threat, ran again to wake him. This time the Creator let Rostam see the dragon. He drew his sword and fought it, but the dragon was so strong it nearly crushed him. Rakhsh rushed in, bit the dragon''s shoulder and tore its flesh, and Rostam struck off its head. He begged Rakhsh''s pardon and thanked the faithful horse for saving his life.','claim.v0350.shn_seven.05','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.6','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',6,'女巫','The witch','第四天，鲁斯塔姆穿过一片美丽的山谷，来到一眼泉水边，只见树荫下铺着华丽的毯子，摆满了烤好的羊肉、面包、水果和一瓶美酒，旁边还放着一把琴。原来这是女巫们设下的筵席。鲁斯塔姆饿了，坐下来大吃大喝，拿起琴弹了起来，唱道：我鲁斯塔姆是个漂泊的人，一生没有安宁的日子，每天不是和狮子打，就是和恶龙斗，很少能见到这样的筵席和美丽的花园。歌声传开，一个女巫听见了，把自己变成一个美丽的少女，脸颊像春天的花朵，走到他的身边坐下。鲁斯塔姆很高兴，倒了一杯酒递给她，并说出了造物主的名字，感谢神赐给他美酒和佳人。可是女巫一听见神的名字，脸色就变了，她的美貌消失了，变回了一个又老又丑的黑脸巫婆。鲁斯塔姆看穿了她，抛出套索套住她的脖子，拔剑把她砍成了两半。','On the fourth day Rostam crossed a lovely valley to a spring, where beneath the trees rich carpets were spread with roast lamb, bread, fruit and a flask of wine, and a lute lay beside them. It was a feast laid by witches. Hungry, Rostam sat down, ate and drank, took up the lute and sang: ''I, Rostam, am a wanderer who never knows a day of rest; every day I fight lions or dragons, and seldom do I find such a feast and so fair a garden.'' A witch heard the song, changed herself into a beautiful girl with cheeks like spring flowers, and came and sat beside him. Pleased, Rostam poured a cup of wine and gave it to her, naming the Creator and thanking God for the wine and the fair companion. But at the name of God the witch''s face changed; her beauty vanished and she became again a black-faced, wrinkled old hag. Seeing through her, Rostam cast his lasso around her neck and cut her in two with his sword.','claim.v0350.shn_seven.06','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.7','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',7,'乌拉德','Olād','第五天，鲁斯塔姆走进了一片没有日月、伸手不见五指的黑暗之地，他让拉赫什自己找路，走了很久，终于重见光明，来到一片绿油油的田野。他又累又困，就脱下被汗浸透的战袍，让拉赫什在田里吃庄稼，自己躺下睡着了。看守田地的人跑来，用棍子打他的脚，骂他放马糟蹋庄稼。鲁斯塔姆醒来，一言不发，抓住那人，一把拧下了他的两只耳朵。看田人捂着头跑去向当地的领主乌拉德哭诉。乌拉德带着一群武士赶来，要惩罚这个陌生人。鲁斯塔姆骑上拉赫什迎战，把乌拉德的武士杀得四散奔逃，又抛出套索，活捉了乌拉德。鲁斯塔姆对他说：如果你给我带路，带我找到白魔的洞穴和国王被关押的地方，等我打败了马赞德兰，我就让你做这片土地的国王；如果你骗我，我就要你的命。乌拉德答应了，从此做了鲁斯塔姆的向导。','On the fifth day Rostam entered a land of darkness without sun or moon, where he could not see his hand before him; he let Rakhsh find the way, and after a long time came out into the light, to green fields. Weary and sleepy, he took off his sweat-soaked war-coat, let Rakhsh graze in the crops and lay down to sleep. The keeper of the fields came running, struck his feet with a stick and cursed him for letting his horse spoil the crops. Rostam woke, said nothing, seized the man and wrenched off both his ears. Holding his head, the keeper ran to complain to the local lord, Olād. Olād came with a band of warriors to punish the stranger. Rostam rode out on Rakhsh, scattered Olād''s men, and caught Olād alive with his lasso. ''If you guide me to the White Demon''s cave and to where the king is held,'' said Rostam, ''when I have conquered Māzandarān I will make you king of this land; if you deceive me, your life is forfeit.'' Olād agreed, and became Rostam''s guide.','claim.v0350.shn_seven.07','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.8','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',8,'阿尔章魔','The demon Arzhang','乌拉德带着鲁斯塔姆向前走，告诉他：前面是魔鬼阿尔章的营地，他是马赞德兰魔鬼军队的首领之一，守着通往国王监牢的道路；过了那里，再往前是七座大山，白魔就住在山中的一个洞穴里，他身边有成千上万的魔鬼守卫。鲁斯塔姆把乌拉德绑在一棵树上，等到天亮，独自骑着拉赫什冲进了阿尔章的营地，发出一声雷鸣般的怒吼，震得魔鬼们心惊胆战。阿尔章听见吼声，从帐篷里冲出来迎战。鲁斯塔姆策马上前，伸手抓住阿尔章的头和耳朵，把他的头从身子上拧了下来，扔到魔鬼们中间。魔鬼们看见首领的头滚落在地，吓得魂飞魄散，四散逃命，许多魔鬼在逃跑中自相践踏而死。鲁斯塔姆杀散了阿尔章的军队，回到乌拉德身边，给他松了绑，让他继续带路，去找被关押的国王。','Olād led Rostam on and told him: ''Ahead is the camp of the demon Arzhang, one of the chiefs of the demon host of Māzandarān, who guards the road to the king''s prison; beyond it lie seven great mountains, and in a cave among them lives the White Demon with thousands of demons on guard.'' Rostam tied Olād to a tree, waited for dawn, then rode alone on Rakhsh into Arzhang''s camp with a roar like thunder that shook the demons'' hearts. Hearing it, Arzhang rushed out of his tent to fight. Rostam spurred forward, seized Arzhang by the head and ears, wrenched his head from his body and flung it among the demons. Seeing their chief''s head roll on the ground, the demons fled in terror, many trampling one another to death as they ran. Having scattered Arzhang''s host, Rostam went back to Olād, untied him, and bade him lead on to the imprisoned king.','claim.v0350.shn_seven.08','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.9','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',9,'白魔的洞穴','The White Demon''s cave','鲁斯塔姆找到了被关押的凯·卡乌斯和伊朗的勇士们，他们都瞎了眼睛，在黑暗中受苦。国王听见鲁斯塔姆的声音，悲喜交加，对他说：只有白魔心脏里的血滴进我们的眼睛，我们才能重见光明。鲁斯塔姆就让乌拉德带他去白魔的住处。他们越过七座大山，来到一个深不见底的洞穴前，洞口有许多魔鬼守卫。乌拉德说：等太阳升到最高、天气最热的时候再进去，那时魔鬼们都会睡觉。鲁斯塔姆等到正午，冲向洞口，杀死了守卫的魔鬼，走进了漆黑的洞中。洞里黑得像地狱一样，他擦了擦眼睛，才看见一个巨大的身影躺在那里，像一座山，脸是黑的，头发是白的，那就是白魔。鲁斯塔姆没有趁他睡着下手，大吼一声把他叫醒。白魔跳起来，拿起一块磨盘大的石头冲了过来。两人在洞中殊死搏斗，鲁斯塔姆一剑砍断了白魔的一条腿，白魔仍用一条腿扑上来和他扭打，鲜血和汗水流满了洞穴。最后，鲁斯塔姆把白魔举起来摔在地上，用匕首刺进他的胸膛，挖出了他的肝。','Rostam found Kay Kāvus and the champions of Iran in their prison, all blind and suffering in darkness. Hearing Rostam''s voice, the king wept with joy and said: ''Only blood from the White Demon''s heart dropped in our eyes can restore our sight.'' So Rostam had Olād guide him to the White Demon''s lair. They crossed the seven mountains to the mouth of a bottomless cave guarded by many demons. Olād said: ''Wait until the sun is highest and the heat greatest; then the demons sleep.'' At noon Rostam rushed the entrance, killed the guards and went into the black cave. It was dark as hell; he rubbed his eyes and at last saw a huge form lying there like a mountain, its face black and its hair white: the White Demon. Rostam would not strike him sleeping, but woke him with a great shout. The White Demon sprang up, seized a stone like a millstone and charged. They fought for their lives in the cave; Rostam cut off one of the demon''s legs, yet still it grappled him on one leg, and blood and sweat ran through the cave. At last Rostam lifted the White Demon, dashed him to the ground, plunged his dagger into his breast, and tore out his liver.','claim.v0350.shn_seven.09','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.10','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',10,'重见光明','Sight restored','鲁斯塔姆带着白魔的肝走出洞穴，阳光照在他满是鲜血的身上，乌拉德看见他，吓得以为是魔鬼出来了，直到他开口说话才放下心来。鲁斯塔姆说：我已经杀死了白魔，打开了马赞德兰的大门，你的功劳我不会忘记。两人一起回到关押国王的地方。鲁斯塔姆把白魔肝中的血滴进凯·卡乌斯的眼睛里，国王的眼睛立刻亮了起来，又看见了阳光，看见了眼前的鲁斯塔姆。接着，鲁斯塔姆又把血滴进伊朗勇士们的眼睛里，所有的人都重见了光明。被囚禁的伊朗军队欢呼起来，他们拥抱鲁斯塔姆，赞美他是伊朗的救星，是勇士中的勇士。凯·卡乌斯坐在宝座上，命令摆设盛大的宴席，一连七天庆祝胜利，向造物主献上感恩的祷告。他对鲁斯塔姆说：没有你，就没有伊朗的国王。','Rostam came out of the cave with the White Demon''s liver, the sunlight on his blood-soaked body; Olād, seeing him, took him for a demon and was terrified until he spoke. ''I have killed the White Demon and opened the gates of Māzandarān,'' said Rostam; ''I will not forget your service.'' Together they went back to where the king was held. Rostam dropped blood from the White Demon''s liver into Kay Kāvus''s eyes, and at once the king''s eyes were bright again; he saw the sunlight and Rostam before him. Then Rostam dropped the blood into the eyes of the champions of Iran, and all of them saw again. The captive Iranian host cheered, embraced Rostam and praised him as the saviour of Iran, the champion of champions. Kay Kāvus took his throne and ordered a great feast to celebrate the victory for seven days, with prayers of thanks to the Creator. He said to Rostam: ''But for you, Iran would have no king.''','claim.v0350.shn_seven.10','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.11','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',11,'化为岩石的国王','The king who became a rock','凯·卡乌斯恢复了力量，写信给马赞德兰的国王，命令他前来投降，交出财宝，做伊朗的属国。马赞德兰国王收到信，傲慢地拒绝了，召集了魔鬼和人组成的大军，带着战象前来迎战。两军在平原上激战了七天，不分胜负。第八天，鲁斯塔姆冲到马赞德兰国王面前，举起长矛刺去，眼看就要刺中，那位会魔法的国王忽然把自己变成了一块巨大的岩石，长矛刺在石头上，再也伤不了他。伊朗的勇士们都惊呆了，谁也搬不动这块岩石。鲁斯塔姆跳下马，一个人把岩石扛了起来，一直扛回伊朗的营地，扔在国王的帐篷前，对着岩石说：快出来，否则我就用铁锤把你砸成碎块，扔到火里烧掉！岩石里的国王害怕了，从石头里走了出来，变回了人形。凯·卡乌斯下令处死了这个作恶多端的魔法师。鲁斯塔姆履行了诺言，请国王封乌拉德为马赞德兰的统治者。伊朗的大军满载着战利品，凯旋回国。','Restored to strength, Kay Kāvus wrote to the king of Māzandarān commanding him to come and submit, surrender his treasure and become a vassal of Iran. The king of Māzandarān haughtily refused, gathered a host of demons and men with war-elephants, and came out to battle. For seven days the armies fought on the plain without either winning. On the eighth day Rostam reached the king of Māzandarān and thrust his spear at him, but just as it was about to strike, the sorcerer king turned himself into a huge rock; the spear struck stone and could not harm him. The Iranian champions were astonished, and none could move the rock. Rostam leapt down, lifted the rock alone, carried it all the way back to the Iranian camp, threw it down before the king''s tent and said to it: ''Come out, or I will smash you to pieces with my hammer and throw you into the fire!'' The king inside the rock was afraid, came out of the stone and took human shape again. Kay Kāvus had the wicked sorcerer put to death. Keeping his promise, Rostam asked the king to make Olād ruler of Māzandarān, and the army of Iran returned home laden with spoils.','claim.v0350.shn_seven.11','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“进军马赞德兰”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_mazinderan.12','storyver.persian.zimmern_mazinderan',12,'飞天的宝座','The flying throne','可是凯·卡乌斯并没有因为这次教训变得谨慎。后来，一个魔鬼又来迷惑他，对他说：你已经统治了整个大地，为什么不去统治天空呢？天上的日月星辰，难道不该属于你吗？凯·卡乌斯又动了心。他问智者们怎样才能飞上天空，最后想出一个办法：他让人从鹰巢里捉来四只小鹰，用最好的肉喂养长大，然后造了一个宝座，在宝座的四角各竖一根长矛，矛尖上挂着一块羊肉，再把四只饿了好几天的鹰绑在宝座的四角。鹰想吃矛尖上的肉，拼命往上飞，就把宝座带上了天空。凯·卡乌斯坐在宝座上，越飞越高，越过了云层。可是鹰飞累了，再也飞不动了，宝座从空中坠落下来，跌进了一片荒凉的森林。凯·卡乌斯侥幸没有摔死，却困在林中又饿又怕。鲁斯塔姆和勇士们四处寻找，终于找到了他。勇士们严厉地责备他：你一次又一次地听信魔鬼，像个疯子一样。凯·卡乌斯羞愧难当，从此闭门忏悔了许多日子。','But Kay Kāvus did not grow cautious from this lesson. Later a demon came again to tempt him: ''You rule the whole earth; why not rule the sky? Should not the sun, moon and stars be yours as well?'' Again Kay Kāvus was tempted. He asked his wise men how to fly to the sky, and at last hit on a plan: he had four young eagles taken from their nests and reared on the choicest meat; then he had a throne made, set a long spear upright at each of its corners with a piece of mutton hanging from each spear-point, and bound four eagles that had been starved for days to its four corners. Straining for the meat above them, the eagles flew upward and carried the throne into the sky. Kay Kāvus rose higher and higher, above the clouds. But the eagles tired and could fly no more, and the throne fell from the sky into a desolate forest. Kay Kāvus escaped death but lay trapped in the forest, hungry and afraid. Rostam and the champions searched far and wide and at last found him. The champions rebuked him sharply: ''Again and again you listen to demons; you act like a madman.'' Ashamed, Kay Kāvus shut himself away in penitence for many days.','claim.v0350.shn_seven.12','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“凯·卡乌斯的更多愚行”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.1','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',1,'丢失的拉赫什','Rakhsh is lost','有一天，鲁斯塔姆心中烦闷，就带上弓箭和套索，骑着拉赫什到伊朗与图兰交界的地方去打猎。他在一片原野上射死了一头野驴，用树枝生起火来，烤了野驴，吃饱以后，摘下拉赫什的辔头让它去吃草，自己躺在草地上睡着了。这时，一群图兰的骑手路过，看见这匹骏马在原野上独自吃草，就抛出套索把它套住，牵回城里去了。鲁斯塔姆醒来，四处寻找，不见拉赫什的踪影，心中又急又恼：没有了拉赫什，他穿着沉重的铠甲，带着铁杵，只能徒步行走，图兰人会怎样嘲笑他？他顺着马蹄印一路追去，一直追到了萨曼甘国的城门口。萨曼甘的国王和贵族们听说鲁斯塔姆来了，都出城迎接，国王对他说：英雄啊，在我的国土上，没有人敢伤害你，你的马一定能找回来，今晚请到我的宫中做客吧。','One day, restless in heart, Rostam took his bow and lasso and rode Rakhsh to hunt on the border between Iran and Tūrān. On a plain he shot a wild ass, kindled a fire of branches, roasted it and ate his fill, then took off Rakhsh''s bridle to let him graze and lay down on the grass to sleep. A band of Turanian riders passed, saw the splendid horse grazing alone, cast their lassos over him and led him off to their city. Rostam woke and searched everywhere, but there was no trace of Rakhsh. He was anxious and vexed: without Rakhsh he must walk in his heavy armour carrying his mace — how the Turanians would mock him! He followed the hoofprints all the way to the gate of the city of Samangān. When the king and nobles of Samangān heard that Rostam had come they rode out to meet him, and the king said: ''Hero, no one in my land would dare to harm you; your horse will surely be found. Be my guest in the palace tonight.''','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.01','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.2','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',2,'塔米娜的夜访','Tahmineh comes by night','萨曼甘的国王为鲁斯塔姆举行了盛大的宴会，鲁斯塔姆喝了酒，被领到一间铺着锦缎的卧室里休息。夜深了，房门忽然悄悄打开，一个侍女提着一支点燃的蜡烛走进来，身后跟着一位美丽的少女，她的脸像月亮，身段像柏树，眉毛像弯弓，头发像黑色的套索。鲁斯塔姆惊奇地问她是谁，深夜来做什么。少女说：我是萨曼甘国王的女儿，名叫塔米娜，在这片土地上没有人见过我的面容。我听过许多关于你的故事：你怎样不怕狮子、恶龙和魔鬼，你的铁杵怎样让大地颤抖。我早就倾心于你，暗暗发誓除了你谁也不嫁。如今神把你送到了我们的城里，我来是要告诉你：如果你愿意，我想做你的妻子，为你生一个像你一样勇敢的儿子。而且，我会让人把你的拉赫什找回来。鲁斯塔姆见她既美丽又聪慧，心中欢喜，第二天就请一位智慧的祭司向国王求婚。国王非常高兴，当即按照礼仪把女儿嫁给了他。','The king of Samangān held a great feast for Rostam, and when he had drunk he was led to a chamber hung with brocade to rest. Deep in the night the door opened softly; a slave-girl came in carrying a lighted candle, and behind her a beautiful maiden, her face like the moon, her figure like a cypress, her brows like bows and her hair like a dark lasso. Astonished, Rostam asked who she was and why she came at night. ''I am the daughter of the king of Samangān,'' she said; ''my name is Tahmineh, and no one in this land has seen my face. I have heard many tales of you — how you fear neither lion nor dragon nor demon, how the earth trembles at your mace. I have long given you my heart and secretly vowed to marry no one but you. Now God has brought you to our city, and I have come to tell you: if you will, I wish to be your wife and bear you a son as brave as you. And I will have your Rakhsh found.'' Seeing her as wise as she was beautiful, Rostam was glad, and the next day he sent a wise priest to ask the king for her hand. The king rejoiced and gave him his daughter by the proper rites.','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.02','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.3','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',3,'留下的信物','The token left behind','婚后不久，人们找回了拉赫什，鲁斯塔姆必须回到伊朗去了。临别时，他从自己的手臂上解下一枚臂环——上面镶着一颗宝石，是一件人人都认得的珍宝——交给塔米娜，说：好好保管它。如果神赐给你一个女儿，就把它编进她的头发里，愿它保佑她平安；如果你生了一个儿子，就把它系在他的手臂上，让他像我一样成为勇敢的人，让他凭着这件信物来找我。说完，他拥抱了塔米娜，跨上拉赫什，向伊朗的方向驰去，从此再也没有回到萨曼甘。九个月以后，塔米娜生下了一个儿子，他的脸像月亮一样明亮，笑起来像花一样灿烂，人们说他长得和鲁斯塔姆、和祖父萨姆一模一样。塔米娜给他取名为苏赫拉布，把那枚臂环系在了他的手臂上。','Soon after the marriage Rakhsh was found, and Rostam had to return to Iran. At parting he took from his arm an armlet set with a jewel, a treasure known to all, and gave it to Tahmineh: ''Keep this well. If God gives you a daughter, braid it into her hair and may it keep her safe; if you bear a son, bind it on his arm, that he may become brave as I am, and by this token let him come to find me.'' He embraced Tahmineh, mounted Rakhsh and rode off towards Iran, and never came back to Samangān. Nine months later Tahmineh bore a son with a face bright as the moon and a smile like a flower, and people said he was the very image of Rostam and of his grandfather Sām. Tahmineh named him Sohrāb and bound the armlet on his arm.','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.03','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.4','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',4,'苏赫拉布的成长','Sohrāb grows up','苏赫拉布长得出奇地快：一个月大时就像一岁的孩子，三岁时就学会了骑马射箭，五岁时有了狮子般的勇气，到了十岁，萨曼甘没有一个人能和他比武。他胸膛宽阔，手臂粗壮，跑起来像风一样快。有一天，他来到母亲面前，问她：我为什么比别的孩子都高大强壮？我的父亲是谁？别人问起我的家世，我该怎样回答？塔米娜说：孩子，不要生气，你的父亲就是伟大的鲁斯塔姆，扎尔的儿子，萨姆的孙子，世上没有比他更勇敢的英雄。她拿出鲁斯塔姆寄来的信和三颗宝石给他看，又叮嘱他：千万不要让图兰王阿夫拉西亚布知道这件事，他是鲁斯塔姆的仇敌，会想办法害你；也不要让你父亲知道你已经长成了一个勇士，否则他会把你从我身边叫走。苏赫拉布听了，热血沸腾，说：我要率领大军进攻伊朗，推翻凯·卡乌斯，让我的父亲鲁斯塔姆坐上王位；然后再去打败阿夫拉西亚布，让天下不再有两个国王。','Sohrāb grew astonishingly fast: at one month he was like a child of a year, at three he rode and shot, at five he had a lion''s courage, and at ten no one in Samangān could match him in arms. Broad-chested and strong-armed, he ran swift as the wind. One day he came to his mother and asked: ''Why am I taller and stronger than other children? Who is my father? What shall I say when people ask about my birth?'' Tahmineh said: ''Do not be angry, child. Your father is the great Rostam, son of Zāl, grandson of Sām; there is no braver hero in the world.'' She showed him a letter Rostam had sent and three jewels, and warned him: ''Never let Afrāsiyāb, king of Tūrān, know this; he is Rostam''s enemy and would seek to harm you. Nor let your father know you have grown into a warrior, or he will call you away from me.'' Sohrāb''s blood was fired: ''I will lead an army against Iran, overthrow Kay Kāvus and set my father Rostam on the throne; then I will defeat Afrāsiyāb, so that the world has no longer two kings.''','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.04','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.5','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',5,'阿夫拉西亚布的阴谋','Afrāsiyāb''s plot','苏赫拉布为自己挑选了一匹配得上他的骏马，召集了一支军队。消息传到图兰王阿夫拉西亚布那里，他心中暗喜，想出了一个阴险的计策。他派出两位将领胡曼和巴尔曼，带着一万二千名精兵和许多礼物去投奔苏赫拉布，表面上是支持他攻打伊朗。他在私下里对两位将领说：这件事一定要保守秘密，千万不能让父子二人相认。如果苏赫拉布在战场上遇见鲁斯塔姆，就要想办法让他们彼此不知道对方是谁。也许这个年轻的勇士会杀死鲁斯塔姆，那样伊朗就失去了最强的支柱；也许鲁斯塔姆会杀死这个孩子，那样鲁斯塔姆的心也会被悲伤压垮。不论哪一种，对我们图兰都有好处。胡曼和巴尔曼领了命令，带着礼物和阿夫拉西亚布的书信来到萨曼甘，苏赫拉布高兴地接待了他们，一点也不知道这背后的阴谋。','Sohrāb chose himself a horse worthy of him and gathered an army. When the news reached Afrāsiyāb, king of Tūrān, he secretly rejoiced and devised a wicked plan. He sent two generals, Hūmān and Bārmān, with twelve thousand picked men and many gifts to join Sohrāb, as though to support his attack on Iran. In private he told the two: ''Keep this secret: father and son must never know each other. If Sohrāb meets Rostam in battle, contrive that neither knows who the other is. Perhaps the young warrior will kill Rostam, and Iran will lose its strongest pillar; perhaps Rostam will kill the boy, and grief will break Rostam''s heart. Either way Tūrān gains.'' Hūmān and Bārmān took their orders and came to Samangān with gifts and a letter from Afrāsiyāb, and Sohrāb welcomed them gladly, knowing nothing of the plot behind them.','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.05','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.6','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',6,'白堡的女战士','The warrior maiden of the White Castle','苏赫拉布率领大军进入伊朗，来到边境上的白堡。守城的勇士哈吉尔出城迎战，几个回合就被苏赫拉布打下马来，成了俘虏。城中的人们惊慌失措。这时，城里有一位名叫戈尔达法丽德的少女，她是守将的女儿，勇敢而精通武艺。她把头发藏进头盔里，穿上铠甲，骑马出城，向苏赫拉布挑战。两人交战，她射出的箭像雨点一样密集，苏赫拉布用盾牌挡住，冲上前去，用长矛挑落了她的头盔，她的长发一下子披散下来，苏赫拉布这才发现对手是一个少女，心中又惊又爱。戈尔达法丽德见势不妙，就对他说：你若在两军面前和一个女子交战，会被人笑话的。不如我们讲和，城堡和城里的一切都归你，我在城中等你。苏赫拉布相信了她，跟到城门前，她却一闪身进了城，关上城门，在城墙上笑着对他说：图兰的勇士啊，你还是回去吧，等鲁斯塔姆来了，你就没有好日子过了。当天夜里，城里的人们从一条秘密的地道逃走了，第二天苏赫拉布攻进城时，城中已经空无一人。','Sohrāb led his army into Iran and came to the White Castle on the border. Its champion Hajīr rode out against him and was unhorsed and taken prisoner within a few passes. The people in the castle were in dismay. Among them was a maiden named Gordāfarid, daughter of the commander, brave and skilled in arms. She hid her hair in a helmet, put on armour, rode out and challenged Sohrāb. They fought; her arrows fell thick as rain, but Sohrāb took them on his shield, charged, and with his spear knocked off her helmet, and her long hair tumbled down. Only then did he see his foe was a maiden, and he was astonished and drawn to her. Seeing her peril, Gordāfarid said: ''You will be mocked if you fight a woman before both armies. Let us make peace: the castle and all in it are yours, and I will wait for you within.'' Sohrāb believed her and followed her to the gate, but she slipped inside, shut the gate, and laughed at him from the walls: ''Warrior of Tūrān, go home; when Rostam comes, your good days are over.'' That night the people of the castle escaped by a secret passage, and when Sohrāb stormed it the next day it was empty.','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.06','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.7','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',7,'国王的召唤','The king''s summons','白堡失守的消息传到凯·卡乌斯那里，国王惊恐万分，立刻写信给鲁斯塔姆，派勇士吉夫日夜兼程送到扎布尔斯坦，请他火速前来抵挡这个年轻的敌人。鲁斯塔姆读了信，听说图兰来了一个年轻的勇士，心中奇怪：图兰怎么会有这样的英雄？我只在萨曼甘有过一个孩子，可他还小，不可能是他。他并不着急，留吉夫在家中饮酒作乐，一连几天才出发。等他们来到凯·卡乌斯的宫廷，国王勃然大怒，骂鲁斯塔姆藐视王命，下令把他和吉夫一起吊死。鲁斯塔姆怒不可遏，说：我是伊朗的守护者，不是你的奴隶！你这次自己去对付那个年轻人吧。他骑上拉赫什扬长而去。众位勇士都慌了，年老的古达尔兹赶去追他，劝他说：人们会说鲁斯塔姆是怕了那个年轻人才走的。鲁斯塔姆听了，不愿背上胆怯的名声，就回到了宫中，凯·卡乌斯也向他道了歉。','When news of the White Castle''s fall reached Kay Kāvus, the king was terrified and at once wrote to Rostam, sending the champion Gīv riding day and night to Zābulistān to beg him to come with all speed against the young enemy. Reading the letter and hearing of a young champion from Tūrān, Rostam wondered: ''How could Tūrān have such a hero? I had only one child, in Samangān, and he is still small; it cannot be he.'' He was in no hurry, kept Gīv feasting at his house for several days, and only then set out. When they reached the court, Kay Kāvus flew into a rage, accused Rostam of scorning the royal command, and ordered him and Gīv hanged. Rostam was furious: ''I am the guardian of Iran, not your slave! Deal with the young man yourself this time.'' He mounted Rakhsh and rode off. The champions were dismayed; old Gūdarz rode after him and said: ''People will say Rostam left because he feared the young man.'' Unwilling to bear the name of coward, Rostam returned to court, and Kay Kāvus begged his pardon.','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.07','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.8','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',8,'营帐边的夜探','Night in the enemy camp','伊朗大军来到白堡前，与图兰军队隔着平原对阵。夜里，鲁斯塔姆换上图兰人的衣服，悄悄潜入敌营，想亲眼看看这个年轻的勇士。他看见苏赫拉布坐在宴席的主位上，身材高大，像一棵挺拔的柏树，相貌英武，鲁斯塔姆心中暗暗称奇。坐在苏赫拉布身边的，是塔米娜的兄弟赞德·拉兹姆，塔米娜派他随军，就是为了在战场上把鲁斯塔姆指认给苏赫拉布。赞德·拉兹姆出帐时，在暗处撞见了陌生的鲁斯塔姆，喝问他是谁，鲁斯塔姆一拳打在他的头上，把他打死了。唯一认得鲁斯塔姆的人，就这样死去了。第二天早晨，苏赫拉布登上高处，让俘虏哈吉尔把伊朗军中每一位勇士的帐篷指给他看，他特别问到一座绿色的大帐篷，帐前竖着一面绘着龙的旗帜，旁边拴着一匹高大的骏马。哈吉尔心想：如果说出这是鲁斯塔姆，这个年轻人一定会去找他决斗，也许会把他杀死。于是他撒谎说：那是一位从中国来的盟友，鲁斯塔姆不在军中，他在扎布尔斯坦过节。','The Iranian army came before the White Castle and faced the Turanians across the plain. At night Rostam put on Turanian clothes and stole into the enemy camp to see the young champion for himself. He saw Sohrāb seated at the head of the feast, tall and straight as a cypress, noble of face, and marvelled in his heart. Beside Sohrāb sat Zende-razm, Tahmineh''s brother, whom she had sent with the army to point out Rostam to Sohrāb on the field. As Zende-razm left the tent he met the stranger in the dark and challenged him; Rostam struck him on the head with his fist and killed him. So the one man who knew Rostam by sight was dead. In the morning Sohrāb climbed a height and had the captive Hajīr point out the tent of each Iranian champion; he asked especially about a great green tent with a banner painted with a dragon before it and a tall horse tethered beside it. Hajīr thought: if I say this is Rostam''s, the young man will seek him out and may kill him. So he lied: ''That is an ally from China; Rostam is not with the army; he is in Zābulistān for a festival.''','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.08','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.9','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',9,'第一天','The first day','苏赫拉布骑马冲到伊朗军前，大声挑战，又冲进营中，砍倒了凯·卡乌斯帐前的旗帜，伊朗的勇士们没有一个敢出来应战。凯·卡乌斯只好请鲁斯塔姆出战。鲁斯塔姆穿上虎皮战袍，骑着拉赫什来到两军之间。他看着眼前这个年轻人，心中涌起一种说不出的感情，对他说：年轻人，你还这样年轻，何必来送死？我见过许多战场，打败过无数魔鬼和勇士，我可怜你，不忍心杀你。苏赫拉布望着这位老英雄，心中也忽然生出一种奇怪的亲切感，问道：请你告诉我实话，你是不是鲁斯塔姆，扎尔的儿子？鲁斯塔姆为了不让对方知道自己的身份，回答说：我不是鲁斯塔姆，我只是他的一个手下，他是一位大英雄，而我连马厩都没有。苏赫拉布失望极了。两人用长矛、刀剑和铁杵交战，长矛断了，刀剑卷了刃，铁杵弯了，两人都汗流浃背，筋疲力尽，直到黄昏也分不出胜负，只好约定第二天再战。','Sohrāb rode up before the Iranian host and shouted his challenge, then charged into the camp and cut down the banner before Kay Kāvus''s tent, and not one Iranian champion dared come out. Kay Kāvus had to ask Rostam to fight. Rostam put on his tiger-skin coat and rode Rakhsh out between the armies. Looking at the young man, he felt a stirring he could not name, and said: ''Young man, you are still so young; why come to your death? I have seen many battlefields and overcome countless demons and champions; I pity you and would not kill you.'' Gazing at the old hero, Sohrāb too felt a strange kinship and asked: ''Tell me truly, are you Rostam, son of Zāl?'' To keep his name hidden, Rostam answered: ''I am not Rostam, only one of his men; he is a great hero, and I have not even a stable of my own.'' Sohrāb''s hope fell. They fought with spears, swords and maces until the spears broke, the swords were notched and the maces bent; both ran with sweat and were spent, and when evening came neither had won, and they agreed to fight again the next day.','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.09','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.10','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',10,'第二天的计谋','The ruse of the second day','第二天，苏赫拉布一见到鲁斯塔姆，就笑着迎上去说：昨夜你睡得好吗？我们为什么要打仗呢？我心里对你有说不出的亲近，我们放下武器，坐下来一起喝酒吧。告诉我，你真的不是鲁斯塔姆吗？鲁斯塔姆说：我们昨天约好了要摔跤，不要再说这些话了。两人下马摔跤，从早晨一直扭打到中午，苏赫拉布像一头狮子扑向猎物一样，把鲁斯塔姆摔倒在地，骑在他的胸口上，拔出匕首就要砍下他的头。鲁斯塔姆急中生智，说：年轻人，在我们伊朗有一个规矩：两个勇士摔跤，第一次把对手摔倒的人不能杀他，要第二次摔倒对手才能取他的性命。苏赫拉布年轻而骄傲，相信了这番话，就放开了他。回到营中，胡曼听说了这件事，责备苏赫拉布说：你放走了一头已经落进网里的狮子，你会为此后悔的。苏赫拉布却不以为意。','On the second day, as soon as Sohrāb saw Rostam he came to meet him smiling: ''Did you sleep well? Why should we fight? My heart feels a kinship with you I cannot explain; let us lay down our arms and sit and drink together. Tell me — are you truly not Rostam?'' Rostam said: ''We agreed yesterday to wrestle; say no more of this.'' They dismounted and wrestled from morning till noon, and Sohrāb, like a lion falling on its prey, threw Rostam to the ground, sat on his chest and drew his dagger to cut off his head. Quick-witted in his peril, Rostam said: ''Young man, among us in Iran there is a custom: when two champions wrestle, the one who first throws his foe may not kill him; only when he throws him a second time may he take his life.'' Young and proud, Sohrāb believed it and let him go. Back in camp Hūmān heard of it and rebuked him: ''You have let go a lion already in the net; you will regret it.'' But Sohrāb thought little of it.','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.10','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.11','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',11,'第三天','The third day','鲁斯塔姆脱身以后，来到一条溪边，洗净了身体，跪在地上向造物主祈祷。据说他年轻的时候力气大得惊人，走在岩石上，双脚都会陷进石头里，他曾经请求神收回一部分力量，好让他能像常人一样行走。现在，他祈求神把那份力量还给他。神应允了他的祈祷。第三天，两人又在战场上相遇，又一次摔起跤来。这一次，鲁斯塔姆恢复了全部的力量，他抓住苏赫拉布的头和腰，像拔起一棵树一样把他举起来，重重地摔在地上。他知道这个年轻人不会被压在地上太久，就迅速拔出匕首，刺进了苏赫拉布的胸膛。苏赫拉布在血泊中翻过身来，叹息着说：这是我自作自受，命运把我的钥匙交到了你的手里。可是你要知道，无论你逃到哪里，就算你变成水里的鱼、天上的星，我的父亲也会找到你，为我报仇。我的父亲就是鲁斯塔姆。我来这里，只是为了找他。','Having escaped, Rostam went to a stream, washed himself, knelt and prayed to the Creator. It is told that in his youth his strength had been so great that when he walked on rock his feet sank into the stone, and he had asked God to take back part of it so that he could walk like other men. Now he prayed God to give that strength back, and God granted his prayer. On the third day they met again and wrestled once more. This time, with all his strength restored, Rostam seized Sohrāb by the head and waist, lifted him like an uprooted tree and dashed him to the ground. Knowing the young man would not stay down long, he drew his dagger and drove it into Sohrāb''s breast. Sohrāb turned over in his blood and sighed: ''I have brought this on myself; fate gave the key of my life into your hand. But know this: wherever you flee, though you become a fish in the sea or a star in the sky, my father will find you and avenge me. My father is Rostam. I came here only to find him.''','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.11','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.persian_zimmern_rustem_sohrab.12','storyver.persian.zimmern_rustem_sohrab',12,'长生药与哀悼','The elixir and the mourning','鲁斯塔姆听见这句话，眼前一黑，昏倒在地。醒来后，他颤抖着问：你有什么能证明你是鲁斯塔姆的儿子？我就是鲁斯塔姆！苏赫拉布说：解开我的铠甲，看看我的手臂。鲁斯塔姆解开铠甲，看见了那枚自己当年留给塔米娜的臂环。他撕扯着自己的衣服和头发，痛哭失声，说：我亲手杀死了我的儿子。苏赫拉布说：哭也没有用了，这是命中注定的。鲁斯塔姆派古达尔兹去见凯·卡乌斯，请求国王赐给他宫中珍藏的长生药，能治好一切创伤。凯·卡乌斯却心想：如果这个年轻人活下来，父子二人联手，我的王位还能保得住吗？他迟迟不肯给药。鲁斯塔姆决定亲自去求，还没有走到国王那里，就有人追上来报告：苏赫拉布已经死了。鲁斯塔姆悲痛欲绝，想用匕首自尽，被众人拦住。他烧掉了自己的帐篷和宝座，用金色的锦缎包裹儿子的遗体，带回扎布尔斯坦安葬。消息传到萨曼甘，塔米娜撕碎了衣裳，日夜痛哭，抱着儿子的战袍和骏马，不到一年，就随着儿子离开了人世。','At these words the world went dark before Rostam''s eyes and he fell senseless. When he came to, he asked trembling: ''What token have you that you are Rostam''s son? I am Rostam!'' Sohrāb said: ''Undo my armour and look at my arm.'' Rostam undid the armour and saw the armlet he had once left with Tahmineh. He tore his clothes and his hair and cried aloud: ''I have killed my own son with my own hand.'' Sohrāb said: ''Weeping is useless now; this was fated.'' Rostam sent Gūdarz to Kay Kāvus to beg the elixir kept in the king''s treasury that heals every wound. But Kay Kāvus thought: if this young man lives and father and son join together, will my throne be safe? and he delayed. Rostam resolved to go and beg it himself, but before he reached the king a messenger overtook him: Sohrāb was dead. In his grief Rostam would have killed himself with his dagger, but the others held him back. He burned his tent and his throne, wrapped his son''s body in golden brocade and carried it home to Zābulistān for burial. When the news reached Samangān, Tahmineh tore her clothes and wept day and night, clasping her son''s armour and his horse, and within a year she followed her son out of the world.','claim.v0350.shn_sohrab.12','齐默恩《列王纪》转述本“鲁斯塔姆与苏赫拉布”一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.1','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',1,'董永与织女','Dong Yong and the Weaving Maiden','汉朝的时候，千乘县有一个人叫董永。他从小失去了母亲，和父亲相依为命，在田里拼命干活，干活时用一辆小鹿车拉着父亲跟在身边。父亲去世了，董永穷得连下葬的钱都没有，就把自己卖给别人做奴仆，用卖身的钱为父亲办了丧事。买他的主人知道他是个贤孝的人，给了他一万钱，让他回家去。董永为父亲守完三年的孝，就动身去主人家，准备履行做奴仆的义务。路上遇到一个女子，对他说：我愿意做你的妻子。两人就一起去了。主人说：钱已经送给你了，你不必来了。董永说：承蒙您的恩惠，我父亲才得以安葬。我虽然是个小人物，也一定要尽力干活，报答您的厚恩。主人问：你的妻子会做什么？董永说：会织布。主人说：既然这样，就让你妻子为我织一百匹细绢吧。董永的妻子就在主人家织绢，十天就织完了。她走出门，对董永说：我是天上的织女。因为你极其孝顺，天帝派我来帮你还债。说完，凌空而去，再也不知道她去了哪里。','In the Han dynasty there was a man of Qiancheng named Dong Yong. He lost his mother young and lived with his father, labouring hard in the fields with his father riding beside him in a little deer-cart. When his father died, Dong Yong was too poor to bury him, so he sold himself as a bondservant and used the price for the funeral. His master, knowing him to be worthy and filial, gave him ten thousand cash and sent him home. When Dong Yong had finished three years of mourning, he set out for his master''s house to fulfil his bond. On the road he met a woman who said: ''I wish to be your wife,'' and they went on together. The master said: ''I gave you the money; you need not come.'' Dong Yong said: ''By your kindness my father was buried. Humble as I am, I must work with all my strength to repay you.'' ''What can your wife do?'' ''She can weave.'' ''If so, let her weave me a hundred bolts of fine silk.'' His wife wove in the master''s house and finished in ten days. Coming out of the gate she said to Dong Yong: ''I am the Weaving Maiden of heaven. Because of your great filial piety, the Lord of Heaven sent me to help you repay your debt.'' With that she rose into the sky, and no one knew where she had gone.','claim.v0350.soushenji.01','《搜神记》卷一“董永”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.2','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',2,'干将莫邪','Gan Jiang and Mo Ye','楚国的干将和莫邪为楚王铸剑，用了三年才铸成。楚王嫌他们太慢，十分恼怒，想要杀掉他们。剑铸成了一对，一雄一雌。那时莫邪怀着身孕，快要生产了。干将对妻子说：我为楚王铸剑，三年才成，楚王已经发怒，我去了他一定会杀我。你若生的是男孩，等他长大了，告诉他：出了家门望南山，松树长在石头上，剑就在它的背后。说完，干将就带着雌剑去见楚王。楚王大怒，叫人察看那把剑，相剑的人说：剑本来有两把，一雄一雌，现在雌剑来了，雄剑却没有来。楚王更加愤怒，立刻杀了干将。莫邪后来生下一个儿子，取名赤比。赤比长大以后，问母亲：我的父亲在哪里？母亲说：你父亲为楚王铸剑，三年才成，楚王发怒，把他杀了。他走的时候嘱咐我告诉你：出了家门望南山，松树长在石头上，剑就在它的背后。赤比走出门向南望去，并没有看见山，只看见堂屋前松木柱子下面的石墩。他用斧头劈开柱子的背面，果然得到了那把雄剑。从此，他日夜想着要向楚王报仇。','Gan Jiang and Mo Ye of Chu forged swords for the king of Chu, and it took them three years. The king, angry at the delay, meant to kill them. The swords were a pair, male and female. Mo Ye was then with child and near her time. Gan Jiang said to his wife: ''I took three years to forge the king''s swords; he is angry, and if I go he will surely kill me. If you bear a son, when he is grown tell him: go out of the door and look at the southern mountain; a pine grows on a stone; the sword is at its back.'' Then he took the female sword to the king. The king, furious, had the sword examined, and the examiner said: ''There should be two swords, a male and a female; the female has come, but the male has not.'' Angrier still, the king had Gan Jiang killed at once. Mo Ye bore a son named Chi Bi. When he was grown he asked his mother: ''Where is my father?'' She said: ''Your father forged swords for the king of Chu; it took three years, the king was angry and killed him. When he left he told me to tell you: go out of the door and look at the southern mountain; a pine grows on a stone; the sword is at its back.'' Chi Bi went out and looked south, but saw no mountain — only the stone base beneath the pine pillar in front of the hall. He split the back of the pillar with an axe and found the male sword. From then on he thought day and night of vengeance on the king of Chu.','claim.v0350.soushenji.02','《搜神记》卷十一“三王墓”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.3','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',3,'三王墓','The Tomb of the Three Kings','楚王梦见一个少年，两眉之间宽一尺，说要来报仇。楚王就悬赏千金捉拿他。赤比听说了，逃进山里，一边走一边悲歌。有一位陌生的侠客遇见他，问：你年纪这么小，为什么哭得这样悲伤？赤比说：我是干将莫邪的儿子，楚王杀了我父亲，我要报仇。侠客说：听说楚王悬赏千金要你的头，你把头和剑交给我，我替你报仇。赤比说：太好了！就自刎而死，两手捧着自己的头和剑交给侠客，身体直挺挺地站着不倒。侠客说：我不会辜负你的。尸体这才倒下。侠客提着头去见楚王，楚王大喜。侠客说：这是勇士的头，应当放在汤镬里煮烂。楚王照办了。头煮了三天三夜，也没有煮烂，还从沸水中跳出来，瞪着眼睛，满脸怒气。侠客说：这孩子的头煮不烂，请大王亲自到锅边看一看，就一定会烂了。楚王走到锅边，侠客挥剑一砍，楚王的头掉进了汤里；侠客又挥剑砍下自己的头，头也掉进了汤里。三颗头一起煮烂，再也分辨不出是谁的。人们只好把汤和肉分成三份埋葬，统称为“三王墓”。书中说，墓在汝南北宜春县境内。','The king of Chu dreamed of a boy with a foot''s breadth between his brows who said he would take vengeance, and the king offered a thousand pieces of gold for him. Hearing of it, Chi Bi fled into the mountains, singing sorrowfully as he went. A stranger met him and asked: ''You are so young; why do you weep so bitterly?'' ''I am the son of Gan Jiang and Mo Ye. The king of Chu killed my father, and I would avenge him.'' The stranger said: ''I hear the king has offered a thousand gold for your head. Give me your head and your sword, and I will avenge you.'' ''Gladly!'' said Chi Bi; he cut his own throat and, holding up his head and sword in both hands, offered them, his body standing rigid. ''I will not fail you,'' said the stranger, and only then did the body fall. The stranger took the head to the king, who was delighted. ''This is the head of a hero,'' said the stranger; ''it should be boiled in a cauldron.'' The king did so. For three days and nights it boiled without softening, and it leapt out of the water, glaring in fury. ''The boy''s head will not soften,'' said the stranger; ''let Your Majesty come and look at it, and it surely will.'' The king went to the cauldron; the stranger swung the sword and the king''s head fell into the soup; then he cut off his own head, and it too fell in. The three heads boiled away together until none could be told apart, so the soup and flesh were divided and buried in three graves, called together the Tomb of the Three Kings. It lies, the text says, in the bounds of Northern Yichun county in Runan.','claim.v0350.soushenji.03','《搜神记》卷十一“三王墓”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.4','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',4,'范巨卿与张元伯','Fan Juqing and Zhang Yuanbo','汉朝的范式，字巨卿，是山阳金乡人，和汝南的张劭是好朋友，张劭字元伯。两人一同在太学读书。后来各自告假回乡，范式对张劭说：两年以后我会回来，到你家拜见你的父母，看看你的孩子。两人约定了日期。约期快到的时候，张劭把这件事告诉母亲，请母亲准备饭菜等候客人。母亲说：两年前的分别，千里之外的一句话，你怎么就这样当真呢？张劭说：巨卿是守信的人，一定不会失约。母亲说：既然这样，我就为你酿酒吧。到了那一天，范式果然来了，登堂拜见，畅饮尽欢而别。后来张劭得了重病去世。范式忽然梦见张劭戴着黑色的礼帽，帽缨垂下，趿着鞋子来叫他：巨卿，我某日死了，将在某时下葬，永远回到黄泉。你若没有忘记我，还能赶得上吗？范式惊醒，悲伤地流下眼泪，穿上为朋友服丧的衣服，赶在下葬的日子飞奔而去。他还没有赶到，送葬的队伍已经出发。到了墓穴边，正要下葬，灵柩却怎么也推不动。张劭的母亲抚着棺木说：元伯，难道你还在盼着谁吗？就把灵柩停了下来。过了一会儿，只见一辆白车、一匹白马号哭着奔来。母亲远远望见，说：这一定是范巨卿。范式来到灵前，叩头说：走吧，元伯！死生不同路，从此永别了。送葬的上千人都流下了眼泪。范式亲手执绋牵引，灵柩这才向前移动。','In the Han dynasty Fan Shi, styled Juqing, of Jinxiang in Shanyang, was a close friend of Zhang Shao of Runan, styled Yuanbo. They studied together at the Imperial Academy. When they took leave to go home, Fan said to Zhang: ''In two years I will come back and visit your parents and see your children,'' and they fixed the day. As the day drew near Zhang told his mother and asked her to prepare food for the guest. She said: ''A parting two years ago, a word spoken a thousand li away — how can you trust it so?'' ''Juqing is a man of his word; he will not fail.'' ''Then I will brew wine for you.'' On the day, Fan came, paid his respects in the hall, drank happily and left. Later Zhang fell gravely ill and died. Fan suddenly dreamed of Zhang in a black ceremonial cap with hanging tassels, slippers on his feet, calling: ''Juqing, I died on such a day and shall be buried at such a time, returning for ever to the Yellow Springs. If you have not forgotten me, can you still come in time?'' Fan woke weeping, put on the mourning dress for a friend and galloped off to reach the burial. Before he arrived the cortège had set out; at the grave, as the coffin was to be lowered, it would not move. Zhang''s mother stroked it and said: ''Yuanbo, are you waiting for someone?'' and they halted. After a while a white carriage drawn by a white horse came wailing. Seeing it, the mother said: ''That must be Fan Juqing.'' Fan knelt before the coffin: ''Go now, Yuanbo! Life and death take different roads; farewell for ever.'' The thousand mourners all wept. Fan took the rope and drew the coffin, and only then did it move forward.','claim.v0350.soushenji.04','《搜神记》卷十一“范式”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.5','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',5,'东海孝妇','The filial widow of Donghai','汉朝的时候，东海郡有一位孝顺的媳妇，奉养婆婆非常尽心。婆婆说：媳妇奉养我这样辛苦，我已经老了，何必吝惜剩下的岁月，长久拖累年轻人呢？就上吊自尽了。婆婆的女儿到官府告状，说：是这个媳妇杀了我母亲。官府把她抓起来，严刑拷打，孝妇受不了酷刑，只好屈打成招。当时，于公在郡里做狱吏，说：这个媳妇奉养婆婆十年，孝顺的名声远近皆知，一定不会杀人。太守不听。于公据理力争也没有用，抱着她的案卷在官府痛哭了一场，辞职而去。此后，郡中大旱，三年不下雨。后来新的太守到任，于公对他说：孝妇不该死，前任太守冤杀了她，灾祸的原因就在这里。太守立刻亲自去祭拜孝妇的坟墓，并为她立碑表彰。天上立刻下起雨来，那一年获得了大丰收。书中又记下了老人们的传说：孝妇名叫周青，临刑前，用车载着一根十丈长的竹竿，上面挂着五面旗幡，她当众发誓：我周青如果有罪，甘愿被杀，血会顺着往下流；如果我是冤死的，血就会逆流而上。行刑以后，她的血呈青黄色，顺着旗杆一直流到了竿顶，又顺着旗幡流了下来。','In the Han dynasty there was in Donghai commandery a filial daughter-in-law who served her mother-in-law with great devotion. The mother-in-law said: ''She works so hard to care for me; I am old — why cling to my remaining years and burden the young?'' and hanged herself. The old woman''s daughter went to the authorities: ''This daughter-in-law killed my mother.'' The officials seized her and tortured her cruelly, and unable to bear it she falsely confessed. Lord Yu, then a prison clerk of the commandery, said: ''This woman served her mother-in-law for ten years and is known everywhere for her filial piety; she would never kill her.'' The governor would not listen. Unable to prevail, Lord Yu wept at the office clasping the record of her case and resigned. Thereafter the commandery suffered drought, with no rain for three years. When a new governor came, Lord Yu told him: ''The filial widow should not have died; the former governor killed her unjustly, and that is the cause of the calamity.'' The governor at once went himself to sacrifice at her grave and set up a memorial to her. Rain fell immediately, and that year brought a great harvest. The text adds what the elders told: her name was Zhou Qing; before her execution she had a carriage bear a bamboo pole ten zhang long hung with five banners, and swore before the crowd: ''If I am guilty, let me be killed and my blood flow down; if I die unjustly, let my blood flow upward.'' After the execution her blood, blue-yellow, ran up the pole to its top and then down along the banners.','claim.v0350.soushenji.05','《搜神记》卷十一“东海孝妇”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.6','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',6,'韩凭夫妇','Han Ping and his wife','宋康王的舍人韩凭，娶了何氏为妻，何氏非常美丽，宋康王就把她夺了去。韩凭心怀怨恨，康王把他关进牢里，罚他去做筑城的苦役。何氏偷偷给韩凭送去一封信，故意写得很隐晦：雨水绵绵，河大水深，太阳照着心。康王得到了这封信，给左右的人看，谁也看不懂。大臣苏贺解释说：雨水绵绵，是说她又愁又想；河大水深，是说彼此不能来往；太阳照着心，是说她心里已经有了寻死的念头。不久，韩凭就自杀了。何氏暗中把自己的衣服弄得腐烂。康王和她一起登上高台，她纵身跳下，左右的人去拉她，衣服一扯就碎，没有拉住，她就摔死了。她在衣带上留下遗书：大王想让我活着，我却愿意死去。希望把我的尸骨赐给韩凭，与他合葬。康王大怒，偏不答应，让同乡人把他们分开埋葬，两座坟遥遥相望。康王说：你们夫妻相爱不止，如果能让两座坟合在一起，我就不再阻拦。一夜之间，两座坟头各长出一棵大梓树，十天就粗得要两人合抱，树干弯曲着彼此靠近，树根在地下交缠，树枝在上面交错。又有一对鸳鸯，一雌一雄，常常栖息在树上，早晚都不离开，交颈悲鸣，声音令人感动。宋国人为他们哀伤，就把这两棵树叫作“相思树”。','Han Ping, a retainer of King Kang of Song, married a woman of the He family who was very beautiful, and King Kang took her from him. When Han Ping showed his resentment, the king imprisoned him and sentenced him to hard labour on the city walls. His wife secretly sent him a letter, its words deliberately obscure: ''The rain falls long; the river is wide and the water deep; the sun rising strikes the heart.'' The king obtained the letter and showed it to his courtiers, but none could understand it. The minister Su He explained: ''The rain falls long — she grieves and longs; the river is wide and the water deep — they cannot reach each other; the sun strikes the heart — in her heart she resolves to die.'' Soon after, Han Ping killed himself. His wife secretly rotted her clothes. When the king took her up a high terrace, she threw herself off; the attendants grabbed at her, but the cloth tore in their hands, and she died. In her sash she left a letter: ''The king wanted me to live; I wanted to die. I beg that my bones be given to Han Ping to be buried with him.'' The king, furious, refused, and had the villagers bury them apart, the two graves facing each other. ''If you two love each other so,'' said the king, ''make your graves join, and I will not stop you.'' Overnight a great catalpa grew from the head of each grave; in ten days each was a full armspan thick, and they bent towards each other, roots entwined below and branches interlaced above. A pair of mandarin ducks, male and female, roosted always in the trees, never leaving morning or evening, necks entwined, crying mournfully. The people of Song pitied them and called the trees the Trees of Longing.','claim.v0350.soushenji.06','《搜神记》卷十一“韩凭夫妇”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.7','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',7,'嫦娥奔月','Chang''e flees to the moon','后羿向西王母求得了长生不死的药，他的妻子姮娥——后来人们多写作嫦娥——偷了这药，奔向月亮。临走之前，她找到一位名叫有黄的占卜者，请他用蓍草为自己占一卦。有黄占卜以后说：吉利。轻盈飘逸的嫁出的女子，独自将要向西远行。路上会遇到天色昏暗，不要害怕，不要惊慌，以后将会大大昌盛。于是姮娥就把自己托身于月亮之中，成了月亮里的蟾蜍。这则记载在《搜神记》里只有寥寥数语，却是后来嫦娥奔月故事最早的几种文字见证之一。书中说她偷药之后化为蟾蜍，与后世“月宫仙子”的美丽形象并不一样；那位占卜者的卦辞，似乎在说这次西行虽然艰险，却会有好的结果。至于后羿为什么去求药、嫦娥为什么偷药，书中都没有说，后来的种种解释，都是在这几句话的空白处生长出来的。','Yi obtained from the Queen Mother of the West the drug of deathlessness, and his wife Heng''e — later more often written Chang''e — stole it and fled to the moon. Before she went she found a diviner named Youhuang and asked him to cast the yarrow stalks for her. Having divined, Youhuang said: ''Auspicious. The light-winged bride goes west alone. She will meet darkness in the sky; let her not fear, let her not be startled; afterwards she shall greatly prosper.'' So Heng''e entrusted herself to the moon and became the toad in the moon. The entry in the Soushen ji is only a few lines, yet it is among the earliest written witnesses to the story of Chang''e''s flight to the moon. Here, having stolen the drug, she becomes a toad — unlike the lovely moon-fairy of later times; and the diviner''s words seem to say that the westward journey, though dark, will end well. Why Yi sought the drug and why Chang''e stole it, the text does not say; all the later explanations have grown in the silence around these few lines.','claim.v0350.soushenji.07','《搜神记》卷十四“姮娥奔月”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.8','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',8,'女化蚕','The girl who became a silkworm','古老的传说讲：远古的时候，有一个人出远门去征战，家里没有别人，只剩下一个女儿和一匹公马，女儿亲自喂养这匹马。她独自住在偏僻的地方，思念父亲，就跟马开玩笑说：你要是能把我父亲接回来，我就嫁给你。马听了这句话，挣断缰绳跑走了，一直跑到父亲那里。父亲看见马，又惊又喜，就骑上了它。马望着来的方向，不停地悲鸣。父亲说：这匹马无缘无故这样，家里莫非出了什么事？急忙骑马赶回了家。父亲觉得这匹马有非同寻常的情义，就用好草好料喂它，马却不肯吃，每次看见女儿进出，就又喜又怒地跳跃踢打，不止一次。父亲觉得奇怪，悄悄问女儿，女儿把实情告诉了他。父亲说：不要声张，免得辱没家门。你也不要再进出了。于是他埋下弓弩射死了马，把马皮晒在院子里。父亲出门后，女儿和邻家的女孩在马皮旁边玩耍，用脚踢着马皮说：你是畜生，还想娶人做妻子？落得被剥皮的下场，何苦呢！话还没说完，马皮忽然跳了起来，卷起女孩就跑。几天以后，人们在一棵大树的枝间找到了女孩和马皮，都已经变成了蚕，在树上吐丝结茧。邻家的妇人把茧取回去养，收成比普通的蚕多好几倍。人们就把那种树叫作“桑”，“桑”就是“丧”的意思。','An old tale says: in high antiquity a man went far away to war, leaving at home only his daughter and a stallion, which she tended herself. Living alone in a remote place and missing her father, she said to the horse in jest: ''If you can bring my father home, I will marry you.'' At these words the horse broke its halter and ran off straight to her father. Surprised and glad, the father mounted it. The horse gazed back the way it had come, neighing mournfully without end. ''This horse would not act so for nothing,'' said the father; ''has something happened at home?'' and he rode home in haste. Thinking the horse had uncommon feeling, he fed it the best fodder, but it would not eat; whenever it saw the girl come or go it leapt and kicked in joy and rage, again and again. Wondering, the father asked his daughter privately, and she told him everything. ''Say nothing,'' he said, ''lest the family be shamed; and do not go in and out.'' He set a crossbow and shot the horse, and spread its hide to dry in the courtyard. While he was away, the girl played with a neighbour''s daughter beside the hide, kicking it: ''You beast, wanting a human wife! You got yourself flayed for it — why bring such misery on yourself?'' Before she had finished, the hide sprang up, wrapped her round and carried her away. Days later she and the hide were found among the branches of a great tree, both turned into silkworms spinning cocoons on the tree. A neighbour woman took the cocoons and raised them, and her yield was many times that of ordinary silkworms. So the tree was named sang, the mulberry — sang, that is, sorrow.','claim.v0350.soushenji.08','《搜神记》卷十四“女化蚕”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.9','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',9,'毛衣女','The women in feather robes','豫章郡新喻县有一个男子，看见田里有六七个女子，都穿着羽毛做的衣服，他不知道她们其实是鸟。他悄悄爬过去，拿到其中一个女子脱下的羽衣，把它藏了起来，然后走向那些鸟。鸟儿们各自飞走了，只有一只没有了羽衣，飞不起来。男子就娶了她做妻子，生了三个女儿。后来，母亲让女儿去问父亲，得知羽衣藏在稻谷堆的下面，就找到了它，穿上羽衣飞走了。后来她又回来迎接三个女儿，女儿们也都飞走了。这则故事在《搜神记》里只有短短几行，却是中国关于“羽衣仙女”最早的记载之一：被藏起的衣服、被迫的婚姻、找回羽衣以后的离去，这些情节后来在许多地方的传说里一再出现，人们常把它与世界各地的“天鹅处女”故事相比。书中没有说她临走时说了什么，也没有说那个男子后来怎样，只留下母亲回来把女儿们一个个接走的结局。','A man of Xinyu county in Yuzhang saw six or seven women in a field, all in robes of feathers, not knowing that they were birds. He crept up, took the feather robe one of them had taken off, hid it, and then went towards the birds. The birds all flew away, but one could not fly without her robe. The man took her as his wife, and she bore him three daughters. Later the mother had her daughters ask their father, learned that the robe was hidden beneath the stacked grain, found it, put it on and flew away. Afterwards she came back for her three daughters, and they too flew away. The tale is only a few lines in the Soushen ji, yet it is among the earliest Chinese records of the feather-robed maiden: the hidden garment, the forced marriage, the flight once the robe is found — motifs that recur in later legends across China and are often compared with swan-maiden tales the world over. The text does not say what she said as she left, nor what became of the man; it leaves only the ending in which the mother comes back and takes her daughters away one by one.','claim.v0350.soushenji.09','《搜神记》卷十四“毛衣女”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.10','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',10,'紫玉与韩重','Ziyu and Han Zhong','吴王夫差的小女儿名叫紫玉，十八岁，才华和容貌都很出众。有一个少年叫韩重，十九岁，懂得道术。紫玉爱慕他，两人私下书信往来，紫玉答应嫁给他。韩重要到齐鲁一带去求学，临走前托父母去向吴王求婚。吴王大怒，不肯把女儿嫁给他。紫玉郁结而死，葬在阊门之外。三年后韩重回来，问父母求婚的事，父母说：吴王大怒，紫玉郁结而死，已经下葬了。韩重痛哭，带着祭品到墓前吊唁。紫玉的魂魄从墓中出来，流着泪对他说：你走以后，你让父母向父王求婚，我以为一定能如愿，没想到分别以后遭此命运，又能怎样呢！她扭过头，弯着颈子唱道：南山有乌鸦，北山张罗网；乌鸦已经高飞去，罗网又能怎么样！她邀韩重进入坟墓，韩重说：死生不同路，我怕有罪过，不敢从命。紫玉说：死生不同路，我也知道；可是今天一别，就永远不会再见了。你是怕我是鬼会害你吗？韩重被她的话感动，跟她进了坟墓，住了三天三夜。临别时，紫玉拿出一颗一寸大的明珠送给他。韩重去见吴王，说出了这件事，吴王大怒，以为他盗墓，要抓他。紫玉的魂魄出现在正在梳妆的吴王面前，跪着替韩重辩白。吴王的夫人闻声出来抱她，紫玉却像烟一样消散了。','Ziyu, youngest daughter of King Fuchai of Wu, was eighteen, outstanding in talent and beauty. A youth named Han Zhong, nineteen, was versed in the arts of the Way. Ziyu loved him; they exchanged letters in secret, and she promised to be his wife. Han Zhong went to study in Qi and Lu, and before leaving asked his parents to seek her hand from the king. The king was furious and refused. Ziyu, her grief pent up within her, died and was buried outside the Chang Gate. Three years later Han Zhong came back and asked his parents about the match. They said: ''The king was furious; Ziyu died of grief and is already buried.'' Weeping bitterly, Han Zhong took offerings to mourn at her tomb. Ziyu''s spirit came out of the grave and said in tears: ''After you left, I thought your parents'' suit to my father would surely succeed; who could have known what fate awaited me after we parted!'' Turning her head and bending her neck she sang: ''A crow on the southern hill, a net spread on the northern hill; the crow has flown high — what can the net do now?'' She asked him into the tomb. ''Life and death take different roads,'' he said; ''I fear to offend; I dare not.'' ''I know that life and death take different roads,'' she said, ''but once we part today we shall never meet again. Do you fear that as a ghost I will harm you?'' Moved, he followed her into the tomb and stayed three days and nights. At parting she gave him a bright pearl an inch across. When Han Zhong went to the king and told what had happened, the king raged, thinking he had robbed the grave, and ordered his arrest. Ziyu''s spirit appeared before the king as he dressed his hair and knelt to clear Han Zhong. The queen, hearing her, came out to embrace her, but Ziyu vanished like smoke.','claim.v0350.soushenji.10','《搜神记》卷十六“紫玉”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.11','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',11,'宋定伯卖鬼','Song Dingbo sells a ghost','南阳有个叫宋定伯的人，年轻的时候，有一次夜里赶路，遇到了一个鬼。他问对方是谁，鬼说：我是鬼。鬼反问：你又是谁？宋定伯骗它说：我也是鬼。鬼问：你要到哪里去？宋定伯说：要到宛城的集市去。鬼说：我也要去宛城的集市。两个就一起走了几里路。鬼说：走路太慢了，我们轮流背着走，怎么样？宋定伯说：好极了。鬼就先背宋定伯走了几里，说：你太重了，莫非不是鬼吧？宋定伯说：我是新死的鬼，所以身子重。轮到宋定伯背鬼，鬼几乎没有一点重量。这样轮换了好几次。宋定伯又说：我是新鬼，不知道鬼有什么害怕和忌讳的？鬼说：只是不喜欢人的唾沫。两个继续往前走，路上遇到一条河，宋定伯让鬼先过，听上去一点声音也没有；宋定伯自己过河，水声哗哗作响。鬼又问：你过河怎么有声音？宋定伯说：我新死不久，还不习惯过水，你别见怪。快到宛城集市时，宋定伯把鬼扛在肩上，紧紧抓住它。鬼大叫起来，声音咋咋地响，要他放自己下来，宋定伯不理它，一直走到集市中间，才把它放在地上，鬼变成了一只羊。宋定伯就把羊卖了，怕它再变化，还朝它吐了唾沫，得了一千五百钱才离开。当时石崇有一句话：定伯卖鬼，得钱千五。','Song Dingbo of Nanyang, when he was young, was walking at night and met a ghost. He asked who it was, and it said: ''I am a ghost. And who are you?'' Dingbo lied: ''I am a ghost too.'' ''Where are you going?'' ''To the market at Wan.'' ''So am I.'' So they went on together for some li. The ghost said: ''Walking is too slow; let us take turns carrying each other.'' ''Excellent,'' said Dingbo. The ghost carried him first for some li and said: ''You are very heavy — are you not a ghost?'' ''I am newly dead,'' said Dingbo, ''so my body is heavy.'' When Dingbo carried the ghost it weighed almost nothing. So they took turns several times. Dingbo said: ''Being a new ghost, I do not know what ghosts fear or avoid.'' ''Only human spittle,'' said the ghost. They went on and came to a river. Dingbo had the ghost cross first and heard no sound at all; when he crossed himself the water splashed noisily. ''Why do you make a sound?'' asked the ghost. ''I died only lately and am not used to crossing water; do not think it strange.'' As they neared the market of Wan, Dingbo hoisted the ghost onto his shoulder and held it fast. The ghost shrieked and begged to be put down, but Dingbo paid no heed and went straight into the market, where he set it down and it turned into a sheep. He sold the sheep, spitting on it lest it change again, and went off with fifteen hundred cash. As Shi Chong said at the time: ''Dingbo sold a ghost and got fifteen hundred.''','claim.v0350.soushenji.11','《搜神记》卷十六“宋定伯”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.12','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',12,'李寄斩蛇','Li Ji slays the serpent','东越闽中有一座庸岭，高几十里，岭的西北山谷里住着一条大蛇，长七八丈，粗十几围，当地人都很害怕。东冶的都尉和属县的官员，有很多被它害死。人们用牛羊祭祀，也得不到平安。大蛇有时托梦给人，有时让巫祝传话，说要吃十二三岁的童女。官员们都很忧虑，可是灾祸一直不止，只好四处寻找婢女所生的女孩和犯罪人家的女儿，养起来，到八月初一祭祀时，送到蛇洞口，大蛇出来把她们吞吃。这样年复一年，已经用了九个女孩。那一年又要征募，还没有找到人。将乐县的李诞家有六个女儿，没有儿子，最小的女儿名叫李寄，自愿应征。父母不答应。李寄说：父母命苦，只生了六个女儿，没有一个儿子，有我们和没有一样。女儿不能像缇萦那样救父母，既不能供养你们，又白白耗费衣食，活着没有益处，不如早点死。卖了我的身子，可以得到一点钱来供养父母，岂不是好事？父母疼爱她，始终不肯让她去，李寄就偷偷地走了。她向官府要了一把好剑和一条会咬蛇的狗。到了八月初一，她坐在庙里，怀里揣着剑，牵着狗。她先把几石米做成的糍粑浇上蜂蜜和炒面，放在洞口。大蛇出来了，头大得像谷仓，眼睛像二尺宽的铜镜，闻到糍粑的香味，就先去吃糍粑。李寄放出狗，狗扑上去撕咬，李寄从后面挥剑砍了好几下。大蛇痛得冲出洞来，到了庙前的空地上就死了。李寄走进洞里察看，找到了那九个女孩的骷髅，全部拿了出来，叹息道：你们胆怯软弱，被蛇吃掉，真是可怜。说完，她慢慢地走回家去。越王听说了，聘李寄为王后，任命她的父亲为将乐县令，母亲和姐姐们也都得到了赏赐。从此，东冶再也没有妖邪之物，歌颂她的歌谣至今还在流传。','In Minzhong of Eastern Yue there is a ridge called Yongling, tens of li high, and in a valley to its northwest lived a great serpent seven or eight zhang long and more than ten spans round, which the local people dreaded. Many of the commandants of Dongye and the officials of its counties died because of it. Sacrifices of oxen and sheep brought no peace. The serpent made known, now in dreams and now through shamans, that it wished to eat a girl of twelve or thirteen. The officials were troubled, but the evil did not cease, so they sought out daughters of bondmaids and of criminals, reared them, and on the first day of the eighth month sent one to the mouth of the serpent''s cave, and it came out and devoured her. So it went year after year, until nine girls had been given. That year they sought again and had found no one. Li Dan of Jiangle county had six daughters and no son; the youngest, named Li Ji, volunteered. Her parents refused. Li Ji said: ''My parents are unlucky: six daughters and no son — it is as if you had none. I cannot save my parents as Tiying did; I cannot support you and only waste food and clothing; my life is no use, and I had better die early. Selling my body will bring a little money to support you — is that not good?'' Out of love her parents still would not let her go, so she slipped away in secret. She asked the officials for a good sword and a dog that could bite snakes. On the first of the eighth month she sat in the shrine with the sword in her bosom and the dog beside her. First she set at the cave mouth several piculs of rice cakes soaked in honey and parched flour. The serpent came out, its head as big as a granary and its eyes like two-foot mirrors, and smelling the cakes it fell to eating them. Li Ji loosed the dog, which seized and bit it, and she struck it from behind several times with the sword. In agony the serpent burst out into the shrine courtyard and died. Li Ji went into the cave and found the skulls of the nine girls; she brought them all out and sighed: ''You were timid and weak, and the serpent devoured you — how pitiful.'' Then she walked slowly home. The king of Yue, hearing of it, took Li Ji as his queen, made her father magistrate of Jiangle, and gave gifts to her mother and sisters. From then on there were no more evil creatures in Dongye, and songs about her are sung to this day.','claim.v0350.soushenji.12','《搜神记》卷十九“李寄”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.chinese_soushenji_selected_13.13','storyver.chinese.soushenji_selected_13',13,'义犬冢','The tomb of the faithful dog','三国孙权的时候，襄阳纪南有一个人叫李信纯，家里养了一条狗，名叫黑龙，他非常喜爱这条狗，走到哪里都带着它，吃饭喝酒的时候也总分给它一份。有一天，李信纯在城外喝酒，喝得大醉，来不及回家，就躺在草丛里睡着了。正好太守郑瑕出城打猎，看见田野里的草很深，就派人放火烧草。李信纯躺的地方正好在下风口，黑龙看见大火烧过来，就用嘴拉扯主人的衣服，可李信纯醉得一动不动。他躺的地方附近有一条小溪，相距三五十步，黑龙就奔到溪边，跳进水里，把全身打湿，跑回主人身边，绕着主人打滚，把身上的水洒在主人周围的草上，这样来回跑了不知多少趟，终于让主人躲过了大火。可是黑龙运水累得筋疲力尽，倒在主人身边死去了。不久，李信纯醒了过来，看见狗已经死了，全身的毛都是湿的，非常惊讶，再看看大火烧过的痕迹，明白了一切，放声痛哭。这件事传到太守那里，太守感慨地说：狗报恩，比人还要深。人若不知道报恩，怎么比得上狗呢！他就命人准备棺椁和衣被，把黑龙安葬了。书中说，今天纪南还有一座义犬冢，高十几丈。','In the time of Sun Quan there lived in Jinan in Xiangyang a man named Li Xinchun who kept a dog called Black Dragon. He loved it dearly; it went everywhere with him, and at every meal and every cup of wine he shared with it. One day Li Xinchun drank outside the city, grew very drunk, could not get home, and fell asleep in the grass. As it happened, the governor Zheng Xia came out to hunt, and seeing the grass in the fields grown deep, had men set fire to it. Li Xinchun lay just downwind. Seeing the fire coming, the dog tugged at its master''s clothes with its teeth, but he lay too drunk to stir. Thirty or fifty paces away there was a stream; the dog ran to it, plunged in, soaked itself, ran back and rolled round and round its master, wetting the grass about him, back and forth again and again, until its master was spared the fire. But exhausted by carrying water, the dog fell dead at his side. When Li Xinchun woke and saw the dog dead with its coat soaked through, he was amazed; then he saw the traces of the fire, understood, and wept aloud. When the governor heard of it he said with feeling: ''This dog repaid kindness more deeply than a man. A man who does not know gratitude — how can he compare with a dog?'' He ordered a coffin, clothes and coverlets, and had Black Dragon buried. Today, the text says, there is a Tomb of the Faithful Dog at Jinan, more than ten zhang high.','claim.v0350.soushenji.13','《搜神记》卷二十“义犬冢”。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.1','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',1,'天地初开','When heaven and earth began','《古事记》的正文从天地开辟写起。它说：天地刚刚分开的时候，在高天原上出现了三位神：天之御中主神、高御产巢日神和神产巢日神。张伯伦把后两个名字分别译作“高贵而能生成万物的奇妙之神”和“神圣而能生成万物的奇妙之神”。这三位神都是独自出现的，出现以后就隐去了身形，书中没有讲他们的样子，也没有讲他们做了什么。那时候，下面的国土还很年轻，像漂在水上的油脂一样，像水母一样随波漂流，没有一处是固定的。就在这时，有一样东西像芦苇的嫩芽一样萌发出来，从中又出现了两位神：宇摩志阿斯诃备比古迟神和天之常立神。这五位神被称为“别天神”，也就是与后来诸神不同的、特别的天神，他们同样是独自出现、随即隐去身形的神。其中的高御产巢日神和神产巢日神，在后面的故事里还会一次次出现：一位主持高天原上的商议，一位把死去的大国主救活。故事就从这样一个漂浮不定、还没有固定下来的世界开始。','The Kojiki proper opens at the time when heaven and earth first began. In the Plain of High Heaven there came into being three deities: Ame-no-minaka-nushi, Takami-musubi and Kami-musubi, the last two of whom Chamberlain renders the High-August-Producing-Wondrous Deity and the Divine-Producing-Wondrous Deity. All three came into being alone and hid their persons; the text says nothing of how they looked or what they did. The land below was still young, like floating oil, drifting about like a jellyfish, with nothing yet fixed. Then, from something that sprouted up like a reed-shoot, two more deities came into being, Umashi-ashi-kabi-hiko-ji and Ame-no-toko-tachi, who likewise came alone and hid their persons. These five are set apart as the separate Heavenly Deities. Takami-musubi and Kami-musubi will return again and again: the one presiding over the councils of High Heaven, the other bringing the dead Ōkuninushi back to life. The story begins in a world still afloat and not yet made firm.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.01','《古事记》张伯伦译本第一节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.2','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',2,'神世七代','The seven divine generations','接着出现的是国之常立神和丰云野神，他们也是独自出现、隐去身形的神。此后诸神开始成对出现：宇比地迩神与须比智迩神，角杙神与活杙神，意富斗能地神与大斗乃辨神，於母陀流神与阿夜诃志古泥神，最后是伊邪那岐神与伊邪那美神。张伯伦把这最后两个名字译作“邀请之男神”和“邀请之女神”：名字里的“伊邪那”有邀请、招呼的意思，“岐”和“美”分别表示男和女。从国之常立神到伊邪那岐、伊邪那美，被称为“神世七代”：前面两位独自出现的神各算一代，后面每一对男女神合算一代。这些名字的意思并不都清楚，有的像在说泥土和沙，有的像在说木桩，有的像在说宽大的门户，有的像在说完满的面貌和敬畏的言辞，像是一步步描写大地成形、万物有了模样的过程，但书中并没有讲他们做了什么。真正开始动手造国的，是最后出现的这一对兄妹神。','Next came into being Kuni-no-toko-tachi and Toyo-kumo-nu, who also came alone and hid their persons. After them the deities came in pairs: U-hiji-ni and Su-hiji-ni, Tsunu-guhi and Iku-guhi, Oho-to-no-ji and Oho-to-no-be, Omo-daru and Aya-kashiko-ne, and last of all Izanagi and Izanami, whom Chamberlain renders the Male-Who-Invites and the Female-Who-Invites: izana- carries the sense of inviting, and -ki and -mi mark male and female. From Kuni-no-toko-tachi to Izanami are called the Seven Divine Generations: the two single deities count a generation each, and each pair counts as one. The meanings of the names are not all clear; some seem to speak of mud and sand, of posts, of great doorways, of a perfect face and of awe, as if the land were slowly taking shape, but the text does not say what these deities did. The work of making the land falls to the last pair.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.02','《古事记》张伯伦译本第二节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.3','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',3,'淤能碁吕岛','The island of Onogoro','天上的众神一齐命令伊邪那岐和伊邪那美：去把这片漂浮的国土整治好，使它坚固，把它生出来。众神的命令里有三件事——整治、巩固、生出——后面的故事，正是一步步完成这三件事。众神赐给他们一支装饰着美玉的长矛，叫作天之沼矛。两位神站在天之浮桥上，把长矛伸进下面的海水里，“咕噜咕噜”地搅动起来——这是书中模仿搅动海水的声音。他们把长矛提起来，从矛尖滴下的海水层层堆积，变成了一座岛，这就是淤能碁吕岛。张伯伦把这个名字译作“自凝岛”，意思是“自己凝结而成的岛”。这是天地间第一块坚固的土地。淤能碁吕岛究竟在哪里，书中没有说，后世有许多说法，都只是推测。两位神从天之浮桥上走下来，降落到这座岛上。造国的工作，就要从这里开始了。','All the heavenly deities commanded Izanagi and Izanami to make, consolidate and give birth to the drifting land — three tasks that the rest of the story works through one by one — and granted them a jewelled spear, the Ame-no-nuhoko. Standing on the Floating Bridge of Heaven, the two pushed the spear down and stirred the brine until it went curdle-curdle, the text imitating the sound. When they drew the spear up, the brine that dripped from its point piled up and became an island: Onogoro, which Chamberlain renders Self-Curdling Island. It was the first firm ground. Where Onogoro lies the text does not say; later identifications are only conjecture. The two deities descended from the Floating Bridge onto the island, and from there the making of the land began.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.03','《古事记》张伯伦译本第三节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.4','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',4,'天之御柱与水蛭子','The heavenly pillar and the leech-child','两位神在岛上竖起了一根天之御柱，又建起一座八寻殿——“寻”是两臂张开的长度，八寻就是极言其宽大。伊邪那岐问伊邪那美：你的身体是怎样长成的？伊邪那美说：我的身体长成了，只有一处没有长合。伊邪那岐说：我的身体也长成了，只有一处长得多余。我想用多余的地方填补你没有长合的地方，生出国土，你看怎样？伊邪那美说：好。伊邪那岐说：那么我们绕着天之御柱走，相遇以后结为夫妻。你从右边绕，我从左边绕。两人绕柱相遇时，伊邪那美先开口说：啊，多么好的男子！伊邪那岐接着说：啊，多么好的少女！说完他又说：女子先开口是不吉利的。可他们还是结合了，生下的第一个孩子是水蛭子。书中没有描写他的样子，只说他们把这个孩子放进芦苇编的小船里，任它漂走了。接着又生了淡岛，也不算在他们的孩子之中。','On the island they set up a heavenly pillar and built a hall eight fathoms wide. Izanagi asked Izanami how her body was formed. She answered that it was fully formed except for one place that had not grown together; he said his was fully formed except for one place that had grown to excess, and proposed to fill her place with his and so give birth to lands. She agreed. ''Then let us walk round this heavenly pillar,'' he said, ''and meet and be joined. You go round from the right, I from the left.'' When they met, Izanami spoke first: ''Ah, what a fair and lovely youth!'' Then Izanagi: ''Ah, what a fair and lovely maiden!'' Afterwards he said it was unlucky for the woman to speak first. Yet they came together, and their first child was a leech-child, whom they put into a boat of reeds and let float away. Next they bore the island of Aha, which also was not reckoned among their children.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.04','《古事记》张伯伦译本第四节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.5','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',5,'重新绕柱，生下大八岛','The Great Eight Islands','两位神商量说：我们生的孩子不好，应该去问问天上的众神。他们一起回到天上，请求众神的指示。天上的众神用太占——一种烧鹿肩骨看裂纹的占卜——占了一卦，说：因为女子先开口，所以不好。回去重新说一遍吧。两人回到岛上，像以前一样绕着天之御柱走，这一次伊邪那岐先说：啊，多么好的少女！伊邪那美再说：啊，多么好的男子！这样结合以后，他们生下的是一个个岛屿：先是淡路岛；接着是伊予之二名岛，它一个身体有四张脸，每张脸都有名字：伊予国叫爱比卖，赞岐国叫饭依比古，粟国叫大宜都比卖，土左国叫建依别；然后是隐岐岛；筑紫岛也是一个身体四张脸，分别是筑紫国、丰国、肥国和熊曾国；再往后是壹岐岛、对马岛、佐渡岛，最后是大倭丰秋津岛，也就是本州。因为这八个岛最先生出，所以这片土地被称为“大八岛国”。之后他们又生下了六个小岛。','The two took counsel: ''The children we have borne are not good. We should report this before the heavenly deities.'' They went up together, and the heavenly deities divined by grand divination — reading the cracks in a burned deer''s shoulder-blade — and said: ''It was not good because the woman spoke first. Go down again and say it anew.'' So they walked round the pillar as before, and this time Izanagi spoke first. Then they gave birth to islands: Awaji first; then Iyo-no-futana, one body with four faces, each named — Iyo called Ehime, Sanuki called Iiyori-hiko, Aha called Ōgetsu-hime and Tosa called Takeyori-wake; then Oki; then Tsukushi, likewise one body with four faces, the lands of Tsukushi, Toyo, Hi and Kumaso; then Iki, Tsushima, Sado, and last Ō-yamato-toyo-aki-tsu-shima. Because these eight islands were born first, the country is called the Land of the Eight Great Islands. After them they bore six smaller islands.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.05','《古事记》张伯伦译本第五节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.6','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',6,'众神诞生','The birth of the deities','生完了国土，伊邪那岐和伊邪那美又接着生神。书中把这些神的名字一个个记下来：大事忍男神，石土毘古神，石巢比卖神，大户日别神，天之吹男神，大屋毘古神，风木津别之忍男神；然后是海神大绵津见神，河口之神速秋津日子神和速秋津比卖神——这一对河口之神又生下了沫那艺、沫那美、颊那艺、颊那美等掌管水沫与水面的神；接着是风神志那都比古神，树木之神久久能智神，山神大山津见神，原野之神鹿屋野比卖神，又名野椎神，大山津见神和野椎神又生下了掌管土地、雾气、山谷的许多神。他们还生下了一位叫作“鸟之石楠船”的神，又叫天鸟船，是诸神乘坐的船；又生下了掌管食物的大宜都比卖。有的名字一长串，读起来就像是在点数天地间的万物：石头、门户、屋顶、海浪、水沫、雾气、山谷……世界就这样一样样有了自己的神灵。可是，在这一连串的诞生之后，等待着他们的却是一场灾祸。','Having borne the lands, Izanagi and Izanami went on to bear deities, and the text records each name: Oho-koto-oshi-wo, Iha-tsuchi-biko, Iha-su-hime, Oho-to-hi-wake, Ame-no-fuki-wo, Oho-ya-biko, Kaza-mo-tsu-wake-no-oshi-wo; the great sea deity Oho-wata-tsu-mi; the river-mouth deities Haya-aki-tsu-hiko and Haya-aki-tsu-hime, who in turn bore deities of foam and of the water''s surface; the wind deity Shina-tsu-hiko, the tree deity Kuku-no-chi, the great mountain deity Oho-yama-tsu-mi and the moor goddess Kaya-nu-hime, also called Nu-dzu-chi, who together bore deities of earth, mist and valleys. They bore the Bird''s Rock-Camphor-Boat, also called the Heavenly Bird-Boat, and the food goddess Oho-ge-tsu-hime. Some names are very long, so that the list reads like a counting of everything in heaven and earth: rocks, doorways, roofs, waves, foam, mist, valleys. So, thing by thing, the world received its deities. But at the end of this long run of births a calamity was waiting.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.06','《古事记》张伯伦译本第六节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.7','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',7,'火神之火','The fire deity','最后，伊邪那美生下了火之迦具土神，也叫火之夜艺速男神、火之炫毘古神。因为生下了火神，她的阴部被烧伤，从此一病不起。她在病中呕吐，从呕吐物中生出了金山毘古神和金山毘卖神，也就是金属之神；从粪便中生出了波迩夜须毘古神和波迩夜须毘卖神，也就是黏土之神；从尿中生出了水神弥都波能卖神，以及和久产巢日神，和久产巢日神的女儿就是掌管食物的丰宇气毘卖神。即使在病痛之中，她的身体也还在不停地生出新的神来：金属、黏土、水和五谷，都是从这位垂死的母神身上来的。可是终于，伊邪那美因为生下火神而离开了人世——《古事记》用的说法是“神避”，也就是神的隐去。书中总结说，伊邪那岐与伊邪那美一起生下的岛屿共十四个，神共三十五位。创造了国土和万物之神的母亲，就这样因为生下火而死去，这是《古事记》里第一次出现死亡。','Last of all Izanami bore the fire deity Kagutsuchi, also called Hi-no-yagi-haya-wo and Hi-no-kaga-biko. In bearing this child her private parts were burned, and she lay down sick. From her vomit were born Kana-yama-biko and Kana-yama-bime, deities of metal; from her dung, Hani-yasu-biko and Hani-yasu-bime, deities of clay; from her urine, the water goddess Mitsu-ha-no-me and Waku-musubi, whose daughter is Toyo-uke-bime, goddess of food. Even in sickness her body went on bringing forth deities: metal, clay, water and grain all came from the dying mother. But at last, because she had borne the fire deity, Izanami divinely retired — the Kojiki''s word for her passing away. The text sums up that together the pair had borne fourteen islands and thirty-five deities. So the mother of the lands and their deities dies from giving birth to fire; it is the first death in the Kojiki.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.07','《古事记》张伯伦译本第六节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.8','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',8,'哀哭与斩杀火神','Mourning and the slaying of the fire deity','伊邪那岐悲痛地说：我亲爱的妹妹啊，我怎么能用你换来这一个孩子！他匍匐在伊邪那美的枕边和脚边痛哭，从他的眼泪里生出了一位女神，叫作泣泽女神，住在香山下的畝尾木本。伊邪那岐把伊邪那美安葬在出云国与伯伎国交界的比婆山上。接着，他拔出佩带的十拳剑，砍下了儿子迦具土神的头。剑尖上溅出的血落在岩石上，生出了石拆神、根拆神和石筒之男神；剑锷附近的血溅出来，生出了甕速日神、樋速日神和建御雷之男神，也就是后来降服出云的建御雷神；从剑柄上滴下、从指缝间漏出的血，又生出了暗淤加美神和暗御津羽神两位山谷之水的神。从迦具土神被砍杀的身体各处——头、胸、腹、阴部、左右手、左右脚——又生出了八位山神。伊邪那岐用来斩杀火神的这把剑，名叫天之尾羽张，又名伊都之尾羽张。','Izanagi cried: ''Oh my beloved younger sister! That I should have exchanged you for this single child!'' He crept weeping round her pillow and her feet, and from his tears was born the goddess Naki-sawa-me, who dwells at the foot of the trees on the hill of Unewo by Mount Kagu. He buried Izanami on Mount Hiba on the border of Izumo and Hahaki. Then he drew the ten-grasp sword at his side and cut off the head of his son Kagutsuchi. The blood on the point of the sword splashed on the rocks and became Iha-saku, Ne-saku and Iha-tsutsu-no-wo; the blood near the guard became Mika-haya-bi, Hi-haya-bi and Take-mika-dzu-chi-no-wo, the Takemikazuchi who will later bring Izumo to submission; the blood dripping from the hilt through his fingers became Kura-okami and Kura-mitsu-ha, deities of the valley waters. From Kagutsuchi''s head, chest, belly, loins, hands and feet eight mountain deities were born. The sword was named Ame-no-wo-ha-bari, also Itsu-no-wo-ha-bari.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.08','《古事记》张伯伦译本第七至八节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.9','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',9,'黄泉：不要看我','Yomi: do not look at me','伊邪那岐思念妻子，追到了黄泉国。伊邪那美从宫殿紧闭的门里出来迎接他，伊邪那岐就隔着门对她说：我亲爱的妹妹啊，我和你一起造的国土还没有完成，请你回来吧！伊邪那美说：可惜你没有早一点来。我已经吃了黄泉灶火煮的食物——这句话的意思是，她已经属于黄泉了。不过，既然我亲爱的哥哥特意来了，我想回去。我要去和黄泉的神商量一下。在这期间，请你千万不要看我。她说完回到殿里，过了很久都没有出来。伊邪那岐等不下去了，他折下插在左边发髻上的多齿梳子的一根边齿，点起火来，走进殿里察看。只见伊邪那美的身上爬满了蛆虫，八位雷神盘踞在她身上：头上是大雷，胸口是火雷，腹部是黑雷，阴部是拆雷，左手是若雷，右手是土雷，左脚是鸣雷，右脚是伏雷。这就是后来故事里所说的“黄泉八雷神”。','Wishing to see his wife again, Izanagi followed her to the Land of Yomi. She came out to meet him from the closed door of the palace. ''My beloved younger sister,'' he said, ''the lands that you and I were making are not yet finished. Come back!'' She answered: ''Alas that you did not come sooner! I have eaten of the food of the furnace of Yomi. Yet since my beloved elder brother has come, I wish to return; I will discuss it with the deities of Yomi. Do not look at me.'' She went back in and stayed so long that he could not wait. He broke off an end tooth of the comb in the left bunch of his hair, lit it, and went in. Maggots were swarming over her, and eight thunder deities had been born on her body: Great-Thunder on her head, Fire-Thunder on her breast, Black-Thunder on her belly, Crack-Thunder below, Young-Thunder on her left hand, Earth-Thunder on her right, Sounding-Thunder on her left foot and Reclining-Thunder on her right.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.09','《古事记》张伯伦译本第九节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.10','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',10,'黄泉丑女与三个桃子','The Ugly Females of Yomi and the three peaches','伊邪那岐看见这副情景，吓得转身就逃。伊邪那美说：你让我蒙受了羞辱！她派黄泉丑女去追赶他。伊邪那岐取下头上的黑色发饰扔在身后，发饰立刻变成了葡萄，丑女们停下来捡葡萄吃；吃完了又追上来，他又折下右边发髻上的梳子扔出去，梳子变成了竹笋，丑女们又停下来拔竹笋吃。伊邪那美又派八位雷神，率领一千五百名黄泉的军队来追。伊邪那岐拔出十拳剑，一边在身后挥舞，一边逃跑。追到黄泉比良坂——黄泉与人间交界的山坡——的坡脚下时，他摘下长在那里的三个桃子，向追兵投去，追兵全都逃回去了。伊邪那岐对桃子说：你们帮助了我，以后苇原中国的人们遇到苦难的时候，你们也要像这样帮助他们。他给桃子取名意富加牟豆美命。','Terrified at the sight, Izanagi turned and fled. ''You have put me to shame!'' cried Izanami, and sent the Ugly Females of Yomi after him. He threw down his black head-dress, which turned into grapes, and while they stopped to eat he fled on; when they came after him again he broke the comb from the right bunch of his hair and threw it, and it turned into bamboo shoots, which they pulled up and ate. Then she sent the eight thunder deities with fifteen hundred warriors of Yomi. He drew his ten-grasp sword and fled brandishing it behind him. At the foot of the Even Pass of Yomi, the slope between Yomi and the living world, he took three peaches growing there and smote the pursuers with them, and they all fled back. He said to the peaches: ''As you have helped me, so help the people of the Central Land of Reed-Plains when they are in trouble,'' and named them Oho-kamu-dzu-mi.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.10','《古事记》张伯伦译本第九节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.11','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',11,'千引石前的诀别','Farewell at the thousand-draught rock','最后，伊邪那美亲自追来了。伊邪那岐搬来一块要一千个人才拉得动的大石头——千引石，堵住了黄泉比良坂。两位神隔着石头面对面站着，说出了诀别的话，书中称之为“度事户”，一般理解为宣告断绝夫妻关系的话。伊邪那美说：我亲爱的哥哥，既然你这样做，我就每天把你国家的人勒死一千个。伊邪那岐回答：我亲爱的妹妹，如果你那样做，我就每天建起一千五百座产房。所以，世上每天必定有一千人死去，也必定有一千五百人出生。从此生与死被这块石头隔开，而活着的人总是比死去的多。伊邪那美从此被称为黄泉津大神，又因为她追上了伊邪那岐，也被叫作道敷大神。堵住黄泉坡的那块石头，被称为道反之大神，也叫塞坐黄泉户大神。书中说，所谓黄泉比良坂，就是现在出云国的伊赋夜坂。','Last of all Izanami herself came in pursuit. Izanagi pulled up a rock that would take a thousand men to lift and blocked the Even Pass of Yomi with it. Standing face to face on either side of the rock, they spoke the words of parting, which the text calls the exchange of the door of words, usually understood as a declaration ending the marriage. Izanami said: ''My beloved elder brother, if you do this, I will strangle a thousand of the people of your land every day.'' Izanagi answered: ''My beloved younger sister, if you do that, I will set up fifteen hundred birth-houses every day.'' That is why a thousand people surely die every day and fifteen hundred are surely born: the rock divides life from death, and the living always outnumber the dead. Izanami was thenceforth called the Great Deity of Yomi, and also the Road-Reaching Great Deity because she had overtaken him. The rock is called the Great Deity of the Road-Turning-Back. The Even Pass of Yomi, the text says, is the pass of Ifuya in Izumo.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.11','《古事记》张伯伦译本第九节。',NULL),
('storysec.v0350.japanese_kojiki_sect1_10.12','storyver.japanese.kojiki_sect1_10',12,'禊祓与三贵子','Purification and the three noble children','伊邪那岐说：我去了一个丑恶污秽的国家，必须洗净我的身体。他来到筑紫日向的橘之小门的阿波岐原，在河口举行禊祓。他把手杖、腰带、袋子、衣服、裤子、帽子和左右手的手镯一件件扔掉，每扔下一件，就生出一位神，从手杖生出的是冲立船户神，一共生出了十二位神。接着他说：上游水太急，下游水太缓。就走进中游的水里洗身，先生出了由黄泉污秽而来的八十祸津日神和大祸津日神，又生出了纠正灾祸的神直毘神、大直毘神和伊豆能卖神，然后在水底、水中、水面洗涤，生出了三位绵津见神和三位筒之男神。书中说，三位绵津见神是阿昙连一族祭祀的祖神，三位筒之男神是墨江的三位大神。最后，他洗左眼时生出了天照大神，洗右眼时生出了月读命，洗鼻子时生出了建速须佐之男命。故事从这里接到下一部：三位尊贵的孩子即将各自领受父亲的命令。','''I have been to a hideous and polluted land,'' said Izanagi, ''so I must purify my body.'' He came to the plain of Ahagi by the river-mouth of Tachibana in Himuka in Tsukushi and performed purification. As he threw down his staff, girdle, bag, garment, trousers, hat and the bracelets of each arm, a deity was born of each — from the staff, Tsuki-tatsu-funa-do — twelve in all. Then, saying that the upper waters were too swift and the lower too sluggish, he went down into the middle reach to wash. First were born the two deities of calamity, Ya-so-maga-tsu-bi and Oho-maga-tsu-bi, from the pollution of Yomi; then Kamu-nahobi, Oho-nahobi and Idzu-no-me to set the calamity right; then, as he washed at the bottom, in the middle and on the surface of the water, three Wata-tsu-mi deities and three Tsutsu-no-wo deities. The text adds that the three Wata-tsu-mi are the ancestral deities worshipped by the Adzumi clan, and the three Tsutsu-no-wo the three great deities of Suminoe. Last, as he washed his left eye, Amaterasu was born; his right eye, Tsukuyomi; his nose, Take-haya-Susanoo. The story passes here to the next part, where the three noble children receive their father''s charge.','claim.v0350.kojiki_izanagi.12','《古事记》张伯伦译本第十节。',NULL);

